window.LUMINA = {"generated":"2026-11-07","topics":[{"id":"mucite-orale","label":"Mucite buccale","short":"Mucite","color":"#d9480f","description":"Prévention et traitement des mucites induites par la chimiothérapie, la radiothérapie ou la greffe de cellules souches hématopoïétiques par photobiomodulation (PBM / LLLT).","cadence":"hebdomadaire","since":"2024-01-01","pubmed_query":"(\"photobiomodulation\"[tiab] OR \"low-level laser\"[tiab] OR \"low level laser\"[tiab] OR \"LLLT\"[tiab] OR \"low-level light\"[tiab] OR \"Low-Level Light Therapy\"[MeSH] OR \"laser therapy\"[tiab] OR \"PBMT\"[tiab]) AND (\"mucositis\"[tiab] OR \"Stomatitis\"[MeSH] OR \"Mucositis\"[MeSH]) NOT (\"peri-implant\"[tiab] OR \"periimplant\"[tiab] OR \"peri-implantitis\"[tiab])","n_raw":140,"n_excluded":30}],"articles":[{"pmid":"42969421","title_fr":"PBM extrabuccale versus intrabuccale pour prévenir la mucite chez l'enfant sous méthotrexate haute dose","type":"rct","level":2,"pop":"pédiatrie","ctx":["chimio"],"aim":"prévention","mode":["intrabuccal","extrabuccal","laser"],"nm":[660],"n":31,"effect":"favorable","theme":"Extra vs intrabuccal","key":true,"metric":{"kind":"rates","outcome":"Mucite cumulée (OMS)","a_label":"Extrabuccale","a":31.6,"b_label":"Intrabuccale","b":59.5},"l1":"Chez 31 enfants (75 cures de méthotrexate haute dose), la PBM extrabuccale à 660 nm a fait moins de mucites que la PBM intrabuccale (31,6 % contre 59,5 %), en moins de temps et sans effet indésirable.","l2":"Essai randomisé ouvert brésilien : chaque cure de méthotrexate haute dose était tirée au sort entre PBM intrabuccale et extrabuccale (lumière rouge 660 nm), quotidienne de la veille de la perfusion jusqu'à la sortie. La mucite était cotée selon l'échelle OMS à J0, J3, J5 et J10.\n\nAprès ajustement sur les cures répétées chez un même enfant, le risque de mucite restait plus élevé avec la voie intrabuccale (RR 1,84 ; IC95 % 1,07–3,19). Douleur, pH salivaire et biochimie salivaire ne différaient pas. La voie extrabuccale était nettement plus rapide à appliquer. C'est un argument pratique fort en pédiatrie, où l'ouverture buccale prolongée est difficile.","l3":{"points":["Plan : randomisation par cure (75 épisodes) et non par patient, avec analyse tenant compte des épisodes répétés.","Intervention : PBM préventive quotidienne, 660 nm, débutée avant la perfusion de MTX-HD et poursuivie jusqu'à la sortie.","Résultat principal : mucite cumulée 22/37 (IO) contre 12/38 (EO) ; RR 1,84 (IC95 % 1,07–3,19 ; p = 0,029) en défaveur de l'intrabuccal.","Critères secondaires (douleur, pH, biochimie salivaire) : pas de différence.","Tolérance : aucun effet indésirable ; temps d'application bien plus court en extrabuccal."],"limites":["Essai ouvert, petit effectif (31 enfants).","Randomisation par épisode : un même enfant reçoit les deux modalités, ce qui crée des dépendances.","Résultat discordant avec l'essai de 155 enfants de la même thématique (équivalence), à lire ensemble."],"pratique":"Chez l'enfant sous MTX-HD, la PBM extrabuccale est au moins aussi efficace que l'intrabuccale et plus simple : elle peut être proposée en première intention."},"topic":"mucite-orale","title":"Extraoral Versus Intraoral Photobiomodulation for the Prevention of Oral Mucositis in Pediatric Patients Undergoing High-Dose Methotrexate Chemotherapy.","journal":"Special care in dentistry : official publication of the American Association of Hospital Dentists, the Academy of Dentistry for the Handicapped, and the American Society for Geriatric Dentistry","journal_abbr":"Spec Care Dentist","authors":["Sassi ML","Cardoso NG","Mobile RZ","et al."],"date":"2026-11-02","year":2026,"week":"2026-S45","doi":"10.1111/scd.70279","pmcid":"PMC13725932","license":"cc by","oa":true,"pdf":"pdfs/42969421.pdf","cited_by":0,"pub_types":["Randomized Controlled Trial","Comparative Study"],"abstract":[{"label":"AIMS","text":"To compare intraoral (IO) and extraoral (EO) photobiomodulation therapy (PBMT) for preventing oral mucositis (OM) in pediatric patients receiving high-dose methotrexate (HD-MTX)."},{"label":"METHODS AND RESULTS","text":"This prospective, open-label, randomized clinical trial included 75 HD-MTX treatment episodes from 31 pediatric patients aged 1-18 years. Each episode was randomized to IO-PBMT or EO-PBMT with red light at 660 nm. Preventive PBMT was initiated before HD-MTX infusion and administered daily until discharge. OM was assessed at baseline and on days 3, 5, and 10 using the World Health Organization scale. Pain, salivary pH, and salivary biochemical profiles were secondary or exploratory outcomes. Cumulative OM occurred in 22/37 IO-PBMT episodes (59.5%) and 12/38 EO-PBMT episodes (31.6%). After accounting for repeated episodes within patients, OM risk was higher with IO-PBMT (risk ratio, 1.84; 95% CI, 1.07-3.19; p = 0.029). Pain scores, salivary pH, and biochemical profiles did not differ significantly between groups. EO-PBMT required substantially less application time, and no adverse effects were reported."},{"label":"CONCLUSION","text":"EO-PBMT was associated with lower cumulative OM occurrence, shorter application time, and good tolerability, supporting its use as a promising, less burdensome preventive strategy in pediatric oncology."}],"keywords":["methotrexate","oral mucositis","pediatric oncology","photobiomodulation therapy","preventive dentistry"]},{"pmid":"42931937","title_fr":"L'odynophagie, conséquence grave et oubliée de la mucite","type":"letter","level":0,"pop":"—","ctx":[],"aim":"—","mode":[],"nm":[],"n":null,"effect":"—","theme":"Correspondance","l1":"Lettre à l'éditeur rappelant que la douleur à la déglutition liée à la mucite est sous-évaluée. Pas de résumé disponible sur PubMed.","l2":"Courrier publié dans Supportive Care in Cancer. Le titre indique un plaidoyer pour mieux évaluer et traiter l'odynophagie (douleur à la déglutition), souvent négligée face à la mucite buccale visible. Le sujet rejoint l'étude de 2026 sur la PBM haute puissance contre l'odynophagie.\n\nAucun résumé n'est indexé : le contenu exact n'a pas pu être analysé.","l3":{"points":["Type : correspondance, sans données originales indexées.","Lien thématique : PBM haute puissance et odynophagie (Oral Oncology, juin 2026)."],"limites":["Contenu non accessible depuis PubMed."],"pratique":"Penser à évaluer la douleur pharyngée et la déglutition, pas seulement la cavité buccale."},"topic":"mucite-orale","title":"Odynophagia: The forgotten serious consequence of mucositis.","journal":"Supportive care in cancer : official journal of the Multinational Association of Supportive Care in Cancer","journal_abbr":"Support Care Cancer","authors":["Zadik Y","Cohen R","Finfter O","et al."],"date":"2026-10-26","year":2026,"week":"2026-S44","doi":"10.1007/s00520-026-11412-4","pmcid":null,"license":null,"oa":false,"pdf":null,"cited_by":0,"pub_types":["Letter"],"abstract":[],"keywords":["Dysphagia","Esophagitis","G-tube","Gastrointestinal mucositis","Oral mucositis","Percutaneous endoscopic gastrostomy","Photobiomodulation","Swallowing Disorder"]},{"pmid":"42897331","title_fr":"Efficacité comparable de la PBM extrabuccale et intrabuccale pour prévenir la mucite sous méthotrexate haute dose en oncopédiatrie : essai randomisé en simple aveugle","type":"rct","level":2,"pop":"pédiatrie","ctx":["chimio"],"aim":"prévention","mode":["intrabuccal","extrabuccal","laser"],"nm":[660],"n":155,"effect":"neutre","theme":"Extra vs intrabuccal","key":true,"metric":{"kind":"rates","outcome":"Mucite (toute sévérité)","a_label":"Extrabuccale","a":30.5,"b_label":"Intrabuccale","b":41.1},"l1":"Sur 155 enfants sous méthotrexate haute dose, la PBM extrabuccale (30 s par séance) protège autant que l'intrabuccale (6 min) : 30,5 % contre 41,1 % de mucites, différence non significative.","l2":"Essai randomisé en simple aveugle, le plus grand de 2026 sur cette question. Les enfants (1 à 18 ans ; leucémie, lymphome ou ostéosarcome) recevaient une PBM préventive quotidienne de l'admission à la sortie, avec une dosimétrie équivalente (660 nm, 100 mW, 1 J par point) par voie intra- ou extrabuccale.\n\nLa mucite est survenue chez 35,5 % des enfants, sans différence entre voies (p = 0,168), ni pour la sévérité, la douleur, le pH salivaire ou la plaque. Les facteurs liés à une mucite plus sévère étaient l'âge et la dose de méthotrexate. La voie extrabuccale divise le temps d'application par plus de dix.","l3":{"points":["Population : 155 enfants (PBM-IO n = 73 ; PBM-EO n = 82), MTX-HD pour LAL, lymphome ou ostéosarcome.","Dosimétrie identique : 660 nm, 100 mW, 1 J/point ; 30 s/séance en EO contre environ 6 min en IO.","Évaluations J0, J3, J5, J10 : OMS, indice de plaque, douleur (EN).","Mucite : 41,1 % (IO) contre 30,5 % (EO), p = 0,168. Douleur médiane nulle à tous les temps.","Régression ordinale : âge (OR 1,14) et dose de MTX (OR 1,24) associés à la sévérité ; la modalité de PBM ne l'est pas."],"limites":["Simple aveugle seulement.","Pas de bras sans PBM : l'essai compare deux PBM, il ne prouve pas l'efficacité de la PBM elle-même.","Essai d'équivalence non formellement annoncé comme tel dans le résumé."],"pratique":"La voie extrabuccale est une alternative crédible et beaucoup plus rapide chez l'enfant sous MTX-HD."},"topic":"mucite-orale","title":"Comparable efficacy of extraoral and intraoral photobiomodulation in preventing oral mucositis during high-dose methotrexate therapy in pediatric oncology: A single-blind randomized controlled trial.","journal":"Supportive care in cancer : official journal of the Multinational Association of Supportive Care in Cancer","journal_abbr":"Support Care Cancer","authors":["Freitas IOP","Schuartz I","Renz GLK","et al."],"date":"2026-10-19","year":2026,"week":"2026-S43","doi":"10.1007/s00520-026-11372-9","pmcid":"PMC13680327","license":"cc by","oa":true,"pdf":"pdfs/42897331.pdf","cited_by":0,"pub_types":["Randomized Controlled Trial","Comparative Study"],"abstract":[{"label":"PURPOSE","text":"This prospective randomized controlled clinical study compared intraoral (IO) and extraoral (EO) photobiomodulation (PBM) for the prevention of chemotherapy-induced oral mucositis (OM) in pediatric oncology patients."},{"label":"METHODS","text":"A total of 155 patients (1-18 years), receiving high-dose methotrexate (HDMTX) for leukemia, lymphoma, or osteosarcoma, were randomized to PBM-IO (n = 73) or PBM-EO (n = 82). Preventive PBM was administered daily from hospital admission through discharge. Both protocols used red light with equivalent dosimetry (660 nm, 100 mW, 1 J per point). PBM-EO required 30 s/session, whereas PBM-IO required approximately 6 min, excluding patient handling time. OM occurrence, severity (WHO scale), plaque index, and pain (numeric rating scale) were assessed on days 0, 3, 5, and 10 after HDMTX infusion."},{"label":"RESULTS","text":"The overall OM occurrence was 35.5%, with no significant difference between PBM-IO (41.1%) and PBM-EO (30.5%) groups (p = 0.168). No statistically significant differences were observed between groups for OM severity, pain, salivary pH, or plaque index at any time point (p > 0.05). Multivariable ordinal logistic regression demonstrated that PBM modality was not independently associated with OM severity. As expected, increasing age (OR = 1.14; 95% CI: 1.04-1.25; p = 0.004) and higher HDMTX dose (OR = 1.24; 95% CI: 1.03-1.49; p = 0.021) were significantly associated with greater OM severity. Median pain scores remained 0 at all assessment points in both groups."},{"label":"CONCLUSION","text":"Extraoral PBM demonstrated comparable effectiveness to intraoral for OM prevention in pediatric patients undergoing HDMTX chemotherapy, while reducing application time."}],"keywords":["Low-level light therapy","Medical oncology","Methotrexate","Oral medicine","Oral mucositis","Pediatrics"]},{"pmid":"42878931","title_fr":"Revue parapluie de la photobiomodulation : les preuves à travers les indications cliniques","type":"umbrella","level":1,"pop":"mixte","ctx":["mixte"],"aim":"les deux","mode":[],"nm":[],"n":null,"effect":"favorable","theme":"Synthèse des preuves","l1":"Synthèse de 100 revues systématiques sur la PBM toutes indications : la mucite et les complications oncologiques ont la plus forte proportion de conclusions favorables, mais 90 % des revues sont de confiance faible ou très faible.","l2":"Cette revue de revues (recherche jusqu'en février 2026) couvre 790 études primaires. Les pathologies bucco-dentaires dominent (53 revues), devant les troubles musculosquelettiques (22) et les complications oncologiques (8). 62 revues concluent en faveur de la PBM, 34 de façon neutre ou mitigée.\n\nLes complications oncologiques, dont la mucite, concentrent la plus forte part de conclusions favorables. La qualité méthodologique est cependant faible : 60 % des revues sont jugées de confiance « critiquement faible » selon AMSTAR 2, et 57 % ne disent rien des effets indésirables. Les auteurs refusent d'en tirer des conclusions fermes sur la sécurité.","l3":{"points":["100 revues systématiques, 1 164 occurrences d'études primaires, 790 études uniques.","AMSTAR 2 : 30 % confiance faible, 60 % critiquement faible.","Effets indésirables : 11 revues en signalent, 32 n'en rapportent aucun, 57 ne renseignent pas.","Indication la plus favorable : complications oncologiques (mucite en tête)."],"limites":["Analyse descriptive du sens des conclusions, sans méta-analyse des effets.","Hétérogénéité majeure des paramètres de traitement."],"pratique":"La mucite reste l'indication la mieux étayée de la PBM, mais la qualité des revues publiées est médiocre : privilégier les méta-analyses récentes de bonne qualité et les recommandations MASCC/ISOO."},"topic":"mucite-orale","title":"Umbrella review of low-level light therapy: evidence across clinical conditions.","journal":"Lasers in medical science","journal_abbr":"Lasers Med Sci","authors":["Song E","Ang L","Yim MH","et al."],"date":"2026-10-15","year":2026,"week":"2026-S42","doi":"10.1007/s10103-026-05064-3","pmcid":"PMC13672599","license":"cc by-nc-nd","oa":false,"pdf":"pdfs/42878931.pdf","cited_by":0,"pub_types":["Systematic Review","Review"],"abstract":[{"label":"","text":"Low-level light therapy (LLLT) and photobiomodulation (PBM) have been studied in diverse clinical fields. Although numerous systematic reviews have been published, their findings are fragmented and inconsistent. This umbrella review of systematic reviews aimed to synthesize the evidence on the effectiveness and safety of LLLT across various clinical conditions. PubMed, Embase, Cochrane Library, and Web of Science databases were searched from inception to February 24, 2026. Eligible studies were systematic reviews or meta-analyses of LLLT in humans. Data on clinical conditions, intervention characteristics, overall direction of conclusions, and adverse events were extracted. Methodological quality was assessed using AMSTAR 2. A total of 100 systematic reviews were included, representing 1,164 primary-study occurrences and 790 unique eligible primary studies. Oral and dental conditions accounted for the largest evidence base (53 reviews), followed by musculoskeletal disorders (22 reviews), oncology-related complications (8 reviews), and wound and lymphatic conditions (8 reviews). Overall, 62 reviews reached conclusions favoring the evaluated intervention, 34 reported neutral or mixed conclusions, and 4 had unclear conclusions. The highest proportion of favorable conclusions was observed for oncology-related complications. Of the 100 reviews, 11 reported one or more adverse events or side effects, 32 explicitly reported no adverse events, and 57 did not provide adverse-event information. Most reviews received low (30.0%) or critically low (60.0%) overall confidence ratings using AMSTAR 2. The review literature suggests potential clinical benefit of LLLT in selected indications, particularly oncology-related complications such as oral mucositis. However, these descriptive patterns do not establish treatment efficacy or the certainty of the underlying evidence. Heterogeneity in treatment parameters, generally low methodological confidence, and incomplete adverse-event reporting limit interpretation and preclude firm conclusions about safety."}],"keywords":["Light therapy","Low–level light therapy","Photobiomodulation","Systematic review","Umbrella review"]},{"pmid":"42941485","title_fr":"PBM chez les patients ORL sous chimioradiothérapie : rôle dans la prévention des toxicités (données de vie réelle)","type":"cohort","level":4,"pop":"adulte","ctx":["ORL"],"aim":"prévention","mode":["intrabuccal","extrabuccal","LED"],"nm":[620,650,820],"n":117,"effect":"favorable","theme":"Vie réelle","metric":{"kind":"rates","outcome":"Odynophagie grade 2–3 en fin de traitement","a_label":"≥ 10 séances","a":16.7,"b_label":"< 10 séances","b":42.2},"l1":"Cohorte rétrospective italienne de 117 patients ORL : ceux ayant reçu au moins 10 séances de PBM ont eu moins d'odynophagie (16,7 % contre 42,2 %) et de dysphagie persistante, sans effet sur la survie.","l2":"Analyse rétrospective (2021–2024) de patients traités par chimioradiothérapie avec PBM concomitante (620/820 nm ou 650 nm, 3–12 J/cm²). En fin de traitement, 38,5 % avaient une mucite de grade 2–3 et 25,6 % une radiodermite de grade 2–3, des taux inférieurs aux séries historiques sans PBM.\n\nLa comparaison interne selon l'exposition (≥ 10 contre < 10 séances) montre moins d'odynophagie et de dysphagie persistante avec davantage de séances. La PBM n'était associée ni à la perte de poids, ni à la survie sans progression, ni à la survie globale, un point rassurant pour la sécurité oncologique.","l3":{"points":["117 patients, suivi médian de 16 mois, toxicités cotées CTCAE v5.","Mucite G2–3 : 38,5 % ; radiodermite G2–3 : 25,6 %.","≥ 10 séances (72 patients) : odynophagie G2–3 16,7 % contre 42,2 % (p = 0,005) ; dysphagie persistante 12,5 % contre 33,3 % (p = 0,009).","Aucune association avec la survie sans progression ni la survie globale."],"limites":["Rétrospectif, sans groupe témoin sans PBM.","Biais probable : les patients qui reçoivent plus de séances sont aussi ceux qui tolèrent mieux le traitement.","Protocoles PBM variables."],"pratique":"Signal de vie réelle en faveur d'un nombre suffisant de séances (au moins 10) pendant la chimioradiothérapie ORL ; à confirmer par essai randomisé."},"topic":"mucite-orale","title":"Photobiomodulation Therapy in Head and Neck Cancer Patients Undergoing Chemoradiotherapy: Role in Toxicities Prevention.","journal":"Journal of clinical medicine","journal_abbr":"J Clin Med","authors":["Rotondi M","Sun R","El-Masri H","et al."],"date":"2026-10-15","year":2026,"week":"2026-S42","doi":"10.3390/jcm15207968","pmcid":"PMC13705853","license":"cc by","oa":true,"pdf":"pdfs/42941485.pdf","cited_by":0,"pub_types":[],"abstract":[{"label":"","text":"Background: Chemoradiotherapy (CRT) for head and neck cancer (HNC) is frequently associated with severe acute toxicities that compromise treatment adherence and quality of life. This study evaluated the effectiveness of photobiomodulation (PBM) in reducing CRT-induced toxicities in a real-world cohort. Methods: Between May 2021 and July 2024, we retrospectively analyzed 117 HNC patients treated with definitive or adjuvant CRT and concomitant PBM: a non-invasive application of red or near-infrared light to cutaneous and mucosal surfaces to modulate inflammatory responses, using 620/820 nm or 650 nm devices, with irradiation times of 2-12 min and energy densities of 3-12 J/cm2 according to the treatment protocol. Toxicities were graded according to CTCAE v 5.0 at baseline, during treatment, at treatment completion, and throughout follow-up. Associations with toxicity were assessed with logistic regression for repeated measurements (generalized estimating equations). Patients were stratified according to PBM exposure (<10 vs. ≥10 sessions). Results: Median follow-up was 16 months. At treatment completion, grade 2-3 oral mucositis and radiodermatitis occurred in 38.5% and 25.6% of patients, respectively, both lower than rates reported in historical cohorts without PBM. Patients receiving ≥10 PBM sessions (72 pts) experienced significantly lower rates of grade 2-3 odynophagia at treatment completion (16.7% vs. 42.2%, p = 0.005) and persistent dysphagia at the last follow-up (12.5% vs. 33.3%, p = 0.009). PBM exposure was not associated with significant differences in weight loss, progression-free survival, or overall survival. Conclusions: This study provides real-world data on toxicity outcomes among HNC patients receiving PBM, with lower rates of selected toxicities observed among patients receiving ≥10 PBM sessions. Although the retrospective design and lack of a control group limit causal inference, these findings provide a strong rationale for prospective randomized trials to define the optimal PBM schedule and validate its clinical impact."}],"keywords":["chemotherapy","head and neck cancer","low level laser therapy","orofacial complications","photobiomodulation","radiotherapy","supportive care","toxicities"]},{"pmid":"42860472","title_fr":"Qualité de rédaction et « spin » dans les résumés d'essais randomisés sur la mucite","type":"methodo","level":0,"pop":"—","ctx":["mixte"],"aim":"—","mode":[],"nm":[],"n":null,"effect":"—","theme":"Méthodologie","l1":"Sur 112 essais randomisés sur la mucite (dont des essais de PBM), les résumés sont mal rédigés selon CONSORT et presque tous ceux analysés présentent un « spin », une présentation trop flatteuse des résultats.","l2":"Étude de méta-recherche sur quatre interventions (benzydamine, PBM, cryothérapie, palifermine), essais publiés jusqu'en septembre 2024. Le score CONSORT pour les résumés est globalement bas : le plan d'étude et les critères de jugement sont souvent mal décrits.\n\nSeize essais ont pu être analysés pour le spin ; deux seulement en étaient exempts. La qualité de rédaction augmente avec la longueur du résumé et avec un financement industriel déclaré. Pour la veille, la leçon est claire : lire les résultats chiffrés et pas seulement la conclusion des résumés.","l3":{"points":["112 ECR analysés avec la grille CONSORT-A ; 16 analysés pour le spin, 14 en présentent.","Corrélation qualité/longueur du résumé : r = 0,35.","Spin inversement corrélé au score Altmetric (r = −0,65)."],"limites":["Résultats non détaillés par intervention dans le résumé.","Faible nombre d'essais évalués pour le spin."],"pratique":"Ne pas se fier aux conclusions des résumés : vérifier critère principal, effectifs et intervalles de confiance. C'est le rôle du niveau 3 de ce site."},"topic":"mucite-orale","title":"Reporting quality and spin in abstracts of randomized clinical trials related to oral mucositis.","journal":"Supportive care in cancer : official journal of the Multinational Association of Supportive Care in Cancer","journal_abbr":"Support Care Cancer","authors":["Al-Abedalla K","Hamdan AA","Alsaghir Q","et al."],"date":"2026-10-11","year":2026,"week":"2026-S41","doi":"10.1007/s00520-026-11329-y","pmcid":null,"license":null,"oa":false,"pdf":null,"cited_by":0,"pub_types":[],"abstract":[{"label":"PURPOSE","text":"Oral mucositis is a common debilitating side effect of cancer therapy. Numerous randomized controlled trials (RCTs) have examined interventions for this complication. Accurate reporting of RCT results in abstracts is crucial to treatment decisions. Spin undermines the integrity of the information presented and compromises evidence-based practice. This study evaluated the reporting quality of, and spin in, abstracts of RCTs of four interventions for oral mucositis: benzydamine, photobiomodulation therapy, oral cryotherapy, and keratinocyte growth factor, and assessed factors that can affect reporting quality and spin."},{"label":"METHODS","text":"PubMed, Cochrane, and Scopus were systematically searched for RCTs related to oral mucositis treatments published until September 2024. The CONSORT for Abstracts (CONSORT-A) and spin checklists were then applied to assess reporting quality of and spin in their abstracts."},{"label":"RESULTS","text":"Among 112 RCTs that were analyzed, the overall CONSORT-A score for reporting quality was low, and key elements such as study design and outcome reporting were frequently not reported. Only 16 of these RCTs were analyzed for spin and only two RCTs had no spin. Higher reporting quality was significantly correlated with greater abstract word counts (r = 0.35; p < 0.001) and pharmaceutical funding when compared with articles that did not report funding (p = 0.015). Spin was negatively correlated with the Altmetric attention score (r =  - 0.652; p = 0.006)."},{"label":"CONCLUSIONS","text":"Abstracts of RCTs on oral mucositis interventions frequently did not meet the reporting standards defined by the CONSORT-A checklist. Enhanced adherence to reporting guidelines is necessary to support evidence-based clinical practices."}],"keywords":["Abstracts","Cancer","Mucositis","Quality of reporting","Randomized controlled trials as topic","Spin","Supportive care"]},{"pmid":"42858922","title_fr":"Le microbiome oral dans la réponse biologique et clinique à la PBM en soins de support : revue critique","type":"review","level":5,"pop":"—","ctx":["mixte"],"aim":"—","mode":[],"nm":[],"n":null,"effect":"—","theme":"Mécanismes","l1":"Revue critique : rien ne prouve aujourd'hui que la PBM agisse via le microbiome oral. L'hypothèse la plus solide reste un effet sur l'hôte (épithélium, inflammation, réparation), qui modifie secondairement l'écologie microbienne.","l2":"Les auteurs classent les preuves en quatre niveaux : cliniques directes, cliniques indirectes, microbiologiques expérimentales, hypothèses. Les données cliniques directes sont rares et contradictoires : un pilote de quatre patients a décrit des changements du microbiome, une série ORL de 2023 n'en a pas trouvé malgré l'amélioration de la mucite.\n\nIls proposent quatre rôles possibles du microbiome (médiateur, modificateur, biomarqueur ou simple conséquence) et listent les exigences des futures études : prélèvements répétés, dosimétrie complète, contrôle des antibiotiques, de l'hygiène et de l'alimentation. Ils rappellent de ne pas confondre PBM et thérapie photodynamique antimicrobienne.","l3":{"points":["Modèle « l'hôte d'abord » : la PBM agit sur l'intégrité épithéliale, la signalisation redox et inflammatoire, la cicatrisation.","Effets photobiologiques directs sur les bactéries plausibles mais très dépendants des paramètres.","Le microbiome est à considérer comme critère de recherche, pas comme mécanisme établi."],"limites":["Revue narrative, sans méthode systématique."],"pratique":"Aucune conséquence clinique directe ; utile pour interpréter les études sur microbiome et PBM."},"topic":"mucite-orale","title":"The oral microbiome in the biological and clinical response to photobiomodulation during cancer supportive care: a critical mechanistic and translational review.","journal":"Photodiagnosis and photodynamic therapy","journal_abbr":"Photodiagnosis Photodyn Ther","authors":["Eftekharian S","Rastkar S"],"date":"2026-10-10","year":2026,"week":"2026-S41","doi":"10.1016/j.pdpdt.2026.105682","pmcid":null,"license":null,"oa":false,"pdf":null,"cited_by":0,"pub_types":["Review"],"abstract":[{"label":"","text":"Cancer therapy can disrupt the oral microbial ecosystem through mucosal injury, salivary dysfunction, immune suppression, antimicrobial exposure, dietary change, and altered oral hygiene. Photobiomodulation therapy (PBMT) is guideline-supported for prevention of oral mucositis in selected oncologic settings, but whether the oral microbiome contributes to PBMT response remains uncertain. This critical narrative review reframes the question from whether PBMT changes the oral microbiome to what role, if any, microbial ecology plays in the biological and clinical response to PBMT during cancer supportive care. Evidence is organized as direct clinical evidence, indirect clinical evidence, experimental microbial evidence, and mechanistic hypothesis. Direct clinical evidence is sparse and internally mixed: a four-patient uncontrolled pilot reported concurrent cytokine and microbiome shifts after PBMT, whereas a 2023 head-and-neck cancer case series reported clinical improvement in mucositis without significant pre-post changes in salivary bacterial community structure. The strongest current rationale therefore supports a host-first model in which PBMT modifies epithelial integrity, inflammatory and redox signaling, tissue repair, and possibly salivary physiology, thereby changing the ecological conditions experienced by oral microorganisms. Direct microbial photobiological effects remain plausible but are highly dependent on wavelength, irradiance, radiant exposure, oxygen availability, organism, and biofilm state, and should not be conflated with antimicrobial photodynamic therapy. We propose four non-equivalent roles for microbiome change: mediator, modifier, biomarker, or consequence of treatment response. Oral mucositis is the most informative clinical model, but causal interpretation requires longitudinal sampling, functional microbial assays, complete PBMT dosimetry, validated clinical and patient-reported outcomes, and rigorous control of antimicrobials, periodontal status, oral hygiene, diet, feeding support, and anticancer treatment. Microbiome modulation should currently be regarded as an emerging mechanistic hypothesis and research endpoint rather than an established mechanism of PBMT efficacy."}],"keywords":["Cancer supportive care","Dysbiosis","Microbial ecology","Oral microbiome","Oral mucositis","Photobiomodulation","Saliva","Translational research"]},{"pmid":"42776303","title_fr":"Acceptation et efficacité perçue de la PBM intrabuccale versus extrabuccale chez l'enfant","type":"survey","level":0,"pop":"pédiatrie","ctx":["chimio"],"aim":"prévention","mode":["intrabuccal","extrabuccal"],"nm":[],"n":32,"effect":"favorable","theme":"Extra vs intrabuccal","l1":"Les parents de 32 enfants ayant reçu les deux modalités préfèrent la PBM extrabuccale (65,6 %) : beaucoup moins d'inconfort (6,3 % contre 43,8 %), sans avoir à ouvrir la bouche.","l2":"Questionnaire mené dans un essai randomisé où chaque enfant a reçu, selon les cycles, une PBM intrabuccale puis extrabuccale. La préférence pour l'extrabuccal tient au confort et à l'absence d'ouverture buccale.\n\nLes parents attribuaient pourtant plus souvent un meilleur contrôle de la douleur à l'intrabuccal (34,4 % contre 12,5 %), une perception que ne confirment pas les données cliniques de l'essai. L'extrabuccal apparaît donc comme l'option la mieux acceptée en pédiatrie.","l3":{"points":["Inconfort : 43,8 % (IO) contre 6,3 % (EO), p = 0,001.","Préférence : 65,6 % EO, 34,4 % IO.","Perception d'un meilleur contrôle de la douleur : IO 34,4 %, EO 12,5 %."],"limites":["Données déclaratives des aidants, analyse descriptive."],"pratique":"Argument d'acceptabilité qui complète les deux essais pédiatriques de 2026 en faveur de l'extrabuccal."},"topic":"mucite-orale","title":"Acceptance and perceived efficacy of intraoral versus extraoral photobiomodulation for oral mucositis prevention in pediatric patients.","journal":"Supportive care in cancer : official journal of the Multinational Association of Supportive Care in Cancer","journal_abbr":"Support Care Cancer","authors":["Freitas IOP","Renz GLK","Schuartz I","et al."],"date":"2026-09-23","year":2026,"week":"2026-S39","doi":"10.1007/s00520-026-11227-3","pmcid":"PMC13601188","license":"cc by","oa":true,"pdf":"pdfs/42776303.pdf","cited_by":0,"pub_types":["Randomized Controlled Trial","Comparative Study"],"abstract":[{"label":"OBJECTIVE","text":"To compare acceptance, tolerability, and perceived efficacy of extraoral (EO) and intraoral (IO) photobiomodulation therapy (PBMT) for oral mucositis (OM) prevention in pediatric oncology patients."},{"label":"METHODS","text":"Caregivers of 32 pediatric patients undergoing chemotherapy completed a structured questionnaire after their children experienced both intraoral and extraoral PBMT for oral mucositis prevention during different chemotherapy cycles within a randomized clinical study. Device preference, discomfort, perceived pain control, and reasons for preference were descriptively analyzed."},{"label":"RESULTS","text":"EO-PBMT was preferred by 65.6% of respondents, whereas 34.4% preferred IO-PBMT. Preference for EO-PBMT was mainly associated with greater comfort and avoidance of mouth opening. Discomfort was reported by 43.8% during IO-PBM and by 6.3% during EO-PBMT, with a statistically significant difference between the modalities (p = 0.001). IO-PBMT was more often perceived to provide better pain control (34.4%), whereas 12.5% favored EO-PBMT."},{"label":"CONCLUSION","text":"EO-PBMT demonstrated greater caregiver acceptance and lower treatment-related discomfort than IO-PBMT. Although caregivers more frequently perceived IO-PBMT as providing better pain control, these subjective perceptions did not correspond to differences in the clinical occurrence or severity of OM between treatment modalities. EO-PBMT therefore represents a feasible and well-accepted approach for supportive care in pediatric oncology."}],"keywords":["Low-level light therapy","Medical oncology","Oral mucositis","Pediatric assistants"]},{"pmid":"42659420","title_fr":"La PBM dans la prise en charge des lésions buccales : approche ciblée fondée sur les preuves","type":"review","level":5,"pop":"mixte","ctx":["mixte"],"aim":"les deux","mode":[],"nm":[],"n":null,"effect":"favorable","theme":"Revue générale","l1":"Revue brésilienne des usages de la PBM en bouche (douleurs, mucite, hyposialie, aphtes, lichen, herpès) : bénéfices réels mais très dépendants de la dosimétrie.","l2":"Revue narrative qui couvre les mécanismes (anti-inflammatoire, antalgique, réparateur), les interactions lumière-tissu et les paramètres d'irradiation. La mucite et l'hyposialie liées aux traitements du cancer figurent parmi les indications les plus étudiées.\n\nLes auteurs insistent sur l'adaptation des paramètres à chaque cible tissulaire et appellent à standardiser les protocoles.","l3":{"points":["Quatre familles d'indications : douleurs orofaciales, oncologie (mucite, hyposialie), maladies immunitaires, infections virales.","Effets immédiats et cumulatifs au fil des séances."],"limites":["Revue narrative, pas de méthode systématique."],"pratique":"Bonne porte d'entrée pédagogique sur la dosimétrie, sans donnée nouvelle."},"topic":"mucite-orale","title":"Photobiomodulation in the management of oral lesions: a targeted, evidence-based approach.","journal":"Brazilian oral research","journal_abbr":"Braz Oral Res","authors":["Koth VS","Gabriel AF","Deckmann I","et al."],"date":"2026-08-24","year":2026,"week":"2026-S35","doi":"10.1590/1807-3107bor-2026.vol40suppl1059","pmcid":"PMC13508680","license":"cc by","oa":true,"pdf":"pdfs/42659420.pdf","cited_by":1,"pub_types":["Review"],"abstract":[{"label":"","text":"This review synthesizes current literature on laser applications in dentistry, with particular emphasis on the biological mechanisms of photobiomodulation (PBM), dosimetric considerations, tissue interactions, and the clinical management of the most prevalent oral lesions. The oral disorders most frequently investigated for the therapeutic use of PBM were: a) oral pain conditions: trigeminal neuralgia, temporomandibular disorders, and burning mouth syndrome; b) oncology-related conditions: oral mucositis and hyposalivation; c) immune-mediated disorders: recurrent aphthous stomatitis and oral lichen planus; and d) viral infections: herpes labialis. A wide range of laser parameters has shown clinical benefits in the treatment of these disorders. These outcomes are attributed to the anti-inflammatory, analgesic, and tissue repair effects of PBM, which may occur immediately and cumulatively over repeated sessions. PBM therapy is a valuable therapeutic resource in contemporary dentistry and may be successfully applied in a wide range of clinical conditions. Clinical effectiveness of PBM depends on appropriate dosimetry and irradiation parameters according to the biological characteristics of each condition and therapeutic target. Therefore further well-designed randomized clinical trials and protocol standardization are still required to strengthen clinical recommendations and optimize reproducibility."}],"keywords":[]},{"pmid":"42629521","title_fr":"PBM pour limiter dysgueusie et xérostomie sous talquetamab dans le myélome : quatre cas","type":"cases","level":4,"pop":"adulte","ctx":["chimio"],"aim":"prévention","mode":["laser"],"nm":[660,808],"n":4,"effect":"—","theme":"Nouvelles indications","l1":"Quatre patients atteints de myélome traités par talquetamab ont reçu une PBM bien tolérée ; aucun n'a eu de mucite, mais la série ne permet pas de conclure sur la dysgueusie ou la sécheresse buccale.","l2":"Le talquetamab, anticorps bispécifique anti-GPRC5D, provoque fréquemment des troubles du goût et une xérostomie. Cette série décrit une PBM bi-longueur d'onde (660/808 nm) adaptée aux symptômes.\n\nTous les patients ont développé une dysgueusie et une xérostomie. Aucune mucite ni douleur significative, aucun effet indésirable de la PBM, aucun arrêt du talquetamab pour toxicité buccale. Les auteurs insistent : c'est une étude de faisabilité, pas d'efficacité.","l3":{"points":["Dysgueusie chez les 4 patients (2 hypogueusies grade 1, 2 agueusies grade 2).","Xérostomie chez les 4, non quantifiée par sialométrie.","Soins de support associés (bain de bouche au xylitol, substituts salivaires)."],"limites":["4 cas, sans témoin, évaluations non standardisées."],"pratique":"Piste à surveiller pour les nouvelles toxicités buccales des immunothérapies ; pas de recommandation possible."},"topic":"mucite-orale","title":"Photobiomodulation to minimize talquetamab-associated dysgeusia and xerostomia in multiple myeloma: a brief report of four cases.","journal":"Supportive care in cancer : official journal of the Multinational Association of Supportive Care in Cancer","journal_abbr":"Support Care Cancer","authors":["Ferreira MH","de Paula Eduardo F","Menti LD","et al."],"date":"2026-08-21","year":2026,"week":"2026-S34","doi":"10.1007/s00520-026-11133-8","pmcid":"PMC13498624","license":"cc by","oa":true,"pdf":"pdfs/42629521.pdf","cited_by":0,"pub_types":["Case Reports"],"abstract":[{"label":"BACKGROUND","text":"Talquetamab, a GPRC5D-targeting bispecific antibody, produces high response rates in relapsed/refractory multiple myeloma (RRMM) but is frequently associated with oral adverse effects, most commonly dysgeusia and xerostomia, which can impair nutrition and quality of life. No evidence-based supportive-care intervention has been established for these effects."},{"label":"METHODS","text":"We describe four adults with RRMM who received photobiomodulation (PBM) concomitantly with talquetamab, using a dual-wavelength (660/808 nm) protocol individualized to symptom evolution and patient availability. Dysgeusia and xerostomia were assessed prospectively at each session through structured clinical interview and oral examination, with severity graded for each patient; no formal psychophysical gustatory testing or sialometry was performed. This descriptive, uncontrolled case series was designed to report the feasibility and tolerability of PBM rather than its efficacy."},{"label":"RESULTS","text":"All four patients developed dysgeusia during talquetamab treatment. Two had grade 1, intermittent hypogeusia; two had grade 2 dysgeusia with complete taste loss (ageusia). No patient developed oral mucositis or clinically significant oral pain. All four patients reported xerostomia, with reduced salivary film and whitish, viscous saliva on examination; salivary flow was not measured quantitatively. All patients also received xylitol-based mouthwash and saliva substitutes from the start of talquetamab as concomitant standard supportive care. PBM was well tolerated, with no PBM-related adverse events, and no patient discontinued talquetamab because of oral toxicity."},{"label":"CONCLUSIONS","text":"This series demonstrates the feasibility and tolerability of PBM delivered alongside talquetamab in four patients. Given the small, uncontrolled sample and concurrent supportive interventions, no conclusion can be drawn regarding the efficacy of PBM for talquetamab-associated dysgeusia or xerostomia. Controlled studies with objective, validated outcome measures are needed."}],"keywords":["Dysgeusia","Multiple myeloma","Photobiomodulation","Supportive care","Talquetamab","Xerostomia"]},{"pmid":"42525285","title_fr":"PBM transcutanée (808 nm) versus intrabuccale (660 nm) pour prévenir la mucite en ORL : protocole d'essai randomisé","type":"protocol","level":0,"pop":"adulte","ctx":["ORL"],"aim":"prévention","mode":["intrabuccal","extrabuccal","laser"],"nm":[660,808],"n":54,"effect":"—","theme":"Extra vs intrabuccal","l1":"Protocole d'un essai de non-infériorité (54 patients ORL) qui comparera une PBM transcutanée infrarouge à la PBM intrabuccale rouge standard. Pas encore de résultats.","l2":"Les patients irradiés (60–70 Gy) recevront trois séances par semaine, soit en intrabuccal (660 nm, 100 mW, 1 J/point, 44 points, 10 s), soit en transcutané (808 nm, 100 mW, 1 J/point, 32 points). Le critère principal est la sévérité OMS de la mucite.\n\nLe transcutané devrait réduire le temps de séance (80 s contre 440 s) et convenir aux patients avec trismus ou douleur buccale sévère.","l3":{"points":["Critères secondaires : CTCAE, douleur (EN), surface ulcérée sur photos, interruptions de traitement, recours imprévus aux soins.","Fluence : 10,2 J/cm² en intrabuccal ; 34,5 et 4,3 J/cm² en transcutané (points central et latéraux)."],"limites":["Pilote, effectif modeste pour une non-infériorité."],"pratique":"À suivre : résultats attendus pour trancher la question extra/intrabuccal en radiothérapie ORL."},"topic":"mucite-orale","title":"Comparative efficacy of two photobiomodulation protocols transcutaneous versus intraoral in the prevention of oral mucositis in head and neck cancer: a randomized study protocol.","journal":"Lasers in medical science","journal_abbr":"Lasers Med Sci","authors":["Kolodziejwski WT","Renz GLK","de Souza IB","et al."],"date":"2026-07-29","year":2026,"week":"2026-S31","doi":"10.1007/s10103-026-04977-3","pmcid":"PMC13421264","license":"cc by","oa":true,"pdf":"pdfs/42525285.pdf","cited_by":0,"pub_types":["Equivalence Trial"],"abstract":[{"label":"UNLABELLED","text":"This study aims to compare the clinical efficacy of transcutaneous photobiomodulation (PBMT) with an 808 nm infrared laser versus intraoral PBMT with a 660 nm red laser in the prevention of oral mucositis (OM). The study is designed to assess whether the transcutaneous approach is non-inferior to the intraoral standard, thereby contributing to the optimization and standardization of PBMT protocols in head and neck cancer (HNC) supportive care."},{"label":"METHODS","text":"A randomized pilot study is being conducted with 54 patients (n = 27 per group) undergoing radiotherapy (60-70 Gy) for HNC. Participants will be randomly assigned to receive either intraoral PBMT (660 nm, 100 mW, 1 J/point, fluence = 10.20 J/ cm², 10 s, 44 points) or transcutaneous PBMT (808 nm, 100 mW, 1 J/point, fluence = 34.5 J/ cm² (central) and 4.3 J/ cm² (lateral), 10 s, 32 points). Interventions occur three times weekly throughout treatment. The primary outcome is OM severity, graded by WHO scale. Secondary outcomes include OM severity (NCI-CTCAE), pain intensity (Numeric Rating Scale, NRS), ulcerated area extension, registered through digital images, treatment interruptions and unscheduled healthcare utilization."},{"label":"EXPECTED OUTCOMES","text":"the primary hypothesis posits that transcutaneous infrared PBMT is as effective as intraoral red PBMT in mitigating OM severity. Additionally, the transcutaneous approach is expected to offer logistical advantages, including a reduction in application time (80 s vs. 440 s) and improved tolerability in patients with radiation-induced trismus or severe oral pain."},{"label":"CONCLUSION","text":"This protocol is expected to provide robust evidence to support the most feasible and effective PBMT modality for OM prevention. By optimizing treatment delivery, the study may enhance adherence, preserve nutritional status, and improve the quality of life for patients undergoing oncological therapy."}],"keywords":["Head and neck neoplasms","Low-level light therapy","Oral mucositis","Photobiomodulation therapy","Radiotherapy"]},{"pmid":"42463621","title_fr":"La PBM dans la prévention et le traitement de la mucite : revue bibliométrique","type":"biblio","level":0,"pop":"—","ctx":["mixte"],"aim":"—","mode":[],"nm":[],"n":null,"effect":"—","theme":"Bibliométrie","l1":"Analyse des 100 articles les plus cités sur PBM et mucite (1993–2025) : le Brésil et les États-Unis dominent, Supportive Care in Cancer est la revue de référence.","l2":"Sur 526 articles éligibles dans Scopus, les 100 plus cités comptent 56 études primaires et 44 synthèses, signées en moyenne par 9 auteurs. La recherche porte surtout sur l'efficacité de la PBM en traitement de la mucite.\n\nUtile pour repérer les équipes et les articles fondateurs, sans apport sur l'efficacité clinique.","l3":{"points":["725 articles retrouvés, 526 éligibles, 100 analysés.","Revue la plus représentée : Supportive Care in Cancer (14 des 100)."],"limites":["Analyse de citations uniquement (Scopus)."],"pratique":"Aucune conséquence clinique."},"topic":"mucite-orale","title":"Photobiomodulation therapy in oral mucositis prevention and treatment: insights from a bibliometric review.","journal":"Supportive care in cancer : official journal of the Multinational Association of Supportive Care in Cancer","journal_abbr":"Support Care Cancer","authors":["Silva CM","da Silva IMB","Pereira JRD","et al."],"date":"2026-07-16","year":2026,"week":"2026-S29","doi":"10.1007/s00520-026-10976-5","pmcid":"PMC13375819","license":"cc by","oa":true,"pdf":"pdfs/42463621.pdf","cited_by":0,"pub_types":["Review"],"abstract":[{"label":"PURPOSE","text":"This study presents the first bibliometric analysis aimed at evaluating the current state of knowledge and identifying limitations regarding the use of photobiomodulation (PBM) in the prevention and treatment of oral mucositis."},{"label":"METHODS","text":"A comprehensive search of the SCOPUS database was undertaken, with bibliometric analyses conducted using the Bibliometrix R-tool, Microsoft Excel, and VOSviewer."},{"label":"RESULTS","text":"A total of 725 articles were retrieved, of which 526 fulfilled the eligibility criteria. The 100 most cited articles, published between 1993 and 2025, were selected for analysis. The list comprised 56 primary studies and 44 secondary studies, collectively authored by 610 individuals, yielding an average of 9 authors per article. Research was predominantly concentrating studies focused on the efficacy of photobiomodulation in treating oral mucositis. The USA and Brazil emerged as leading contributors for the highest number of citations and scientific publications, both independently and in international collaborations. Among scientific journals, Supportive Care in Cancer stood out, with 14 of the most cited documents on the subject."},{"label":"CONCLUSION","text":"The list of articles included in this review serves as a relevant source of quantitative data, highlighting studies considered as references, as well as authors, journals, and countries with the highest scientific output. Additionally, it provides emerging insights on the topic."}],"keywords":["Cancer","Chemotherapy","Low level laser therapy","Oral mucositis","Photobiomodulation","Radiotherapy"]},{"pmid":"42438170","title_fr":"Méthotrexate salivaire comme prédicteur de mucite chez l'adulte en hématologie : essai randomisé de phase II en double aveugle","type":"rct","level":2,"pop":"adulte","ctx":["chimio"],"aim":"prévention","mode":["intrabuccal","laser"],"nm":[],"n":53,"effect":"favorable","theme":"Efficacité vs placebo","key":true,"metric":{"kind":"rates","outcome":"Mucite ulcérée (OMS ≥ 2) à 96 h","a_label":"PBM","a":11.5,"b_label":"Placebo","b":51.9},"l1":"Chez 53 adultes sous méthotrexate haute dose, la PBM préventive réduit la mucite ulcérée de 51,9 % à 11,5 % (il suffit de traiter 2 patients pour en éviter une) ; le méthotrexate salivaire prédit la sévérité.","l2":"Essai brésilien randomisé, en double aveugle, contre laser simulé. La PBM était appliquée chaque jour jusqu'à l'élimination du méthotrexate (cinq séances au plus) ; la mucite était suivie neuf jours selon l'OMS.\n\nÀ 96 heures, la mucite de grade ≥ 2 touchait 11,5 % du groupe PBM contre 51,9 % du groupe placebo : réduction relative du risque de 77,7 %, nombre de sujets à traiter de 2. Le méthotrexate sérique n'était pas lié à la mucite, alors que le méthotrexate salivaire augmentait au fil du temps et corrélait à la sévérité : un biomarqueur non invasif prometteur.","l3":{"points":["Randomisation : PBM n = 26, placebo n = 27 ; essai enregistré (ReBEC 12218).","Effet : RRR 77,7 %, NST = 2, p < 0,01 ; odds de mucite sévère 8,97 fois plus élevé sous placebo.","Dosages sériques et salivaires du MTX à 24, 48 et 72 h, plus pH urinaire et créatinine."],"limites":["Phase II, monocentrique, petit effectif.","Paramètres laser non détaillés dans le résumé."],"pratique":"Confirme la PBM préventive chez l'adulte sous MTX-HD ; le MTX salivaire pourrait aider à cibler les patients à risque."},"topic":"mucite-orale","title":"Salivary Methotrexate as a Predictor of Oral Mucositis in Hematological Cancer Patients: A Phase II Randomized, Double-Blind Clinical Trial.","journal":"Oral diseases","journal_abbr":"Oral Dis","authors":["Rodrigues-Oliveira L","Bonfim CG","Mores AL","et al."],"date":"2026-07-12","year":2026,"week":"2026-S28","doi":"10.1111/odi.70381","pmcid":null,"license":"cc by","oa":false,"pdf":null,"cited_by":0,"pub_types":[],"abstract":[{"label":"OBJECTIVE","text":"To evaluate the efficacy of prophylactic photobiomodulation therapy (PBMT) in preventing oral mucositis (OM) and to assess serum and salivary methotrexate (MTX) levels as predictors of OM in adults receiving high-dose methotrexate (HD-MTX)."},{"label":"METHODS","text":"This phase II randomized, double-blind, placebo-controlled trial enrolled adults with hematological malignancies undergoing a first HD-MTX cycle at a tertiary cancer center in Brazil. Participants were randomized to PBMT or sham laser therapy, administered daily until MTX clearance or for up to five sessions. OM was assessed for 9 days using WHO criteria. Serum and salivary MTX concentrations were measured at 24, 48, and 72 h post-infusion, along with urinary pH and serum creatinine."},{"label":"RESULTS","text":"Fifty-three patients were randomized (PBMT n = 26; placebo n = 27). PBMT significantly reduced ulcerative OM (grade ≥ 2) at 96 h (11.5% vs. 51.9%), yielding a 77.7% relative risk reduction and a number needed to treat of 2 (p < 0.01). Placebo recipients had an 8.97-fold higher odds of severe OM. Serum MTX was not associated with OM, whereas salivary MTX increased disproportionately over time and independently correlated with OM severity."},{"label":"CONCLUSIONS","text":"PBMT significantly reduced OM incidence and severity in adults receiving HD-MTX. Salivary MTX is a promising non-invasive biomarker for OM risk."},{"label":"TRIAL REGISTRATION","text":"Trial was prospectively registered in the Brazilian Registry of Clinical Trials (ReBec, No. 12218)."}],"keywords":["cancer","low‐level laser therapy","methotrexate","oral mucositis"]},{"pmid":"42677117","title_fr":"Laser basse énergie, PBM et thérapie photodynamique combinées en odontologie : revue systématique","type":"sr","level":3,"pop":"mixte","ctx":["mixte"],"aim":"les deux","mode":["PDT"],"nm":[],"n":null,"effect":"favorable","theme":"Associations","l1":"Huit études (2021–2025) combinant PBM et thérapie photodynamique en odontologie, dont la mucite : effets additifs suggérés, mais protocoles très hétérogènes.","l2":"La revue n'a retenu que des études primaires associant PBM et thérapie photodynamique (PDT) avec des paramètres clairement rapportés. Les applications vont de la mucite à l'orthodontie et à l'endodontie.\n\nL'association apparaît supérieure à chaque technique seule pour l'effet antimicrobien, les marqueurs inflammatoires, la cicatrisation et les résultats rapportés par les patients. Le faible nombre d'études et leur hétérogénéité limitent la portée du résultat.","l3":{"points":["Bases : PubMed, ScienceDirect, ProQuest, DOAJ, Google Scholar.","8 études incluses, toutes indications dentaires confondues."],"limites":["Pas de méta-analyse ; mélange d'indications.","Qualité des études non détaillée dans le résumé."],"pratique":"Pour la mucite, l'association PBM + PDT reste une piste (voir aussi la méta-analyse PDT de 2024)."},"topic":"mucite-orale","title":"Low-Level Laser Therapy, Photobiomodulation, and Photodynamics with Photosensitizer Combination in Dentistry: A Systematic Review.","journal":"Journal of lasers in medical sciences","journal_abbr":"J Lasers Med Sci","authors":["Muntadir L","Narmada IB","Sulisetyawati TIB","et al."],"date":"2026-07-09","year":2026,"week":"2026-S28","doi":"10.34172/jlms.49244","pmcid":"PMC13527693","license":null,"oa":false,"pdf":null,"cited_by":0,"pub_types":["Review"],"abstract":[{"label":"","text":"Introduction: Photobiomodulation (PBM), photodynamic therapy (PDT), and combined therapies have recently attracted attention due to their potentially beneficial antimicrobial, analgesic, and regenerative properties in dentistry. Although PBM and PDT have been extensively studied separately, evidence regarding their combined application is limited. This study aimed to systematically review studies employing combined PBM + PDT protocols in dental and oral applications. Methods: A systematic search of PubMed, ScienceDirect, ProQuest, DOAJ, and Google Scholar (2021-2025) identified original studies using combined PBM and PDT. Inclusion criteria: dental-related PBM + PDT, clear dosimetric parameters, English, primary research. Exclusion: PBM-only, PDT-only, non-dental studies, insufficient parameters Inclusion criteria: dental-related PBM and PDT; clearly reported dosimetric parameters; English language; primary research. Results: Eight studies met eligibility criteria. Applications included oral mucositis management, orthodontic acceleration, endodontic disinfection, oral lesion therapy, antimicrobial modulation, and regenerative endodontics. Combined PBM + PDT demonstrated enhanced antimicrobial activity, reduced inflammatory markers, accelerated wound healing, and improved patient-reported outcomes compared with single-modality treatments. Conclusion: PBM + PDT shows synergistic therapeutic potential across multiple dental fields. Despite promising outcomes, heterogeneity in parameters remains a limitation. Standardized protocols and high-quality clinical trials are required."}],"keywords":["Photobiomodulation","Photodynamics","Therapy-in-dentistry"]},{"pmid":"42399564","title_fr":"PBM pour prévenir la mucite et l'altération fonctionnelle après greffe de cellules souches : essai randomisé POMFITT","type":"rct","level":2,"pop":"adulte","ctx":["CSH"],"aim":"prévention","mode":["intrabuccal","laser"],"nm":[660,808],"n":30,"effect":"favorable","theme":"Efficacité vs soins standards","metric":{"kind":"rates","outcome":"Mucite sévère","a_label":"PBM","a":0,"b_label":"Soins standards","b":26.6},"l1":"Essai chilien (30 greffés) : aucune mucite sévère sous PBM contre 26,6 % dans le groupe témoin, adhésion de 100 %, mais pas d'effet sur la force musculaire ni la qualité de vie à la sortie.","l2":"Essai randomisé contrôlé chez des adultes greffés pour hémopathie maligne. Laser diode (660 et 808 nm, 100 mW, 2 J, 20 points) trois fois par semaine, du début du conditionnement à J+3.\n\nLa mucite sévère a été évitée chez tous les patients traités (0 % contre 26,6 % ; p = 0,032). L'acceptation était excellente (10/10 pour 93 % des patients). En revanche, durée d'hospitalisation, force de préhension, capacité aérobie et force des membres inférieurs ne différaient pas. C'est la publication des résultats du protocole paru dans BMJ Open en 2024.","l3":{"points":["Critères : OMS, test de marche 2 min, dynamomètre Jamar, lever de chaise, FACT-BMT.","Mucite sévère : 0 % contre 26,6 % (p = 0,032).","Enregistrement : NCT06260111 (protocole publié en 2024, PMID 39461860)."],"limites":["30 patients seulement ; témoin sans simulation (pas de laser placebo).","Évaluation fonctionnelle seulement à la sortie."],"pratique":"Confirme la PBM préventive en greffe ; ne pas en attendre de bénéfice fonctionnel global à court terme."},"topic":"mucite-orale","title":"Photobiomodulation to prevent oral mucositis and physical function impairments after hematopoietic cell transplantation: POMFITT randomized trial.","journal":"Supportive care in cancer : official journal of the Multinational Association of Supportive Care in Cancer","journal_abbr":"Support Care Cancer","authors":["López-Espinoza T","Araya-Castro P","Gutiérrez-Silva L","et al."],"date":"2026-07-03","year":2026,"week":"2026-S27","doi":"10.1007/s00520-026-10970-x","pmcid":null,"license":null,"oa":false,"pdf":null,"cited_by":0,"pub_types":["Randomized Controlled Trial"],"abstract":[{"label":"AIM","text":"To assess the effectiveness of photobiomodulation, compared to standard care, to prevent oral mucositis and functional impairment, and improve quality of life in adults who underwent hematopoietic cell transplantation (HCT)."},{"label":"METHODS","text":"Randomized, controlled clinical trial with 30 patients with hematologic malignancies."},{"label":"INTERVENTION","text":"InGaIP diode laser treatment, starting on the first day of conditioning until day + 3, three times a week."},{"label":"PARAMETERS","text":"wavelengths of 660 nm and 808 nm, 100 mW, 2 J, 20 points."},{"label":"OUTCOME MEASURES","text":"oral mucositis (WHO scale), functional capacity (2-min step test), handgrip strength (Jamar dynamometer), lower limb strength (Sit-to-Stand test), quality of life (FACT-BMT), and acceptance (0-10 numerical scale)."},{"label":"RESULTS","text":"Participants fully adhered to the intervention (n = 15; 100%). Acceptance was rated 10/10 by 93.3% of participants. Severe oral mucositis occurred in 26.6% of the control group and 0% of the photobiomodulation group (p = 0.032). No significant differences were found between the groups in hospitalization days, handgrip strength, aerobic capacity, or lower-limb strength."},{"label":"CONCLUSIONS","text":"Photobiomodulation was associated with a lower occurrence of severe oral mucositis but did not affect physical function or quality of life at discharge. Acceptance in the Chilean context was very high."},{"label":"TRIAL REGISTRATION","text":"ClinicalTrials.gov: NCT06260111."}],"keywords":["Hematologic neoplasms","Hematopoietic stem cell transplantation","Low-level light therapy","Physical fitness","Physical therapy modalities","Stomatitis"]},{"pmid":"42360517","title_fr":"Essai randomisé en double aveugle contre simulation d'un dispositif LED intrabuccal de PBM pendant la radiothérapie ORL","type":"rct","level":2,"pop":"adulte","ctx":["ORL"],"aim":"prévention","mode":["intrabuccal","LED"],"nm":[],"n":85,"effect":"favorable","theme":"Efficacité vs placebo","key":true,"metric":{"kind":"rates","outcome":"Mucite sévère (OMS ≥ 3) sur 6 semaines","a_label":"PBM LED","a":36.8,"b_label":"Simulation","b":57.1},"l1":"Essai multicentrique américain (85 patients, 12 centres) : une PBM LED intrabuccale quotidienne de 10 minutes réduit la mucite sévère (36,8 % contre 57,1 %) et préserve mieux le goût.","l2":"Les patients traités par radiothérapie en modulation d'intensité (IMRT) pour un cancer ORL, avec ou sans chimiothérapie, recevaient chaque jour avant la séance une PBM LED ou une simulation. 99,5 % des séances ont été réalisées, sans effet indésirable lié au dispositif.\n\nEn intention de traiter, la mucite de grade OMS ≥ 3 était moins fréquente sous PBM pendant les six semaines (36,8 % contre 57,1 % ; p = 0,046) et deux semaines après (10,8 % contre 36,4 %). En per protocole : meilleure préservation du goût, moins de douleurs buccales et pharyngées, et une tendance à moins de sondes d'alimentation. C'est l'un des essais les plus solides de la période.","l3":{"points":["Double aveugle contre simulation, 12 sites, enregistrement NCT03972527.","Mucite OMS ≥ 3 : 36,8 % contre 57,1 % (p = 0,046) ; à 2 semaines post-IMRT : 10,8 % contre 36,4 % (p = 0,042).","Per protocole : goût (p = 0,034), douleur bouche/gorge (p = 0,029), douleur pharyngée (p = 0,028), sondes d'alimentation (p = 0,073).","Observance : 99,5 % des séances initiées complétées."],"limites":["Effectif modeste pour un multicentrique (85 patients).","Certains bénéfices seulement en per protocole.","Essai d'un dispositif commercial : vérifier les financements dans le texte intégral."],"pratique":"Valide un dispositif LED intrabuccal autonome et quotidien, plus simple à déployer qu'un laser opéré à la main."},"topic":"mucite-orale","title":"Randomized, double-blind, sham-controlled trial of an intraoral photobiomodulation device for oral mucositis and associated complications due to radiotherapy for head and neck cancer.","journal":"Supportive care in cancer : official journal of the Multinational Association of Supportive Care in Cancer","journal_abbr":"Support Care Cancer","authors":["Hu K","Shah P","Nguyen MC","et al."],"date":"2026-06-26","year":2026,"week":"2026-S26","doi":"10.1007/s00520-026-10904-7","pmcid":null,"license":null,"oa":false,"pdf":null,"cited_by":1,"pub_types":["Randomized Controlled Trial","Multicenter Study"],"abstract":[{"label":"PURPOSE","text":"This study evaluated the safety and effectiveness of an intraoral light-emitting diode (LED)-based photobiomodulation (PBM) device to reduce the incidence and severity of oral mucositis (OM) from intensity modulated radiation therapy (IMRT) for head and neck cancer (HNC)."},{"label":"METHODS","text":"This randomized, double-blind, sham-controlled trial enrolled patients with HNC undergoing high-dose IMRT over 6-8 weeks, with or without concurrent chemotherapy. Participants received daily 10-min PBM or sham treatments immediately before IMRT sessions. Assessments were conducted at baseline, daily and weekly during IMRT, and two weeks post-IMRT."},{"label":"RESULTS","text":"Eighty-five participants (42 PBM; 43 sham) were enrolled across 12 US sites. No device-related adverse events were observed, and 99.5% of initiated sessions were completed. In the intent-to-treat population, severe OM (WHO Grade ≥ 3) incidence was significantly lower with PBM across six weeks of IMRT (36.8% vs 57.1%; p = 0.046) and at two weeks post-treatment (10.8% vs 36.4%; p = 0.042). In the per-protocol population, the PBM arm reported significantly greater taste preservation (p = 0.034), lower increases in mouth/throat soreness (p = 0.029) and throat pain (p = 0.028) and needed fewer feeding tube placements (p = 0.073) than the control arm."},{"label":"CONCLUSION","text":"Daily intraoral PBM therapy using an LED-based device was safe, well tolerated, and significantly reduced the incidence of severe OM and associated complications in HNC patients undergoing IMRT with or without concurrent chemotherapy. These findings align with guidelines recommending daily intraoral PBM therapy for preventing cancer therapy-related OM, a dose-limiting toxicity for which effective preventive interventions are needed."},{"label":"TRIAL REGISTRATION","text":"Clinical Trials.gov Registration Number NCT03972527. Registered on June 3, 2019. Daily intraoral PBM therapy using an LED-based device was safe, well tolerated, and significantly reduced the incidence of severe OM and associated complications in HNC patients undergoing IMRT with or without concurrent chemotherapy. These findings align with guidelines recommending daily intraoral PBM therapy for preventing cancer therapy-related OM, a dose-limiting toxicity for which effective preventive interventions are needed."}],"keywords":["Head and neck cancer","Oral mucositis","Photobiomodulation","Radiotherapy"]},{"pmid":"42450035","title_fr":"Biomarqueurs du stress oxydatif dans la cicatrisation muqueuse buccale et la PBM : voies biochimiques et perspectives","type":"review","level":5,"pop":"—","ctx":["mixte"],"aim":"—","mode":[],"nm":[],"n":null,"effect":"—","theme":"Mécanismes","l1":"Revue des mécanismes redox : la PBM ne serait pas un simple antioxydant mais un modulateur dépendant de la dose, qui favorise une signalisation transitoire utile à la cicatrisation.","l2":"Les auteurs distinguent le signal mitochondrial transitoire (ROS, monoxyde d'azote), favorable à la réparation, du stress oxydatif soutenu d'origine NADPH oxydase, délétère. Ils passent en revue les voies Nrf2/HO-1, NF-κB, HIF-1α/VEGF, MAPK/ERK, PI3K/Akt et MMP/TIMP.\n\nIls proposent une grille de choix des biomarqueurs pour les futures études de PBM, en précisant qu'elle n'est pas un outil de décision clinique validé.","l3":{"points":["Biomarqueurs : peroxydation lipidique, oxydation protéique, ADN oxydé, défenses antioxydantes, thiols/disulfures.","Concept clé : réponse biphasique à la dose."],"limites":["Revue narrative, cadre conceptuel non validé."],"pratique":"Aucune conséquence clinique immédiate ; utile pour comprendre l'importance de la dose."},"topic":"mucite-orale","title":"Oxidative Stress Biomarkers in Oral Mucosal Wound Healing and Photobiomodulation: Biochemical Pathways, Experimental Models, and Translational Perspectives.","journal":"International journal of molecular sciences","journal_abbr":"Int J Mol Sci","authors":["Dragojević IM","Kisić B","Mirić D","et al."],"date":"2026-06-26","year":2026,"week":"2026-S26","doi":"10.3390/ijms27135763","pmcid":"PMC13361522","license":"cc by","oa":true,"pdf":"pdfs/42450035.pdf","cited_by":0,"pub_types":["Review"],"abstract":[{"label":"","text":"Oral mucosal repair is a redox-regulated process that may be impaired by diabetes, chronic inflammation, infection, and chemotherapy- or radiotherapy-induced oral mucositis. Reactive oxygen species (ROS) support host defense, epithelial migration, angiogenesis, extracellular matrix remodeling, and adaptive repair when their production is transient and compartmentalized. In contrast, persistent ROS promote lipid, protein, and DNA oxidation, mitochondrial dysfunction, and extracellular matrix damage. Photobiomodulation (PBM) is increasingly used to support oral tissue repair, but its effects should be interpreted as dose- and context-dependent redox modulation rather than as simple antioxidant activity. This narrative review synthesizes oxidative stress biomarkers and redox-sensitive pathways relevant to oral mucosal repair and PBM, including oxidant-antioxidant balance, lipid and protein oxidation, oxidative DNA damage, antioxidant defense, thiol/disulfide homeostasis, mitochondrial and NADPH oxidase-derived ROS, Nrf2/HO-1, NF-κB, HIF-1α/VEGF, MAPK/ERK, PI3K/Akt, and MMP/TIMP signaling. The review emphasizes the distinction between transient mitochondrial ROS/nitric oxide signaling and sustained NADPH oxidase-driven oxi-inflammatory stress. It proposes a practical redox-guided framework for biomarker selection, PBM response interpretation, and future study design, while noting that this framework remains conceptual and is not yet a validated clinical decision algorithm."}],"keywords":["low-level laser therapy","oral mucosal wound healing","oxidative stress","oxidative stress biomarkers","photobiomodulation","reactive oxygen species","redox signaling","redox-sensitive pathways"]},{"pmid":"42319507","title_fr":"PBM et mucite en cancérologie : prévention, prise en charge et preuves issues des essais randomisés","type":"sr","level":1,"pop":"adulte","ctx":["mixte"],"aim":"les deux","mode":["intrabuccal"],"nm":[],"n":868,"effect":"favorable","theme":"Synthèse des preuves","l1":"Revue de 15 essais randomisés contre simulation (868 adultes) : la PBM réduit la mucite sévère, la douleur, les antalgiques et le recours à l'alimentation artificielle, surtout en prévention intrabuccale.","l2":"Revue systématique enregistrée (PROSPERO), recherche jusqu'au 31 mars 2026, limitée aux essais randomisés contre simulation chez l'adulte. La plupart des essais rapportent des bénéfices : moins de mucites sévères, moins de douleur, moins d'antalgiques et de supports nutritionnels.\n\nLes protocoles préventifs intrabuccaux donnent les résultats les plus favorables. La variabilité des paramètres et des critères empêche de recommander un protocole optimal. La qualité méthodologique des essais est jugée acceptable.","l3":{"points":["15 ECR, 868 patients adultes, comparateur simulation (sham).","Bases : PubMed, Web of Science, Scopus.","Signal le plus net : PBM préventive intrabuccale."],"limites":["Pas de méta-analyse quantitative dans le résumé.","Hétérogénéité des paramètres et de la mesure des critères."],"pratique":"Conforte la PBM préventive intrabuccale chez l'adulte ; la recherche doit maintenant porter sur le protocole optimal et le traitement curatif."},"topic":"mucite-orale","title":"Photobiomodulation for oral mucositis in cancer care: prevention, clinical management, and evidence from randomized controlled trials.","journal":"Supportive care in cancer : official journal of the Multinational Association of Supportive Care in Cancer","journal_abbr":"Support Care Cancer","authors":["Parra-Rojas S","Cassol-Spanemberg J","Pérez AN","et al."],"date":"2026-06-19","year":2026,"week":"2026-S25","doi":"10.1007/s00520-026-10834-4","pmcid":null,"license":null,"oa":false,"pdf":null,"cited_by":0,"pub_types":["Systematic Review"],"abstract":[{"label":"PURPOSE","text":"This systematic review aimed to evaluate the efficacy of photobiomodulation (PBM) for the prevention and clinical management of oral mucositis (OM) in adult cancer patients, with particular emphasis on protocol characteristics potentially associated with favorable clinical outcomes."},{"label":"METHODS","text":"A systematic search of PubMed, Web of Science, and Scopus was conducted up to March 31, 2026, in accordance with PRISMA guidelines. Randomized controlled trials in adult cancer patients comparing PBM with sham interventions were included. Outcomes of interest included OM incidence, severity, pain, and related clinical outcomes. The review protocol was prospectively registered in PROSPERO."},{"label":"RESULTS","text":"Fifteen randomized controlled trials involving 868 adult patients were included. Most studies reported favorable effects of PBM, including reductions in severe OM incidence, pain intensity, analgesic requirements, and nutritional support needs compared with controls. Preventive intraoral PBM protocols were generally associated with more favorable outcomes, although variability in treatment parameters, delivery methods, and outcome reporting limited comparability. Most included studies demonstrated acceptable methodological quality."},{"label":"CONCLUSIONS","text":"PBM appears to be a promising supportive care intervention for reducing the burden of OM in adult cancer patients, particularly in preventive settings. However, variability across protocols precludes definitive conclusions regarding optimal treatment parameters. Further high-quality randomized trials with standardized reporting are needed to refine protocol recommendations and expand evidence in therapeutic settings."}],"keywords":["Cancer therapy","Oral mucositis","Photobiomodulation","Randomized controlled trials","Supportive care"]},{"pmid":"42283739","title_fr":"Impact de la mucite traitée par protocole de PBM sur la qualité de vie des enfants atteints de leucémie aiguë lymphoblastique","type":"cohort","level":4,"pop":"pédiatrie","ctx":["chimio"],"aim":"les deux","mode":[],"nm":[],"n":113,"effect":"—","theme":"Qualité de vie","l1":"Chez 113 enfants traités par méthotrexate avec PBM en routine, la mucite garde un fort impact sur la qualité de vie (85 %), surtout quand elle est sévère et longue à cicatriser.","l2":"Étude transversale brésilienne (2020–2024) qui utilise ChIMES (auto- et hétéro-évaluation), C-OIDP et l'échelle OMS. La PBM préventive et curative fait partie du protocole du service pour tous les enfants.\n\nLa sévérité de la mucite et le temps de cicatrisation sont les deux facteurs indépendants d'une moins bonne qualité de vie. Sans groupe sans PBM, l'étude ne mesure pas l'effet propre de la PBM ; elle montre le poids de la mucite qui persiste malgré elle.","l3":{"points":["Âge moyen 10,8 ans ; 85 % rapportent un impact élevé.","Régression : sévérité (β = 0,30 ; p = 0,021) et durée de cicatrisation (β = 0,49 ; p < 0,001)."],"limites":["Transversal, sans comparateur."],"pratique":"Même avec une PBM systématique, la mucite pèse lourd en pédiatrie : continuer à optimiser prévention et traitement."},"topic":"mucite-orale","title":"Impact of oral mucositis treated by photobiomodulation protocol on the quality of life of pediatric oncology patients with acute lymphoblastic leukemia.","journal":"Lasers in medical science","journal_abbr":"Lasers Med Sci","authors":["Alves de Melo Júnior W","Correia Basto da Silva G","Suely Barros Dantas F","et al."],"date":"2026-06-12","year":2026,"week":"2026-S24","doi":"10.1007/s10103-026-04903-7","pmcid":"PMC13263282","license":"cc by","oa":true,"pdf":"pdfs/42283739.pdf","cited_by":0,"pub_types":[],"abstract":[{"label":"","text":"To assess factors associated with methotrexate-induced oral mucositis and its impact on the quality of life of pediatric patients with acute lymphoblastic leukemia, considering chemotherapy dose and mucositis severity within a clinical context where photobiomodulation is routinely used as part of the institutional care protocol. A cross-sectional study was conducted with 113 pediatric patients aged 5 to 18 years undergoing methotrexate-based chemotherapy between 2020 and 2024 in a university hospital in Northeastern Brazil. Data were collected using the Children's International Mucositis Evaluation Scale (ChIMES; self-report and proxy), the Child Oral Impacts on Daily Performances (C-OIDP), the WHO Oral Mucositis Grading Scale, photobiomodulation records, and chemotherapy regimen data. Descriptive statistics, Spearman correlation, and multiple linear regression analyses were performed (α = 0.05). Among the participants, 85% reported a high impact on quality of life. The mean age was 10.8 years (SD = 3.49), and most participants were boys. Higher mucositis severity, older age, higher methotrexate doses, and longer wound healing time were associated with worse quality of life. A strong positive correlation was observed between C-OIDP and ChIMES scores (p < 0.001). In the regression analysis, mucositis severity (β = 0.304; p = 0.021) and wound healing time (β = 0.492; p < 0.001) remained significantly associated with higher impact scores. Methotrexate-induced oral mucositis was associated with a substantial negative impact on quality of life in pediatric patients with acute lymphoblastic leukemia. Greater mucositis severity and longer wound healing time were the main factors associated with worse outcomes. Although photobiomodulation was systematically applied as a preventive and therapeutic intervention, no causal inference regarding its independent effect can be established."}],"keywords":["Acute lymphoblastic leukemia","Antineoplastic therapy","Methotrexate","Oral mucositis","Photobiomodulation therapy","Quality of life"]},{"pmid":"42269385","title_fr":"PBM laser haute puissance pour soulager immédiatement l'odynophagie liée à la mucite","type":"cases","level":4,"pop":"adulte","ctx":["mixte"],"aim":"traitement","mode":["intrabuccal","extrabuccal","haute puissance"],"nm":[940],"n":46,"effect":"favorable","theme":"Douleur","l1":"Chez 46 patients, une séance de PBM laser haute puissance (940 nm) fait baisser la douleur à la déglutition d'environ 40 % (7,4 à 4,6 sur 10), avec un effet qui dure près de 4 jours.","l2":"Étude israélienne avant-après : laser diode pulsé 940 nm, intrabuccal sans contact à 2–3 W et extrabuccal cervical au contact à 0,3–0,5 W. La douleur était mesurée avant et après chaque séance.\n\nSur 80 séances évaluables, 73 % apportaient une amélioration cliniquement significative (au moins 2 points). L'effet durait 3,8 jours en moyenne et était plus marqué quand la douleur initiale était forte. Les auteurs y voient un moyen de retarder ou d'éviter une gastrostomie.","l3":{"points":["85 séances, 80 évaluables ; douleur 7,4 ± 2,5 puis 4,6 ± 2,7 (p < 0,001).","Amélioration ≥ 2 points : 73 % des séances ; durée moyenne 3,8 ± 3,8 jours.","Paramètres : 940 nm pulsé, spot 1 cm², mouvements rotatifs."],"limites":["Pas de groupe témoin : effet placebo et évolution naturelle non contrôlés.","Douleur seule, sans critère objectif."],"pratique":"Option à considérer pour une odynophagie rebelle, en attendant des essais contrôlés ; même équipe que l'étude de 2023 sur la mucite réfractaire."},"topic":"mucite-orale","title":"High-power laser photobiomodulation therapy for immediate relief of mucositis-related odynophagia.","journal":"Oral oncology","journal_abbr":"Oral Oncol","authors":["Zadik Y","Gavish L","Hanut A","et al."],"date":"2026-06-10","year":2026,"week":"2026-S24","doi":"10.1016/j.oraloncology.2026.108049","pmcid":null,"license":"cc by-nc","oa":false,"pdf":null,"cited_by":0,"pub_types":[],"abstract":[{"label":"BACKGROUND","text":"Mucositis-related odynophagia (pain on swallowing) presenting during or shortly after radiotherapy, chemotherapy, hematopoietic cell transplantation, or other oncologic treatments is a common and clinically significant complication that may adversely affect oral intake, nutrition, weight, and quality of life. However, effective treatment options remain limited, and management often relies on supportive care or enteral feeding. This study evaluated the effect of combined intraoral and extraoral high-power laser photobiomodulation therapy (PBMT) on mucositis-related odynophagia."},{"label":"METHODS","text":"Patients with mucositis-related odynophagia received ≥ 1 session of 940 nm pulsed diode laser PBMT (intraoral: 2-3 W, non-contact, rotatory motion, 1 cm2 spot size; extraoral: 0.3-0.5 W, contact over the cervical region). Pain intensity was assessed before and after each session using a 0-10 numerical rating scale (NRS)."},{"label":"RESULTS","text":"Forty-six patients underwent 85 treatment sessions, of which 80 were evaluable. Pain scores decreased significantly following PBMT (mean ± SD: 7.4 ± 2.5 vs. 4.6 ± 2.7; p < 0.001), corresponding to a mean reduction of 2.8 ± 2.1 points (∼40 %). Clinically meaningful improvement (≥ 2 NRS points) was observed in 73 % of sessions. The analgesic effect persisted for a mean of 3.8 ± 3.8 days. Treatment response was consistent across subgroups, with greater reductions observed in patients with higher baseline pain (β = 0.239, p = 0.006)."},{"label":"CONCLUSIONS","text":"High-power PBMT was associated with rapid and clinically meaningful pain relief in oncotherapy-induced odynophagia. This effect may help delay or reduce the need for gastrostomy tube (G-tube) placement and support oral intake. Accordingly, high-power PBMT may represent an additional layer in supportive care strategies for mucositis and its related complications."}],"keywords":["Dysphagia","Esophagitis","Gastrointestinal mucositis","Percutaneous endoscopic gastrostomy","Swallowing disorder"]},{"pmid":"42344781","title_fr":"Analyse bibliométrique de la PBM pour la mucite chimio- et radio-induite (2016–2025)","type":"biblio","level":0,"pop":"—","ctx":["mixte"],"aim":"—","mode":[],"nm":[],"n":null,"effect":"—","theme":"Bibliométrie","l1":"152 publications (2016–2025) : forte croissance, menée par le Brésil, les États-Unis et la France.","l2":"Analyse des tendances de publication, des institutions (Goiás, São Paulo, Harvard), des revues (Supportive Care in Cancer, Lasers in Medical Science) et des mots-clés. La prévention monte en puissance dans les thèmes.\n\nPas d'évaluation de l'efficacité clinique.","l3":{"points":["Bases : Scopus, Web of Science, PubMed.","La France fait partie des trois pays les plus productifs."],"limites":["Bibliométrie seule."],"pratique":"Aucune conséquence clinique."},"topic":"mucite-orale","title":"A bibliometric analysis of photobiomodulation therapy for the prevention and treatment of chemotherapy- and radiotherapy-induced oral mucositis.","journal":"Frontiers in oral health","journal_abbr":"Front Oral Health","authors":["Kurmanalina M","Tamadon A","Ismagulova E","et al."],"date":"2026-06-09","year":2026,"week":"2026-S24","doi":"10.3389/froh.2026.1811389","pmcid":"PMC13287024","license":"cc by","oa":true,"pdf":"pdfs/42344781.pdf","cited_by":0,"pub_types":["Review"],"abstract":[{"label":"INTRODUCTION","text":"Photobiomodulation therapy has emerged as an effective non-invasive intervention for preventing and managing chemotherapy- and radiotherapy-induced oral mucositis, a frequent complication that negatively affects oral health and quality of life in cancer patients."},{"label":"METHODS","text":"This bibliometric study was guided by PRISMA 2020 principles for study identification and selection. A total of 152 publications indexed in Scopus, Web of Science, and PubMed between 2016 and 2025 were analyzed using bibliometric indicators, including publication trends, authorship, institutional productivity, country contributions, citation analysis, and keyword evolution."},{"label":"RESULTS","text":"The results demonstrated a substantial increase in scientific production between 2016 and 2025, with Brazil, the United States, and France leading research output. The most productive institutions included Universidade Federal de Goiás, Universidade de São Paulo, and Harvard University, while Supportive Care in Cancer and Lasers in Medical Science were the most influential journals. International collaboration networks showed strong global partnerships, and keyword analysis revealed increasing focus on photobiomodulation therapy, oral mucositis, chemotherapy, radiotherapy, and prevention."},{"label":"CONCLUSION","text":"These findings indicate rapid global expansion and increasing scientific and clinical relevance of photobiomodulation therapy research, providing valuable insights into scientific development, key contributors, and emerging research directions that support future advances in evidence-based oral healthcare. However, the bibliometric nature of the study, heterogeneity of the included literature, and the lack of direct assessment of clinical outcomes should be considered as limitations."}],"keywords":["bibliometrics","chemotherapy","oral mucositis","photobiomodulation","radiotherapy"]},{"pmid":"42234177","title_fr":"PBM et mucite : expérience d'un programme de soins de support dans un institut indien d'oncologie ORL","type":"cohort","level":4,"pop":"adulte","ctx":["ORL"],"aim":"traitement","mode":[],"nm":[],"n":132,"effect":"favorable","theme":"Vie réelle","l1":"En Inde, 132 patients ORL traités par PBM dès les premiers signes de mucite ont eu des mucites moins sévères et moins longues que 100 témoins historiques, avec moins d'opioïdes (57 % contre 85 %).","l2":"Cohorte observationnelle dans un centre caritatif. La PBM débutait à l'apparition d'une mucite (OMS ≥ 1), donc en mode curatif précoce. La mucite sévère passait de 57 % au début de la PBM à 2 % à 8 semaines.\n\nPar rapport à des témoins historiques appariés : grade maximal plus faible (2,9 contre 3,5), mucite sévère plus courte (1,2 contre 3,4 semaines), moins d'opioïdes. Les auteurs présentent la PBM comme un complément réaliste dans les contextes à ressources limitées.","l3":{"points":["132 patients PBM contre 100 témoins historiques.","Grade maximal 2,9 contre 3,5 ; durée de mucite sévère 1,2 contre 3,4 semaines ; opioïdes 57 % contre 85 % (p < 0,01)."],"limites":["Témoins historiques : soins de support et techniques de radiothérapie ont pu changer entre les périodes.","Paramètres de PBM non précisés dans le résumé."],"pratique":"Intéressant pour l'implantation dans des structures à moyens limités ; preuve de faible niveau."},"topic":"mucite-orale","title":"Photobiomodulation therapy for oral mucositis: clinical experience from a supportive care program at the Indian Institute of Head and Neck Oncology.","journal":"Supportive care in cancer : official journal of the Multinational Association of Supportive Care in Cancer","journal_abbr":"Support Care Cancer","authors":["Dharkar D","Kulkarni K","Bensadoun RJ","et al."],"date":"2026-06-03","year":2026,"week":"2026-S23","doi":"10.1007/s00520-026-10823-7","pmcid":null,"license":null,"oa":false,"pdf":null,"cited_by":0,"pub_types":["Observational Study"],"abstract":[{"label":"BACKGROUND","text":"Photobiomodulation (PBM), the therapeutic use of non-ionizing red to near-infrared light, has emerged as a safe, non-invasive, evidence-based modality for managing oral mucositis (OM) in oncology."},{"label":"OBJECTIVE","text":"To evaluate the feasibility and therapeutic outcomes of photobiomodulation therapy (PBMT) within a structured multidisciplinary supportive care pathway at a charitable oncology center in India, specifically comparing outcomes against a historical control cohort."},{"label":"METHODS","text":"This observational cohort study included 132 patients with head and neck squamous cell carcinoma undergoing curative or adjuvant radiotherapy/chemoradiotherapy. PBMT was initiated at first clinical evidence of mucositis (WHO grade ≥ 1). Baseline for analysis was defined as the time of PBMT initiation. Outcomes were compared with a matched historical control cohort (n = 100) treated prior to PBMT implementation."},{"label":"RESULTS","text":"Severe mucositis (grades 3-4) in the PBMT cohort declined from 57% at baseline (time of PBMT initiation) to 2% at 8 weeks. The PBMT cohort demonstrated significantly superior outcomes compared to historical controls, including lower peak mucositis grades (2.9 vs. 3.5; p < 0.01), shorter duration of severe mucositis (1.2 vs. 3.4 weeks; p < 0.01), and reduced opioid reliance (57% vs. 85%; p < 0.01)."},{"label":"CONCLUSION","text":"PBMT was associated with improved mucositis severity and pain outcomes within a structured supportive care framework in head and neck cancer patients in tertiary cancer center. Compared with historical controls, PBMT was associated with lower peak mucositis grades shorter duration of severe mucositis and reduced opioid use. This findings suggest that PBMT may represent a practical adjunct in resource constrain oncology settings."}],"keywords":["Head and neck cancer","Low-resource setting","Oral mucositis","Photobiomodulation therapy","Supportive care"]},{"pmid":"42098536","title_fr":"PBM pour traiter et prévenir la mucite chimio- et/ou radio-induite : méta-analyse des essais randomisés des six dernières années","type":"ma","level":1,"pop":"mixte","ctx":["mixte"],"aim":"les deux","mode":["intrabuccal","extrabuccal","laser"],"nm":[635,660,980],"n":329,"effect":"favorable","theme":"Synthèse des preuves","metric":{"kind":"rr","outcome":"Mucite (incidence/sévérité)","rr":0.5,"lo":0.35,"hi":0.73,"k":7},"l1":"Méta-analyse de 7 essais randomisés récents (2020–2026, 329 patients) : la PBM divise par deux le risque de mucite (RR 0,50 ; IC95 % 0,35–0,73).","l2":"Revue systématique (PRISMA) sur de nombreuses bases, dont LILACS et Redalyc, limitée aux essais randomisés publiés depuis 2020. L'hétérogénéité est modérée (I² = 40 %).\n\nLes longueurs d'onde vont de 635 à 980 nm, avec 660 nm en intrabuccal le plus souvent ; les temps d'application intrabuccale varient de 3 à 20 s par point. Tous les essais utilisent l'échelle OMS. Les auteurs concluent à une forte efficacité, avec un faible risque de biais, mais l'effectif total reste limité.","l3":{"points":["7 ECR, 329 patients, période 2020–2026.","RR 0,50 (IC95 % 0,35–0,73) ; I² = 40 %.","Paramètres : 635–980 nm ; 660 nm majoritaire en intrabuccal."],"limites":["Petit nombre d'essais retenus sur une fenêtre de six ans.","Mélange prévention et traitement, chimiothérapie et radiothérapie."],"pratique":"Confirme l'ordre de grandeur de l'effet préventif, environ 50 % de mucites en moins, cohérent avec les autres méta-analyses."},"topic":"mucite-orale","title":"Photobiomodulation for the treatment and prevention of chemotherapy- and/or radiotherapy-induced oral mucositis in cancer patients: a systematic review and meta-analysis of randomized clinical trials published in the last six years.","journal":"Supportive care in cancer : official journal of the Multinational Association of Supportive Care in Cancer","journal_abbr":"Support Care Cancer","authors":["G R","R EP","A D","et al."],"date":"2026-05-07","year":2026,"week":"2026-S19","doi":"10.1007/s00520-026-10729-4","pmcid":null,"license":null,"oa":false,"pdf":null,"cited_by":0,"pub_types":["Systematic Review","Meta-Analysis"],"abstract":[{"label":"PURPOSE","text":"To systematically evaluate and update the evidence about photobiomodulation therapy (PBMT) as a therapy for the prevention and/or treatment of oral mucositis (OM) induced by antineoplastic therapies."},{"label":"METHODS","text":"This systematic review followed the Preferred Reporting Items for Systematic Reviews and Meta-Analyses (PRISMA) guidelines. A systematic literature search was conducted across PubMed, Web of Science, Scopus, and Embase as the primary databases. Additional searches were performed in the Virtual Health Library (VHL) and LILACS through the BIREME platform, as well as in Redalyc and the Wiley Online Library, covering the period from 2020 to 2026. Only randomized clinical trials published within the last six years investigating PBMT as a preventive or therapeutic intervention for oral mucositis (OM) in cancer patients receiving chemotherapy and/or radiotherapy were included."},{"label":"RESULTS","text":"Seven studies comprising 329 patients were included. PBMT proved to be highly effective in the management of OM (RR 0.50, 95% CI 0.35-0.73). Moderate heterogeneity was observed across studies (I2 = 40%). PBMT protocols varied, with laser wavelengths ranging from 635 to 980 nm, most commonly 660 nm for intraoral (IO) application. Application sites included IO and/or extraoral (EO) approaches, with IO application times ranging from 3 to 20 s. OM was consistently assessed using World Health Organization (WHO) criteria across all studies. The included trials demonstrated a low risk of bias and publication bias."},{"label":"CONCLUSION","text":"Evidence from recent randomized clinical trials confirms that PBMT is highly effective for the management of oral mucositis, reducing its incidence and severity, improving quality of life and treatment adherence, and potentially lowering healthcare costs during cancer therapy."}],"keywords":["Cancer","Chemotherapy","Oral Mucositis","Photobiomodulation","Radiotherapy"]},{"pmid":"42082093","title_fr":"Complications bucco-dentaires et leur prise en charge chez les patients atteints de cancer ORL : revue parapluie","type":"umbrella","level":1,"pop":"adulte","ctx":["ORL"],"aim":"les deux","mode":[],"nm":[],"n":null,"effect":"favorable","theme":"Synthèse des preuves","l1":"131 revues systématiques sur les complications buccales des cancers ORL : la PBM est associée à moins de mucites, mais l'ensemble des preuves reste de faible confiance.","l2":"Revue parapluie enregistrée (PROSPERO), méthode JBI/Cochrane, qualité évaluée par AMSTAR-2 et recouvrement des études mesuré. La mucite est la complication la plus étudiée, devant xérostomie, dysphagie, trismus, caries et dysgueusie.\n\nLa PBM ressort comme associée à une réduction de la mucite ; l'IMRT réduit la xérostomie et l'ostéoradionécrose mandibulaire. La plupart des revues sont de confiance faible ou critiquement faible.","l3":{"points":["131 revues systématiques publiées depuis 2015 ; recherche au 19 juin 2025.","Recouvrement des études primaires mesuré (CCA)."],"limites":["Synthèse narrative, pas de méta-analyse."],"pratique":"La PBM fait partie des rares interventions buccales en ORL dont le signal est cohérent ; les preuves globales restent faibles."},"topic":"mucite-orale","title":"Oral and dental complications and management in head and neck cancer patients: An umbrella review.","journal":"Journal of dentistry","journal_abbr":"J Dent","authors":["Craig R","O'Kane R","Rodriguez J","et al."],"date":"2026-05-03","year":2026,"week":"2026-S18","doi":"10.1016/j.jdent.2026.106731","pmcid":null,"license":"cc by","oa":false,"pdf":null,"cited_by":0,"pub_types":["Systematic Review"],"abstract":[{"label":"OBJECTIVES","text":"To synthesise evidence on oral and dental side-effects experienced by head and neck cancer (HNC) patients, assess the strength and quality of existing systematic reviews, evaluate the evidence for prevention and management interventions and identify key gaps to inform future research."},{"label":"DATA","text":"Systematic reviews (with/without meta-analysis) published from 2015 onwards involving adults with HNC reporting oral/dental complications and/or interventions were included."},{"label":"SOURCES","text":"MEDLINE (OVID), Embase, Cochrane Database of Systematic Reviews and Scopus were searched (19 June 2025). Two reviewers screened and extracted data independently."},{"label":"STUDY SELECTION","text":"An umbrella review (PROSPERO CRD420251063188) was conducted following JBI/Cochrane guidance and reported in line with PRISMA 2020. Methodological quality was assessed using AMSTAR-2 and primary-study overlap quantified using corrected-covered-area (CCA). Owing to heterogeneity, findings were synthesised narratively by domain."},{"label":"CONCLUSIONS","text":"A total of 131 systematic reviews were included, with most rated as low or critically low confidence. Oral mucositis was the most frequently reported complication, followed by xerostomia, dysphagia, trismus, dental caries, and dysgeusia; osteoradionecrosis was less common. Preventive and therapeutic interventions are widely studied but largely supported by low-quality evidence. Photobiomodulation was associated with reduced oral mucositis, while intensity-modulated radiotherapy was associated with reduced xerostomia and mandibular osteoradionecrosis. Overall, the evidence base is fragmented and of low confidence. Standardised outcome measures and high-quality prospective studies are needed to strengthen the evidence base and improve survivorship care."},{"label":"CLINICAL SIGNIFICANCE","text":"Head and neck cancer patients experience a high burden of oral complications, yet dental management strategies are supported by limited high-quality evidence. This review highlights key areas of uncertainty and reinforces the need for multidisciplinary, evidence-based care, while identifying priorities for research to improve long-term oral health and quality of life."}],"keywords":["Cancer survivorship","Chemoradiotherapy","Dental care","Dental complications","Dysgeusia","Dysphagia","Head and neck cancer/neoplasms","Oral complications","Oral health-related quality of life","Oral mucositis"]},{"pmid":"42060123","title_fr":"PBM et açaï clarifié pour protéger la glande submandibulaire pendant la chimiothérapie : étude chez le rat","type":"preclinical","level":5,"pop":"—","ctx":["chimio"],"aim":"prévention","mode":["laser"],"nm":[660],"n":102,"effect":"favorable","theme":"Préclinique","l1":"Chez le rat avec mucite induite par 5-FU, la PBM (660 nm), seule ou avec de l'açaï, protège la glande submandibulaire du stress oxydatif et des lésions structurelles.","l2":"102 rats répartis en cinq groupes (témoin, mucite non traitée, PBM, açaï, PBM + açaï). PBM à 660 nm, 100 mW, 0,24 J par point, appliquée sur la zone de mucite.\n\nLa PBM augmente la capacité antioxydante et réduit peroxydation lipidique et métabolites du monoxyde d'azote ; l'architecture glandulaire reste proche de celle des animaux sains. Même équipe que l'étude 2025 sur la parotide.","l3":{"points":["Mucite chimio-induite : 5-FU 60 puis 40 mg/kg.","Analyses à J8, J10, J14 : biochimie oxydative et histologie."],"limites":["Modèle animal ; transposition à l'humain incertaine."],"pratique":"Argument mécanistique pour un effet de la PBM sur les glandes salivaires pendant la chimiothérapie."},"topic":"mucite-orale","title":"Preclinical evidence of photobiomodulation and clarified açaí in submandibular gland protection during antineoplastic therapy: an experimental study.","journal":"Lasers in medical science","journal_abbr":"Lasers Med Sci","authors":["Melo WWP","Aragão WAB","Bittencourt LO","et al."],"date":"2026-04-30","year":2026,"week":"2026-S18","doi":"10.1007/s10103-026-04876-7","pmcid":"PMC13132951","license":"cc by","oa":true,"pdf":"pdfs/42060123.pdf","cited_by":0,"pub_types":[],"abstract":[{"label":"","text":"PURPOSE: This study aimed to investigate the biochemical and morphological effects of photobiomodulation (PBM) and supplementation with clarified açaí on the submandibular glands in a chemically induced oral mucositis model in rats. Methods: 102 rats were divided into five groups- control: no induction of oral mucositis (OM); untreated group: OM induction, no treatment; PBM: OM induction and laser application (660 nm, 100 mW, 0.24 J, 2.4 s per point, 8 J/cm2 and in contact only at the OM site, bilaterally); açaí: OM induction and supplementation with clarified açaí; and PBM + açaí: OM induction and combination of the two treatments mentioned above. Oral mucositis was chemically induced by 5-fluorouracil (5-FU) on days 0 (60 mg/kg) and 2 (40 mg/kg). PMB and supplementation with clarified açaí (dose of 1 ml/100 g body weight) were used from day 0 of the experiment until euthanasia. On days 0 (control group), 8, 10, and 14 (intervention groups), submandibular glands were collected for analysis, with the right side used for oxidative biochemical analysis and the left side for histological analysis. A two-way statistical analysis of variance (ANOVA) was performed, followed by Tukey's post-hoc test (p < 0.05). Results: PMB, alone or in combination with clarified açaí, significantly increased antioxidant capacity levels and showed significantly lower levels of lipid peroxidation and nitric oxide metabolites compared to the control group (p < 0.0001). Morphologically, PBM, alone or combined with clarified açaí, maintained parenchyma, stroma, and acinar structures similar to the control group, which was not subjected to OM induction. Conclusion: It was experimentally demonstrated that PBM, when used alone or combined with clarified açaí, resulted in improved antioxidant response, in addition to providing glandular protection against structural damage caused by chemotherapy."}],"keywords":["Euterpe oleracea Mart—Antioxidant—Submandibular salivary gland","Chemotherapy","Photobiomodulation","Rat"]},{"pmid":"42052855","title_fr":"Pourquoi et quand les interventions validées sont-elles adoptées en soins de support pédiatriques ? Étude qualitative sur l'implantation de la PBM","type":"qualitative","level":0,"pop":"pédiatrie","ctx":["mixte"],"aim":"prévention","mode":["laser","LED"],"nm":[],"n":27,"effect":"—","theme":"Implantation","l1":"Au Royaume-Uni, la PBM est recommandée mais peu utilisée en oncopédiatrie : son adoption dépend moins des preuves que de l'organisation locale (personnel, réseaux, standardisation). Les systèmes LED progressent.","l2":"Entretiens avec 27 personnes (18 soignants de 11 centres, 1 industriel, 4 binômes enfant/parent) analysés selon le cadre CFIR. Cinq thèmes : présenter la PBM comme un soin de routine, une intervention simple et gratifiante, le besoin de réseaux multiprofessionnels, l'équilibre entre individualisation et standardisation, et l'essor des LED, qui posent moins d'obstacles.\n\nLes auteurs recommandent des stratégies d'implantation fondées sur la théorie pour réduire les inégalités d'accès.","l3":{"points":["Expérience des participants : laser (n = 15), LED (n = 13).","Analyse déductive (CFIR) puis inductive, avec des groupes de patients partenaires."],"limites":["Étude qualitative, contexte britannique."],"pratique":"Pour implanter la PBM : personnel dédié, protocole standardisé, réseau régional ; les LED facilitent le déploiement."},"topic":"mucite-orale","title":"Why and When Are Evidence-Based Interventions Adopted in Paediatric Supportive Care? A Qualitative Exploration of the Determinants of Photobiomodulation Implementation.","journal":"Pediatric blood & cancer","journal_abbr":"Pediatr Blood Cancer","authors":["Heggie C","Gray-Burrows KA","Day PF","et al."],"date":"2026-04-29","year":2026,"week":"2026-S18","doi":"10.1002/1545-5017.70282","pmcid":null,"license":"cc by","oa":false,"pdf":null,"cited_by":0,"pub_types":[],"abstract":[{"label":"BACKGROUND","text":"Oral mucositis is a common and debilitating side effect of childhood cancer and stem cell transplant treatments. It affects the quality of life of children and young people (CYP) and places a strain on services. Photobiomodulation is recommended for oral mucositis prevention in international guidance but is poorly implemented in UK Principal Treatment Centres (PTCs). Our study explored the key barriers and facilitators to implementing photobiomodulation into standard care."},{"label":"METHODS","text":"CYP, their parents, and healthcare professionals (HCPs) were purposively sampled for diversity in theoretically important characteristics: stage of adoption of PTC, professional role (HCPs), age (CYP), and photobiomodulation modality. Initial deductive framework analysis on semi-structured interview data was guided by the Consolidated Framework for Implementation Research (CFIR). Qualitative summaries were then inductively generated, articulating the valence of influence and effect. Overarching themes were developed and refined in collaboration with patient involvement groups."},{"label":"RESULTS","text":"Twenty-seven participants were interviewed: 18 HCPs from 11 diverse PTCs, 1 industry representative, and 4 CYP/Parent dyads. Participants had experience with low-level laser (n = 15) and light-emitting diode (LED) (n = 13) systems. Perceived barriers and facilitators varied between PTCs, participant groups, and photobiomodulation systems. Five themes were developed: (1) framing photobiomodulation as essential to routine care in busy cancer services; (2) photobiomodulation as simple, proactive, and rewarding; (3) need for navigable, interconnected, multiprofessional networks to support implementation; (4) balancing individualisation of services with the need for guidance and standardisation; and (5) increasing implementation of LED systems, with fewer barriers, over time."},{"label":"CONCLUSION","text":"Implementing photobiomodulation in paediatric oncology depends less on its clinical evidence and more on the local conditions that enable reliable delivery. This study highlights the importance of aligning service structures, workforce capacity, and professional networks to 'embed' photobiomodulation as routine care. Theory-informed implementation strategies can reduce current inequities in access and provide a transferable model for introducing other evidence-based supportive care interventions."}],"keywords":["implementation","oral health","oral mucositis","photobiomodulation","supportive care"]},{"pmid":"41999121","title_fr":"Association de l'ILIB (irradiation laser du sang) et de la PBM dans la mucite : étude prospective","type":"nrct","level":3,"pop":"adulte","ctx":["mixte"],"aim":"les deux","mode":["laser"],"nm":[],"n":76,"effect":"favorable","theme":"Associations","l1":"Chez 76 patients, l'ILIB associée à la PBM buccale réduit davantage la mucite (−55 à −61 %) et améliore plus la qualité de vie que l'ILIB seule (−25 %).","l2":"Étude quasi expérimentale à quatre groupes de 19 patients : ILIB seule ou ILIB + laser buccal, en préventif ou en curatif. La mucite (OMS) et la qualité de vie (OHIP-14) étaient mesurées avant et après.\n\nLes groupes combinés ont mieux répondu. Il manque un groupe PBM buccale seule : on ne peut pas savoir ce qu'apporte l'ILIB par rapport à la PBM locale, qui est le standard.","l3":{"points":["Mucite : −55,2 % et −60,7 % (combiné) contre −25 % et −25,9 % (ILIB seule).","OHIP-14 : −31 % et −36 % contre −15,8 % et −15,5 %."],"limites":["Non randomisé.","Pas de bras PBM locale seule : l'apport de l'ILIB n'est pas démontré."],"pratique":"Ne justifie pas l'ILIB ; confirme surtout l'intérêt de la PBM buccale locale."},"topic":"mucite-orale","title":"Effectiveness of the Association Between ILIB and Photobiomodulation in Oral Mucositis Among Oncology Patients: A Prospective Study.","journal":"Head & neck","journal_abbr":"Head Neck","authors":["do Nascimento CRE","Dantas FSB","Pereira TF","et al."],"date":"2026-04-17","year":2026,"week":"2026-S16","doi":"10.1002/hed.70285","pmcid":null,"license":null,"oa":false,"pdf":null,"cited_by":0,"pub_types":[],"abstract":[{"label":"BACKGROUND/PURPOSE","text":"Oral mucositis is one of the most frequent and debilitating adverse effects of antineoplastic therapy, significantly impairing treatment adherence and patients' quality of life. This study aimed to evaluate the effectiveness of Intravenous Laser Irradiation of Blood (ILIB), applied alone or combined with low-level laser therapy, in reducing oral mucositis and improving the quality of life of oncology patients."},{"label":"METHODS","text":"This quasi-experimental prospective study with parallel groups was conducted between 2023 and 2024 at the Memorial Hospital of Arcoverde (PE), involving 76 patients equally allocated into four groups (n = 19): ILIB alone (A and C) and ILIB combined with laser therapy (B and D), applied therapeutically or preventively over weeks. Oral mucositis grade (WHO scale) and quality of life (OHIP-14) were assessed before and after the interventions."},{"label":"RESULTS","text":"No baseline differences were observed between groups (p > 0.05). After the intervention, the combined therapy groups showed greater reductions in mucositis (B: -1.60; -55.2% and D: -1.70; -60.7%) compared with ILIB alone (A: -0.70; -25.0% and C: -0.70; -25.9%) (p < 0.05). Improvements in quality of life were also more pronounced in the combined groups (B: -9.00; -31.0% and D: -10.40; -36.0%) compared with ILIB alone (A: -4.50; -15.8% and C: -4.30; -15.5%) (p < 0.05)."},{"label":"CONCLUSION","text":"The ILIB + laser therapy association was more effective than ILIB alone in reducing oral mucositis and improving quality of life, both in prevention and treatment contexts."}],"keywords":["Oral manifestations","low‐level light therapy","photobiomodulation","quality of life","stomatitis"]},{"pmid":"42073454","title_fr":"Toxicité cytokinique et dysbiose bactérienne dans la mucite chimio- et radio-induite : mécanismes et traitements","type":"review","level":5,"pop":"—","ctx":["mixte"],"aim":"—","mode":[],"nm":[],"n":null,"effect":"—","theme":"Mécanismes","l1":"Revue des deux moteurs de la mucite, l'excès de cytokines pro-inflammatoires et la dysbiose ; la PBM figure parmi les approches anti-inflammatoires validées chez l'animal.","l2":"IL-1β, IL-6, TNF, iNOS et monoxyde d'azote amplifient la lésion ; la perte des bactéries commensales l'aggrave. La revue recense pentoxifylline, atorvastatine, IL-11 recombinante, laser basse énergie, probiotiques et transplantation de microbiote oral.\n\nElle fournit un cadre de lecture des mécanismes sans apport clinique direct sur la PBM.","l3":{"points":["Cible commune : la cascade inflammatoire et la barrière muqueuse."],"limites":["Revue narrative."],"pratique":"Aucune conséquence directe."},"topic":"mucite-orale","title":"Cytokine Toxicity and Bacterial Dysbiosis in Chemotherapy- and/or Radiotherapy-Induced Oral Mucositis: Pathophysiological Mechanisms and Therapeutic Interventions.","journal":"Life (Basel, Switzerland)","journal_abbr":"Life (Basel)","authors":["Abdolmohammadi P","Aali M","Lehmann C"],"date":"2026-04-11","year":2026,"week":"2026-S15","doi":"10.3390/life16040644","pmcid":"PMC13117281","license":"cc by","oa":true,"pdf":"pdfs/42073454.pdf","cited_by":0,"pub_types":["Review"],"abstract":[{"label":"","text":"Chemotherapy- and/or radiotherapy-induced oral mucositis (CRIOM) is a common complication in patients with head and neck cancer, driven largely by excessive proinflammatory cytokine signalling and treatment-associated bacterial dysbiosis. This narrative review synthesizes current mechanistic evidence and summarizes emerging therapeutic strategies targeting these pathways. Research indicates that elevated levels of IL-1β, IL-6, TNF, iNOS, and nitric oxide amplify tissue injury and ulceration, while disruption of oral and gut microbial communities, characterized by loss of beneficial commensals and enrichment of pathogenic taxa, further exacerbates mucosal inflammation. Anti-inflammatory agents, including pentoxifylline, atorvastatin, trans-caryophyllene, azilsartan, recombinant human IL-11, and low-level laser therapy have been shown in preclinical models to reduce cytokine levels and promote mucosal healing. Similarly, microbiome-targeted approaches, such as oral microbiota transplantation and multi-strain probiotic formulations, have demonstrated potential in restoring microbial balance and attenuating CRIOM severity, with current evidence including both preclinical and clinical studies. Overall, current findings highlight cytokine toxicity and dysbiosis as synergistic drivers of CRIOM and support anti-inflammatory and microbiome-modulating strategies as promising adjunctive approaches; however, further well-designed clinical studies are required to validate their efficacy and guide clinical translation."}],"keywords":["anti-inflammatory","bacterial dysbiosis","chemotherapy","cytokine","cytokine toxicity","head and neck cancer","microbiome","oral microbiota manipulation","oral mucositis","radiotherapy"]},{"pmid":"41935072","title_fr":"Effet de la PBM intra- et extrabuccale sur le stress oxydatif et les cytokines salivaires pendant la radiothérapie ORL","type":"nrct","level":4,"pop":"adulte","ctx":["ORL"],"aim":"prévention","mode":["intrabuccal","extrabuccal"],"nm":[],"n":18,"effect":"favorable","theme":"Mécanismes","l1":"Chez 18 patients irradiés, la PBM est associée à une hausse des défenses antioxydantes salivaires (SOD, glutathion) et à une baisse d'IL-6 ; moins de 40 % ont eu une mucite sévère.","l2":"Étude biologique brésilienne : 10 patients en PBM intrabuccale, 8 en extrabuccale, cinq fois par semaine dès le début de la radiothérapie, et 20 témoins sains. Prélèvements salivaires avant, à mi-parcours et en fin de radiothérapie.\n\nSOD et glutathion augmentaient sous PBM, IL-6 et IL-10 diminuaient ; la myéloperoxydase restait élevée. Tous les patients ont eu une mucite, mais moins de 40 % une forme sévère.","l3":{"points":["Marqueurs : MPO, MDA, SOD, GSH, IL-6, IL-10.","Pic transitoire de MDA à mi-parcours, sans lien avec la sévérité."],"limites":["Très petit effectif, pas de groupe irradié sans PBM."],"pratique":"Données mécanistiques ; pas de conséquence pratique directe."},"topic":"mucite-orale","title":"Effect of intraoral and extraoral photobiomodulation on salivary oxidative stress and inflammatory cytokines in patients with radiotherapy-induced oral mucositis.","journal":"Scientific reports","journal_abbr":"Sci Rep","authors":["Loureiro FJA","Klein IP","de Farias Gabriel A","et al."],"date":"2026-04-04","year":2026,"week":"2026-S14","doi":"10.1038/s41598-026-43141-3","pmcid":"PMC13201865","license":"cc by-nc-nd","oa":true,"pdf":"pdfs/41935072.pdf","cited_by":0,"pub_types":[],"abstract":[{"label":"","text":"Photobiomodulation (PBM) is an evidence-based approach for managing oral mucositis (OM) in patients undergoing head and neck radiotherapy (RT), though its biological mechanisms remain under investigation. This study evaluated salivary oxidative stress (OS) and inflammatory biomarkers in patients with head and neck squamous cell carcinoma (HNSCC) who received intraoral (IOPBM, n = 10) or extraoral PBM (EOPBM, n = 8) as supportive therapy during radiotherapy, and not as a cancer treatment. Preventive IOPBM/EOPBM were applied five times weekly from RT initiation until OM healing. Twenty healthy individuals served as the control group. Unstimulated saliva was collected at pre-, mid-, and final-RT (last RT session) to assess myeloperoxidase (MPO), malondialdehyde (MDA), superoxide dismutase (SOD), glutathione (GSH), interleukin-6 (IL-6), and interleukin-10 (IL-10). HNSCC patients exhibited altered oxidative and inflammatory biomarker profiles during RT. MDA levels showed a transient increase at mid-RT (p < 0.05), particularly in the IOPBM group, but were not associated with OM severity (p > 0.05). MPO activity was significantly higher at all RT time points and across OM severity grades compared with CT (p < 0.05–0.01). In contrast, SOD activity were consistently elevated throughout RT in PBM-treated patients compared with CT (p < 0.001), and GSH levels increased at mid-RT (p < 0.01). IL-6 and IL-10 levels decreased from pre- to final-RT compared to CT (p < 0.05). Clinically, all patients developed OM, although fewer than 40% experienced severe OM. These findings suggest that PBM enhances antioxidant defenses and modulates inflammatory responses, supporting its role in reducing RT-induced oral toxicity."}],"keywords":["Cytokines","Inflammation","Low-level laser therapy","Oxidative stress"]},{"pmid":"41948009","title_fr":"Relation dose-effet entre radiothérapie et mucite chez les patients ORL : étude rétrospective","type":"cohort","level":4,"pop":"adulte","ctx":["ORL"],"aim":"prévention","mode":[],"nm":[],"n":121,"effect":"neutre","theme":"Facteurs de risque","l1":"Sur 121 patients irradiés, le risque de mucite dépend surtout de la localisation intrabuccale de la tumeur et de la dose ; le laser préventif montre une tendance protectrice qui disparaît après ajustement.","l2":"Analyse rétrospective (radiothérapie conformationnelle 3D, Brésil, 2020–2023). 42 % des patients ont eu une mucite. Tumeur intrabuccale (OR ajusté 9,1), nombre de séances, dose cumulée et sexe féminin étaient liés à sa survenue.\n\nLa PBM préventive n'était pas significative en analyse multivariée. Cela rappelle que les études observationnelles peinent à isoler son effet face aux facteurs de risque dominants.","l3":{"points":["Tumeur intrabuccale : ORa 9,06 ; par séance : ORa 1,11 ; par Gy : ORa 1,05 ; femme : ORa 3,10.","Aucune mucite de grade 4."],"limites":["Rétrospectif ; usage de la PBM non randomisé ni standardisé."],"pratique":"Utile pour cibler les patients à haut risque (tumeur buccale, forte dose) chez qui la PBM est prioritaire."},"topic":"mucite-orale","title":"Dose-Dependence Relationship Between Radiotherapy and Oral Mucositis in Patients With Head and Neck Cancer: A Retrospective Study.","journal":"International journal of dentistry","journal_abbr":"Int J Dent","authors":["França SR","Pinto FC","Oliveira VB","et al."],"date":"2026-04-03","year":2026,"week":"2026-S14","doi":"10.1155/ijod/3972232","pmcid":"PMC13051859","license":"cc by","oa":true,"pdf":"pdfs/41948009.pdf","cited_by":0,"pub_types":[],"abstract":[{"label":"INTRODUCTION AND PURPOSE","text":"To evaluate the association between radiotherapy (RT) exposure and the presence and severity of radiation-induced oral mucositis (RIOM) in patients with head and neck cancer (HNC)."},{"label":"METHODS","text":"This retrospective observational study analyzed medical records of 121 patients with HNC treated with 3D conformal RT at a referral hospital in Brazil (2020-2023). Sociodemographic, clinical, and RT-related variables were collected. RIOM was clinically assessed using World Health Organization (WHO) criteria and analyzed using three approaches: presence of RIOM, overall severity (grades 0-4), and severity among affected patients (grades 1-4). Analyses included descriptive, bivariate, and adjusted multivariable binary and ordinal logistic regression models."},{"label":"RESULTS","text":"RIOM was identified in 51 patients (42.1%). No cases of WHO grade 4 were observed. In multivariable analysis, intraoral tumor (aOR = 9.06; 95% CI: 3.51-23.38; p < 0.001), number of RT sessions (aOR = 1.11 per session; 95% CI: 1.04-1.17; p  < 0.001), cumulative dose (aOR = 1.05 per Gy; 95% CI: 1.02-1.08; p = 0.001) and female sex (aOR = 3.10; 95% CI: 1.13-8.43; p = 0.027) were independently associated with RIOM presence. Intraoral tumor and increasing RT exposure remained significantly associated with greater RIOM severity (p  < 0.01) in ordinal models. Preventive laser therapy showed a protective trend in bivariate analysis but did not remain significant after adjustment."},{"label":"CONCLUSION","text":"RT exposure and intraoral tumor location were the main factors associated with both the presence and severity of RIOM, highlighting the importance of anatomical site and radiation burden for risk stratification."}],"keywords":["epidemiology","head and neck cancer","observational study","oral mucositis","radiotherapy"]},{"pmid":"41948031","title_fr":"Zinc L-carnosine et PBM Nd:YAG pour la mucite radio-induite : série de cas","type":"cases","level":4,"pop":"adulte","ctx":["ORL"],"aim":"traitement","mode":["laser"],"nm":[1064],"n":3,"effect":"favorable","theme":"Associations","l1":"Trois patients irradiés avec mucite de grade 2–3 : bain de bouche au zinc L-carnosine et PBM au laser Nd:YAG soulagent en deux jours et cicatrisent en trois semaines.","l2":"Série italienne (Parme) : cancers de l'oropharynx, de la langue et du voile. La surface des lésions était mesurée par analyse d'image (Fiji).\n\nTrois cas, sans témoin : simple piste pour une association inhabituelle (Nd:YAG à 1 064 nm).","l3":{"points":["2 mucites OMS III, 1 OMS II.","Douleur nettement réduite à J2, cicatrisation complète en 3 semaines."],"limites":["3 cas, sans comparateur ; évolution naturelle possible."],"pratique":"Pas de recommandation possible."},"topic":"mucite-orale","title":"A Case Series on the Combined Use of Zinc L-Carnosine and Nd:YAG Photobiomodulation for Radiation-Induced Oral Mucositis: Pain Reduction and Lesion Improvement.","journal":"Case reports in dentistry","journal_abbr":"Case Rep Dent","authors":["De Francesco P","Vescovi P","Grondelli C","et al."],"date":"2026-04-03","year":2026,"week":"2026-S14","doi":"10.1155/crid/7025093","pmcid":"PMC13051797","license":"cc by","oa":true,"pdf":"pdfs/41948031.pdf","cited_by":1,"pub_types":[],"abstract":[{"label":"","text":"Radiation-induced oral mucositis (RIOM) is a severe complication in patients with head and neck cancer (HNC) undergoing radiotherapy (RT). Despite several proposed strategies for prevention and treatment, the results remain inconsistent and unpredictable. A combined approach with zinc L-carnosine (ZnC) mouthwash and Nd:YAG laser photobiomodulation (PBM) was evaluated in three patients affected by RIOM, treated at the Unit of Oral Medicine, Pathology and Laser-assisted Surgery of the University of Parma, Italy. One patient had oropharyngeal cancer, one had tongue cancer, and one had soft palate cancer. Two patients presented with World Health Organization (WHO) grade III mucositis, and one with WHO grade II mucositis. The percentage reduction in oral lesions was calculated using Fiji software. All patients reported clinical improvement and a reduction in algic symptoms. A significant decrease in pain was observed after only 2 days, while complete mucosal healing was achieved within 3 weeks. These preliminary results suggest that the combined use of ZnC and Nd:YAG laser PBM may be a promising therapeutic option for patients affected by RIOM, warranting further investigation in larger clinical studies."}],"keywords":["Nd:YAG laser photobiomodulation","case report","oral pathology","radiation-induced oral mucositis","zinc L-carnosine"]},{"pmid":"41880047","title_fr":"Effets de la PBM sur la mucite, la douleur, la xérostomie, le débit salivaire et la qualité de vie en ORL : revue systématique et méta-analyse","type":"ma","level":1,"pop":"adulte","ctx":["ORL"],"aim":"les deux","mode":[],"nm":[],"n":1748,"effect":"favorable","theme":"Synthèse des preuves","key":true,"metric":{"kind":"rr","outcome":"Mucite sévère","rr":0.46,"lo":0.29,"hi":0.71,"k":30},"l1":"La plus grande méta-analyse de 2026 (30 essais, 1 748 patients ORL) : la PBM réduit de 54 % le risque de mucite sévère et de 65 % celui de douleur sévère, sans effet net sur la xérostomie. Certitude faible à très faible.","l2":"Méta-analyse à trois niveaux d'essais randomisés (recherche jusqu'en avril 2025). La PBM réduit la mucite sévère (RR 0,46 ; IC95 % 0,29–0,71) et la douleur sévère (RR 0,35 ; 0,23–0,53) et améliore le débit salivaire (DMS 0,75).\n\nElle ne modifie pas significativement la xérostomie ressentie (DMS −0,07) ; la qualité de vie montre une tendance favorable non significative (DMS 1,06 ; p = 0,06). Les bénéfices sont stables d'un protocole à l'autre. La certitude GRADE va de très faible à faible : la plupart des essais ont un risque de biais élevé.","l3":{"points":["33 articles, 30 ECR, 1 748 patients ; modèle à effets mixtes à trois niveaux.","Mucite sévère : RR 0,46 (0,29–0,71) ; douleur sévère : RR 0,35 (0,23–0,53).","Débit salivaire : DMS 0,75 (0,03–1,46) ; xérostomie : DMS −0,07 (NS) ; qualité de vie : DMS 1,06 (−0,03 à 2,14).","Effet cohérent quel que soit le protocole."],"limites":["Certitude GRADE très faible à faible.","Risque de biais élevé dans la majorité des essais."],"pratique":"Référence quantitative de 2026 en ORL : environ deux fois moins de mucites sévères et trois fois moins de douleurs sévères. À présenter avec la réserve sur la qualité des essais."},"topic":"mucite-orale","title":"Effects of photobiomodulation on oral mucositis, oral pain, xerostomia, salivary flow rate, and quality of life in patients with head and neck cancer: a systematic review and meta-analysis.","journal":"Supportive care in cancer : official journal of the Multinational Association of Supportive Care in Cancer","journal_abbr":"Support Care Cancer","authors":["Pedroso MNM","Rech A","Casagrande N","et al."],"date":"2026-03-25","year":2026,"week":"2026-S13","doi":"10.1007/s00520-026-10575-4","pmcid":"PMC13018037","license":"cc by","oa":true,"pdf":"pdfs/41880047.pdf","cited_by":2,"pub_types":["Systematic Review","Meta-Analysis"],"abstract":[{"label":"PURPOSE","text":"To systematically review and analyze the effects of photobiomodulation on oral mucositis, xerostomia, salivary flow rates, oral pain, and quality of life in patients with head and neck cancer."},{"label":"METHODS","text":"A systematic search of six databases was conducted up to April 2025. Eligible randomized controlled trials (RCTs) examined the effects of photobiomodulation on the specified outcomes in patients with head and neck cancer. Pooled estimates (risk ratio [RR] and standardized mean difference [SMD]) were calculated using a three-level mixed-effects meta-analysis."},{"label":"RESULTS","text":"Thirty-three articles describing 30 RCTs (n = 1748) were included. Photobiomodulation significantly reduced the risk of severe oral mucositis (RR 0.46, 95% CI: 0.29-0.71, P < 0.001) and severe oral pain (RR 0.35, 95% CI: 0.23-0.53, P < 0.001) compared with controls. Significant improvements were also observed for salivary flow rate (SMD 0.75, 95% CI: 0.03-1.46, P = 0.044). No significant effects were observed on xerostomia (SMD -0.07, 95% CI: -0.47-0.33, P = 0.646) and quality of life (SMD 1.06, 95% CI: -0.03-2.14, P = 0.060). The benefits were consistent across different photobiomodulation protocols for oral mucositis and quality of life."},{"label":"CONCLUSIONS","text":"Photobiomodulation appears to be a promising supportive care intervention, reducing the risk of severe mucositis and oral pain, while potentially improving salivary flow rates and preserving quality of life in patients with head and neck cancer. However, the certainty of the evidence ranged from very low to low and most included studies presented a high risk of bias; therefore, these findings should be interpreted with caution."}],"keywords":["Head and neck neoplasms","Low-level light therapy","Mouth dryness","Quality of life"]},{"pmid":"41864516","title_fr":"Effet thérapeutique de la PBM sur la mucite radio-induite en ORL : essai prospectif en double aveugle, autocontrôlé","type":"nrct","level":3,"pop":"adulte","ctx":["ORL"],"aim":"traitement","mode":["intrabuccal","laser"],"nm":[660],"n":53,"effect":"favorable","theme":"Traitement curatif","key":true,"metric":{"kind":"rates","outcome":"Cicatrisation complète de la lésion","a_label":"Lésion traitée","a":52.4,"b_label":"Lésion témoin","b":11.9},"l1":"Chez 53 patients ayant deux lésions comparables, la lésion traitée par PBM (660 nm) a guéri bien plus souvent que la lésion témoin (52,4 % contre 11,9 %), avec une douleur soulagée dès la première semaine.","l2":"Plan original en « bouche divisée » : chez chaque patient, une lésion reçoit la PBM (660 nm, 150 mW, 5 J par point, deux fois par semaine) et l'autre une simulation, en double aveugle. Suivi hebdomadaire par OMAS et EVA jusqu'à guérison ou deux semaines après la radiothérapie.\n\nLa douleur baisse dès la première semaine, le score OMAS à partir de la troisième. Le site tumoral primitif était le facteur prédictif de guérison. C'est un des rares essais contrôlés en traitement curatif.","l3":{"points":["163 patients sélectionnés, 53 analysés (deux lésions comparables).","Guérison complète : 52,4 % contre 11,9 % (p < 0,001).","Facteur prédictif : site du cancer primitif (ORa 5,78)."],"limites":["Effet de diffusion possible entre lésions d'une même bouche.","Forte sélection (53 sur 163)."],"pratique":"Soutient la PBM en traitement curatif de la mucite radio-induite installée, deux séances par semaine."},"topic":"mucite-orale","title":"Therapeutic effect of Photobiomodulation therapy on radiation-induced oral mucositis in head and neck Cancer: A prospective double-blind, self-controlled clinical trial.","journal":"Radiotherapy and oncology : journal of the European Society for Therapeutic Radiology and Oncology","journal_abbr":"Radiother Oncol","authors":["Ameri A","Lesan M","Vafaei V","et al."],"date":"2026-03-20","year":2026,"week":"2026-S12","doi":"10.1016/j.radonc.2026.111495","pmcid":null,"license":null,"oa":false,"pdf":null,"cited_by":0,"pub_types":[],"abstract":[{"label":"BACKGROUND","text":"Oral mucositis (OM) is a common and painful complication in patients with head and neck cancer (HNC) undergoing radiotherapy or chemoradiotherapy. Photobiomodulation therapy (PBMT) historically referred to as low-level laser therapy (LLLT) has been suggested as a treatment for reducing OM severity and pain."},{"label":"METHODS","text":"In this prospective, double-blind clinical trial, 163 patients were screened, and 53 patients with two comparable OM lesions were ultimately included in the analysis. One lesion received PBMT (660 nm, 150 mW, 5 J per point, twice weekly), and the other received sham treatment as control. Lesion severity and pain were assessed weekly using the Oral Mucositis Assessment Scale (OMAS) and the Visual Analog Scale (VAS) until complete healing or two weeks after radiotherapy."},{"label":"RESULTS","text":"PBMT significantly reduced OMAS and VAS scores compared to control lesions. VAS reduction was observed from the first week, whereas OMAS decreased from week three. Complete healing occurred in 52.4% of intervention lesions versus 11.9% of control lesions (P < 0.001). PBMT accelerated recovery without adverse effects. According to multivariable regression analysis, Primary cancer site was the independent predictive factor of healing of radiation-induced oral mucositis following PBMT (aOR 5.78, 95% CI 1.28-36.0, P = 0.03)."},{"label":"CONCLUSIONS","text":"PBMT was associated with a significant reduction in pain and lesion severity in patients with radiation-induced oral mucositis undergoing head and neck radiotherapy. These findings support its potential therapeutic role as a supportive care intervention."}],"keywords":["Head and neck cancer","Oral mucositis","Photobiomodulation therapy","Quality of life","Radiotherapy"]},{"pmid":"41841486","title_fr":"Moins de morphine grâce à la PBM en ORL : cohorte appariée de 280 cas","type":"cohort","level":4,"pop":"adulte","ctx":["ORL"],"aim":"prévention","mode":["intrabuccal","extrabuccal","LED","laser"],"nm":[660,810],"n":280,"effect":"mitigé","theme":"Douleur","key":true,"metric":{"kind":"values","outcome":"Morphine en fin de RT (mg/j)","a_label":"PBM","a":22.9,"b_label":"Témoins","b":37.4,"unit":"mg/j"},"l1":"Cohorte française appariée (280 patients) : la PBM préventive diminue la morphine en fin de radiothérapie (22,9 contre 37,4 mg/j) mais pas la fréquence des mucites de grade 3–4 (63 % dans les deux groupes).","l2":"Patients opioïdo-naïfs traités pour cancer de la cavité buccale ou de l'oropharynx. PBM trois fois par semaine par des manipulateurs : 660 nm intrabuccal (2,25 J/cm²) et LED 810 nm extrabuccale (6 J/cm²) sur la zone pharyngolaryngée. Témoins historiques (2012–2019) appariés 1:1 sur le site tumoral, le traitement systémique et la chirurgie.\n\nLa morphine est réduite en fin de radiothérapie et à un mois (10,3 contre 27,2 mg/j) ; 60 % des patients PBM n'ont pas eu besoin de morphine contre 47 %. Le taux de mucite sévère est identique. La PBM agirait donc d'abord sur la douleur, un résultat important pour le choix des critères de jugement.","l3":{"points":["280 patients ; 45 % cavité buccale ; 43 % en postopératoire ; 42 % sans traitement systémique.","Morphine : 22,9 contre 37,4 mg/j (p = 0,002) ; à M1 : 10,3 contre 27,2 (p = 0,0001).","Mucite G3–4 : 63 % contre 63 % ; survie sans mucite G3–4 identique (p = 0,99).","Bénéfice plus marqué en oropharynx, en radiothérapie exclusive et avec traitement concomitant."],"limites":["Témoins historiques (évolution possible des pratiques antalgiques).","Non randomisé."],"pratique":"La PBM préventive peut réduire l'usage d'opioïdes même quand le grade de mucite ne change pas : suivre la douleur et la consommation d'antalgiques, pas seulement le grade."},"topic":"mucite-orale","title":"Reduced Morphine Consumption with Photobiomodulation in Head and Neck Cancer: A Matched Cohort of 280 Cases.","journal":"Photobiomodulation, photomedicine, and laser surgery","journal_abbr":"Photobiomodul Photomed Laser Surg","authors":["Giraud N","Delhiat AC","Lafitte A","et al."],"date":"2026-03-17","year":2026,"week":"2026-S12","doi":"10.1177/25785478261432857","pmcid":null,"license":null,"oa":false,"pdf":null,"cited_by":0,"pub_types":[],"abstract":[{"label":"INTRODUCTION","text":"Radiation-induced oral mucositis (RIOM) may cause severe pain and impaired quality of life during head and neck radiotherapy (RT). Preventive photobiomodulation (PBM) has shown promising clinical benefits."},{"label":"MATERIALS AND METHODS","text":"Eligible patients had oropharyngeal or oral cavity cancer and were opioid-naïve at the start of curative-intent RT. Patients in the PBM group were treated between 2020 and 2023. PBM was applied to the entire oral mucosa at 2.25 J/cm2 (λ = 660 nm). Extraoral PBM was delivered using an LED cluster device at 6 J/cm2 (λ = 810 nm) to target the pharyngolaryngeal mucosa. PBM was administered three times per week by trained radiation therapists. A historical control cohort treated between 2012 and 2019 was matched 1:1 based on tumor site, concurrent systemic therapy, and prior surgery. The primary objective was to evaluate differences in daily morphine consumption at the end of RT according to the preventive use of thrice-weekly oral PBM in patients receiving curative-intent RT for head and neck cancer."},{"label":"RESULTS","text":"A total of 280 patients were included: 45% had oral cavity cancers, 43% were treated postoperatively, and 42% received RT without systemic therapy. Morphine use was significantly lower in the PBM group at the end of RT (22.9 vs. 37.4 mg/day; p = 0.002) and 1 month post-RT (10.3 vs. 27.2 mg/day; p = 0.0001). No morphine was required in 60% of PBM patients compared with 47% of controls. PBM appeared particularly beneficial in oropharyngeal cancer, in patients receiving definitive RT, and with concurrent treatment. In contrast, rates of grade 3-4 RIOM at the end of RT were identical (63% in both groups), and grade 3-4 mucositis-free survival did not differ (p = 0.99)."},{"label":"CONCLUSION","text":"In this large matched cohort, preventive PBM reduced morphine consumption during and after RT, supporting its use to alleviate RIOM-related pain in head and neck cancer patients."}],"keywords":["head and neck cancer","morphine consumption","oral mucositis","photobiomodulation","radiotherapy"]},{"pmid":"41776117","title_fr":"Prise en charge de la mucite en greffe de cellules souches en Australie et Nouvelle-Zélande (enquête CAN EAT)","type":"survey","level":0,"pop":"mixte","ctx":["CSH"],"aim":"prévention","mode":[],"nm":[],"n":24,"effect":"—","theme":"Implantation","l1":"Dans 24 centres de greffe australiens et néo-zélandais, la PBM est rarement utilisée (coût, accès) car les recommandations nationales EviQ ne l'incluent pas, contrairement à MASCC/ISOO.","l2":"Enquête auprès des infirmières spécialisées de 41 hôpitaux (taux de réponse 59 %). Soins de bouche (100 %) et cryothérapie avec melphalan (96 %) sont quasi universels ; PBM et palifermine restent marginales.\n\nLes recommandations EviQ (67 %) sont plus suivies que celles de MASCC/ISOO (17 %). Les auteurs plaident pour réévaluer la faisabilité de la PBM et harmoniser les recommandations.","l3":{"points":["Outils : échelle OMS (42 %), scores de douleur (46 %), EviQ (42 %).","Douleur : opioïdes à la demande (100 %), PCA (58 %)."],"limites":["Enquête déclarative, 24 centres."],"pratique":"Illustre l'écart entre recommandations internationales et pratique ; le coût et l'accès freinent la PBM."},"topic":"mucite-orale","title":"Oral mucositis management in hematopoietic cell transplantation in Australia and New Zealand (CAN EAT Survey).","journal":"Supportive care in cancer : official journal of the Multinational Association of Supportive Care in Cancer","journal_abbr":"Support Care Cancer","authors":["Munro E","Gavin NC","Nakagaki M"],"date":"2026-03-03","year":2026,"week":"2026-S10","doi":"10.1007/s00520-026-10423-5","pmcid":"PMC12956983","license":"cc by","oa":true,"pdf":"pdfs/41776117.pdf","cited_by":0,"pub_types":[],"abstract":[{"label":"PURPOSE","text":"The Multinational Association of Supportive Care in Cancer and the International Society of Oral Oncology (MASCC/ISOO) has published evidence-based international guidelines for the management of oral mucositis (OM). However, adherence to these guidelines has not been evaluated. This survey assessed OM management in hematopoietic cell transplantation (HCT) centers in Australia and New Zealand."},{"label":"METHODS","text":"Specialist nurses in 41 transplant hospitals were invited to complete a survey. Questions covered general unit information, oral assessment (tools, assessors, frequency), prevention strategies (guidelines, oral care, cryotherapy, photobiomodulation, palifermin, and barriers to use), and treatment strategies (pain management and nutritional support)."},{"label":"RESULTS","text":"Of 41 hospitals, 24 (59%) responded. All but one responder were specialized nurses. Oral assessment was primarily conducted by nurses (100%) and doctors (80%). The most commonly used tools were the World Health Organization grading scale (42%), pain scores (46%), and EviQ (Australian online cancer resource) (42%). EviQ guidelines (67%), which exclude palifermin and photobiomodulation, followed by institutional guidelines (29%) and MASCC/ISOO guidelines (17%). Oral care (100%) and cryotherapy with melphalan (96%) were widely adopted, while photobiomodulation and palifermin were rarely used due to cost and access barriers. For OM-related pain, as-needed opioids (100%), patient-controlled analgesia (58%), and topical analgesics (46%) were common. When OM impaired oral intake, supportive measures included supplemental drinks (88%), enteral nutrition (50%), and parenteral nutrition (67%)."},{"label":"CONCLUSION","text":"In Australia and New Zealand, national EviQ guidelines are used more often than the MASCC/ISOO guidelines. Feasibility for implementing palifermin and photobiomodulation should be reassessed, and greater collaboration in developing guidelines is necessary."}],"keywords":["Assessment","Oral mucositis","Prevention","Survey","Treatment"]},{"pmid":"41760795","title_fr":"La PBM prévient-elle la mucite chez les patients d'hématologie sous chimiothérapie ? Revue systématique d'essais randomisés","type":"sr","level":1,"pop":"mixte","ctx":["chimio"],"aim":"prévention","mode":["laser"],"nm":[660],"n":200,"effect":"favorable","theme":"Synthèse des preuves","l1":"Six essais randomisés (1997–2024, 200 patients) en hématologie : la PBM préventive, presque toujours en lumière rouge, réduit la fréquence de la mucite et la douleur.","l2":"Revue avec évaluation du risque de biais (Cochrane) et de la certitude (GRADE) sur six bases dont LILACS. Les six essais utilisent le rouge, un seul y ajoute l'infrarouge.\n\nLa PBM diminue la fréquence de la mucite et la douleur et améliore la qualité de vie. Le résumé ne chiffre ni l'effet poolé ni le niveau GRADE.","l3":{"points":["6 ECR, 200 patients, comparateurs non laser."],"limites":["Petit effectif total ; essais anciens (depuis 1997).","Niveau GRADE non précisé dans le résumé."],"pratique":"Va dans le sens des recommandations pour la chimiothérapie à haute dose en hématologie."},"topic":"mucite-orale","title":"Is photobiomodulation an effective preventive strategy for oral mucositis in patients with hematologic diseases undergoing chemotherapy?: A systematic review of randomized controlled trials.","journal":"Lasers in medical science","journal_abbr":"Lasers Med Sci","authors":["Xavier MGA","de Mendonça Silva AMS","de Oliveira GRW","et al."],"date":"2026-02-27","year":2026,"week":"2026-S09","doi":"10.1007/s10103-026-04814-7","pmcid":"PMC12948774","license":"cc by","oa":true,"pdf":"pdfs/41760795.pdf","cited_by":0,"pub_types":["Systematic Review"],"abstract":[{"label":"","text":"Purpose to conduct a systematic review to assess whether photobiomodulation is effective in preventing oral mucositis in patients with hematologic diseases undergoing chemotherapy. Methods: A systematic search was conducted in the Embase, Medline/PubMed, Cochrane, Scopus, Web of Science and LILACS databases. The strategy included selected randomized clinical trials that evaluated the prevention of oral mucositis, directly comparing the use of photobiomodulation for chemotherapy-induced oral mucositis in patients with hematologic diseases with alternative non-laser therapies. The risk of bias in the studies was assessed using the Cochrane Collaboration’s tool for assessing risk of bias and the certainty of evidence was assessed using GRADE. Results: After applying all predetermined criteria, six randomized clinical trials, dated between 1997 and 2024, were included. A total of 200 patients were evaluated, with all six studies using the red wavelength, with only one study using a combination with the infrared wavelength. It was observed that photobiomodulation can be effective in preventing oral mucositis, reducing its frequency. Furthermore, a reduction in pain intensity and an improvement in quality of life were also identified. Conclusion: The use of photobiomodulation shows promising results in preventing oral mucositis, consequently reducing the frequency and pain of patients with hematologic diseases undergoing chemotherapy."}],"keywords":["Chemotherapy","Hematologic diseases","Oral mucositis","Photobiomodulation"]},{"pmid":"41715303","title_fr":"Revue critique (scoping) de la PBM extrabuccale pour la mucite en oncologie","type":"sr","level":3,"pop":"mixte","ctx":["mixte"],"aim":"les deux","mode":["extrabuccal"],"nm":[],"n":null,"effect":"mitigé","theme":"Extra vs intrabuccal","l1":"Neuf études sur la PBM extrabuccale exclusive, dont un seul essai la comparant à l'intrabuccale (sans différence) : prometteuse et rapide, mais preuves encore limitées.","l2":"Revue exploratoire classant les études selon l'Oxford CEBM : 3 de niveau 2, 6 de niveau 4. Le seul essai randomisé extra contre intrabuccal ne trouvait pas de différence ; face au placebo ou à l'absence de traitement, les résultats étaient mitigés.\n\nRecherche antérieure aux essais publiés fin 2025 et en 2026 qui renforcent l'extrabuccal, notamment en pédiatrie.","l3":{"points":["9 études ; OCEBM : 3 niveau 2, 6 niveau 4.","Avantage : séances plus courtes, accès plus facile."],"limites":["Peu d'études, designs hétérogènes."],"pratique":"À relire avec les essais de 2025–2026 (Brésil, pédiatrie, ORL) qui ont depuis enrichi le dossier."},"topic":"mucite-orale","title":"A Critical Scoping Review of Extraoral Photobiomodulation for Oral Mucositis in Oncological Patients.","journal":"Special care in dentistry : official publication of the American Association of Hospital Dentists, the Academy of Dentistry for the Handicapped, and the American Society for Geriatric Dentistry","journal_abbr":"Spec Care Dentist","authors":["Monteiro JLGC","Martins MD","Treister NS","et al."],"date":"2026-02-20","year":2026,"week":"2026-S08","doi":"10.1111/scd.70145","pmcid":null,"license":null,"oa":false,"pdf":null,"cited_by":0,"pub_types":["Scoping Review"],"abstract":[{"label":"AIMS","text":"Oral mucositis (OM) is a potentially severe side effect of oncological treatment. This study aimed to conduct a comprehensive scoping review of extraoral photobiomodulation (EOPBM) in the treatment or prevention of OM in oncologic patients. Studies were categorized according to the Oxford Centre for Evidence-Based Medicine (OCEBM) recommendations."},{"label":"METHODS","text":"A search was conducted in three databases for comparative or non-comparative studies. There were no language or publication year restrictions."},{"label":"RESULTS","text":"The review included nine studies, with varying levels of evidence. Three studies were categorized as OCEBM level 2, and six as level 4. While EOPBM offered advantages, such as shorter session durations and potentially more accessible light application, the review found limited evidence for its exclusive use in preventing or treating OM. It included a single randomized controlled trial (RCT) comparing EOPBM with IOPBM, which found no significant differences between the groups in OM development or severity. Additionally, studies comparing EOPBM with sham sessions, placebo, or no treatment yielded mixed results."},{"label":"CONCLUSIONS","text":"EOPBM shows promise in preventing OM in oncological patients, with some advantages such as shorter session durations. However, the current level of evidence for exclusive EOPBM protocols remains limited."}],"keywords":["chemotherapy","low‐level light therapy","oral mucositis","radiotherapy"]},{"pmid":"41647299","title_fr":"Interventions contre la mucite et qualité de vie des patients atteints de cancer : revue parapluie","type":"umbrella","level":1,"pop":"mixte","ctx":["mixte"],"aim":"les deux","mode":[],"nm":[],"n":null,"effect":"favorable","theme":"Qualité de vie","l1":"Huit revues systématiques (257 études) : PBM, miel, mûre noire et SAMITAL améliorent de façon constante la qualité de vie liée à la mucite.","l2":"Revue parapluie (PRISMA 2020, JBI, AMSTAR 2, PROSPERO) limitée aux revues mesurant la qualité de vie avec des outils validés (EORTC QLQ-C30, FACT-HN, UW-QOL).\n\nLa PBM figure parmi les interventions qui améliorent douleur, fonction buccale et bien-être émotionnel. La gabapentine réduit les opioïdes avec des résultats variables. L'hétérogénéité des instruments limite les comparaisons.","l3":{"points":["8 revues, 257 études primaires ; recherche jusqu'en mars 2024."],"limites":["Instruments de qualité de vie très variés."],"pratique":"Inclure systématiquement des critères de qualité de vie dans le suivi des patients traités par PBM."},"topic":"mucite-orale","title":"Therapeutic Interventions to Manage Oral Mucositis and Their Impact on Quality of Life in Cancer Patients: An Umbrella Review.","journal":"Pain research & management","journal_abbr":"Pain Res Manag","authors":["de Sousa Gomes Costa JL","Díaz L","Chaple Gil A","et al."],"date":"2026-02-03","year":2026,"week":"2026-S06","doi":"10.1155/prm/3601001","pmcid":"PMC12868910","license":"cc by","oa":true,"pdf":"pdfs/41647299.pdf","cited_by":0,"pub_types":["Systematic Review"],"abstract":[{"label":"BACKGROUND","text":"Oral mucositis (OM) is a debilitating complication of cancer therapy, particularly in head and neck cancer patients, with significant adverse effects on quality of life (QoL). Although numerous interventions have been investigated, their impact on QoL remains inconsistently reported and poorly synthesized."},{"label":"METHODS","text":"An umbrella review was conducted following PRISMA 2020 and Joanna Briggs Institute (JBI) guidelines. Five databases (PubMed, Embase, Scopus, Web of Science, and Cochrane Library) were searched from inception to March 2024. Reviews were eligible if they evaluated any therapeutic intervention for OM and reported QoL-related outcomes using validated tools. Methodological quality was appraised using AMSTAR 2, and findings were narratively synthesized and thematically categorized. The protocol was registered in PROSPERO (CRD420251044088)."},{"label":"RESULTS","text":"Eight systematic reviews (257 primary studies) met the inclusion criteria. Photobiomodulation, honey, black mulberry, and botanical agents such as SAMITAL showed consistent improvement in QoL domains, including pain relief, oral function, and emotional well-being. Gabapentin demonstrated potential in reducing opioid use and early mucositis-related pain, though findings were mixed across trials. QoL was most commonly assessed using the EORTC QLQ-C30, FACT-HN, and UW-QOL. Overall, heterogeneity in intervention protocols and QoL instruments limited comparability."},{"label":"CONCLUSIONS","text":"This review highlights the most promising interventions for improving QoL in patients with OM. Standardizing QoL measurement and prioritizing patient-reported outcomes in future trials is essential to inform evidence-based supportive oncology care."}],"keywords":["cancer treatment","gabapentin","oral mucositis","patient-reported outcomes","photobiomodulation","quality of life","supportive care","umbrella review"]},{"pmid":"41622096","title_fr":"Mucite et xérostomie radio-induites en ORL : revue des essais de phase III–IV","type":"review","level":5,"pop":"adulte","ctx":["ORL"],"aim":"les deux","mode":[],"nm":[],"n":null,"effect":"favorable","theme":"Revue générale","l1":"Revue des essais de phase III et IV terminés sur ClinicalTrials.gov : pour la mucite, le laser basse énergie fait partie des options non médicamenteuses utiles, aux côtés du miel et de l'aloe vera.","l2":"Côté médicaments, palifermine, avasopasem, doxépine, bain de bouche à la morphine et gabapentine ressortent pour la mucite ; pilocarpine, amifostine et céviméline pour la xérostomie.\n\nL'hétérogénéité des plans d'étude limite les conclusions.","l3":{"points":["Source : essais enregistrés et terminés (phase III–IV)."],"limites":["Revue non systématique au sens PRISMA."],"pratique":"Situe la PBM dans l'arsenal global."},"topic":"mucite-orale","title":"Clinical evidence for managing radiotherapy-induced oral mucositis and xerostomia in head and neck cancer patients: a review of Phase III-IV trials.","journal":"International journal of oral and maxillofacial surgery","journal_abbr":"Int J Oral Maxillofac Surg","authors":["Alsahafi E"],"date":"2026-01-31","year":2026,"week":"2026-S05","doi":"10.1016/j.ijom.2026.01.020","pmcid":null,"license":null,"oa":false,"pdf":null,"cited_by":1,"pub_types":["Review"],"abstract":[{"label":"","text":"Oral Mucositis and xerostomia are common debilitating side effects of radiotherapy in head and neck cancer patients, leading to treatment interruptions and negatively impacting quality of life. This review was performed to examine the efficacy and safety of interventions for managing these complications, evaluated in completed Phase III and IV clinical trials registered on ClinicalTrials.gov. A range of pharmacological and non-pharmacological therapies were reviewed, with analysis of intervention types, outcomes, and sample sizes. For oral mucositis, effective pharmacological agents included palifermin, avasopasem manganese, doxepin, morphine mouthwash, and gabapentin, while non-pharmacological options such as low-level laser therapy, honey, and Aloe vera showed additional benefit in reducing severity and improving healing. In managing radiotherapy-induced xerostomia, pilocarpine, amifostine, and cevimeline significantly enhanced salivary function, with adaptive radiotherapy techniques offering protective effects on the salivary glands. Phase IV studies supported the role of benzydamine, MuGard, Biotène, and herbal mouthwashes in symptom relief and patient comfort. Although several interventions demonstrated promising clinical efficacy, the heterogeneity in study design, outcome measures, and follow-up duration limits definitive conclusions. Therefore, customized, evidence-driven approaches informed by existing recommendations and developed data are crucial for enhancing outcomes and reducing the burden of therapy-induced toxicities in head and neck cancer patients."}],"keywords":["Clinical trial","Head and neck neoplasms","Oral mucositis","Radiotherapy","Xerostomia"]},{"pmid":"41584371","title_fr":"Interventions contre la mucite chimio- et radio-induite : revue systématique et méta-analyse","type":"ma","level":1,"pop":"mixte","ctx":["mixte"],"aim":"les deux","mode":[],"nm":[],"n":null,"effect":"favorable","theme":"Synthèse des preuves","l1":"27 articles (2019–2024) : cryothérapie et PBM, ainsi que des agents naturels (miel, curcuma, thé vert), sont efficaces pour prévenir et traiter la mucite.","l2":"Méta-analyse croate couvrant interventions médicamenteuses et non médicamenteuses. Le résumé ne donne pas d'estimation chiffrée par intervention.\n\nContribution modeste par rapport aux méta-analyses spécifiques de la PBM.","l3":{"points":["27 articles, 2019–2024."],"limites":["Résultats non chiffrés dans le résumé ; designs inclus non précisés."],"pratique":"Pas d'apport spécifique sur la PBM."},"topic":"mucite-orale","title":"Interventions to Chemotherapy and Radiotherapy Induced Oral Mucositis: a Systematic Review and Meta-analysis.","journal":"Acta stomatologica Croatica","journal_abbr":"Acta Stomatol Croat","authors":["Pereira CP","Marques I","Martins JP"],"date":"2026-01-26","year":2026,"week":"2026-S05","doi":"10.15644/asc59/4/6","pmcid":"PMC12826651","license":"cc by-nc-nd","oa":true,"pdf":"pdfs/41584371.pdf","cited_by":0,"pub_types":[],"abstract":[{"label":"INTRODUCTION","text":"Oral mucositis is a common adverse effect of chemotherapy and radiotherapy, characterized by erythematous lesions, edema, ulceration, atrophy, or even hemorrhage. Current evidence highlights the complexity of its management and the importance of tailoring therapeutic approaches to the individual patient profile in order to improve quality of life during cancer treatment."},{"label":"OBJECTIVES","text":"To access the efficacy of pharmacological and non-pharmacological interventions for the prevention and treatment of chemotherapy- and/or radiotherapy-induced oral mucositis."},{"label":"MATERIALS AND METHODS","text":"A systematic review with meta-analysis of 27 articles published between 2019 and 2024 was also performed, aiming to characterize and contextualize therapeutic interventions for oral mucositis."},{"label":"RESULTS AND DISCUSSION","text":"This systematic review and meta-analysis suggests that various non-pharmacological interventions (such as cryotherapy and photobiomodulation) and pharmacological interventions, particularly natural agents (such as honey, curcumin, green tea), are effective for the prevention and treatment of oral mucositis."},{"label":"CONCLUSION","text":"Oral mucositis is a highly prevalent adverse effect in patients undergoing chemotherapy and/or radiotherapy, with significant functional, nutritional, and treatment adherence implications. The systematic review with meta-analysis demonstrated the efficacy of non-pharmacological and natural interventions."}],"keywords":["Chemotherapy","Curcuma","Drug Therapy","Herbal Teas","Honey","MeSH Terms: Stomatitis","Non-pharmacological interventions","Oral mucositis","Pharmacologic Actions","Pharmacological treatment"]},{"pmid":"41410110","title_fr":"Laser et mucite : revue parapluie, recommandations officielles de la Société polonaise de dentisterie laser","type":"umbrella","level":1,"pop":"mixte","ctx":["mixte"],"aim":"les deux","mode":["intrabuccal","extrabuccal","PDT"],"nm":[630,670,780,850],"n":null,"effect":"favorable","theme":"Recommandations","key":true,"l1":"Synthèse de 22 revues servant de recommandations officielles en Pologne : la PBM préventive réduit de 40 à 80 % la mucite sévère et la PBM curative raccourcit les ulcères de 4 à 7 jours.","l2":"Revue parapluie (PRISMA 2020, JBI) des revues et méta-analyses publiées jusqu'en juillet 2025. La PBM diminue l'incidence, la sévérité, la douleur et la durée de cicatrisation, chez l'adulte comme chez l'enfant. L'association avec la thérapie photodynamique améliore encore la cicatrisation.\n\nParamètres retenus comme optimaux : 630–670 nm en intrabuccal, 780–850 nm en extrabuccal, 2 à 6 J/cm². Aucun effet indésirable grave. La société savante fait de la PBM un soin de support standard, en particulier en greffe de cellules souches et en chimioradiothérapie ORL.","l3":{"points":["22 revues incluses (PubMed, Embase, Scopus, Cochrane).","Prévention : −40 à −80 % de mucites de grade 3–4 ; traitement : ulcères raccourcis de 4 à 7 jours.","Fenêtre dosimétrique : 2–6 J/cm²."],"limites":["Qualité des revues incluses non détaillée dans le résumé.","Recommandation nationale de société savante, pas d'analyse GRADE indiquée."],"pratique":"Donne une fenêtre de paramètres concrète, utile pour écrire un protocole de service."},"topic":"mucite-orale","title":"Laser therapy for the management of oral mucositis: An umbrella review - official recommendations of the Polish Society for Laser Dentistry.","journal":"Dental and medical problems","journal_abbr":"Dent Med Probl","authors":["Grzech-Leśniak K","Fiegler-Rudol J","Grzech-Leśniak Z","et al."],"date":"2025-12-18","year":2025,"week":"2025-S51","doi":"10.17219/dmp/213958","pmcid":null,"license":"cc by","oa":false,"pdf":null,"cited_by":3,"pub_types":["Systematic Review"],"abstract":[{"label":"","text":"Oral mucositis (OM) is a common and debilitating side effect of cancer therapy that impairs nutrition, increases infection risk, and often disrupts oncologic treatment. Photobiomodulation therapy (PBMT) has emerged as an effective, non-invasive method for OM prevention and management.This umbrella review was conducted in accordance with the Preferred Reporting Items for Systematic reviews and Meta-Analyses (PRISMA) 2020 guidelines and the Joanna Briggs Institute (JBI) methodology. A comprehensive search of PubMed®/MEDLINE, Embase, Scopus, and Cochrane Library identified systematic reviews and meta-analyses on laser therapy for OM published through July 2025. The data extraction process centered on clinical outcomes, laser parameters and safety.Twenty-two reviews met the inclusion criteria. Photobiomodulation therapy significantly reduced the incidence of OM, disease severity, pain, and healing time across adult and pediatric populations. Preventive PBMT decreased the risk of severe OM (grade 3-4) by 40-80%, while therapeutic PBMT shortened ulcer duration by 4-7 days. The combination of PBMT and photodynamic therapy (PDT) enhanced mucosal healing and alleviated pain. Optimal outcomes were achieved when wavelengths of 630-670 nm (intraoral) and 780-850 nm (extraoral) were used, with fluences of 2-6 J/cm2. No serious adverse events were reported.Photobiomodulation therapy demonstrates strong efficacy and safety in the management of OM, improving quality of life and treatment continuity in oncology patients. The Polish Society for Laser Dentistry (PTSL) endorses PBMT as a standard supportive care modality, particularly in the context of hematopoietic stem cell transplantation (HSCT) and head and neck chemoradiation. Protocol adherence and parameter standardization are essential to ensure the reproducibility and clinical effectiveness of research findings."}],"keywords":["cancer therapy","head and neck cancer","hematopoietic stem cell transplantation","low-level laser therapy","photobiomodulation"]},{"pmid":"41394293","title_fr":"Gel mucoadhésif d'Arrabidaea chica contre laser basse énergie pour cicatriser la mucite en greffe de moelle : essai randomisé","type":"rct","level":2,"pop":"adulte","ctx":["CSH"],"aim":"traitement","mode":["laser"],"nm":[],"n":44,"effect":"défavorable","theme":"Comparateurs actifs","metric":{"kind":"values","outcome":"Délai de cicatrisation (jours)","a_label":"Gel A. chica","a":5.2,"b_label":"Laser (témoin)","b":11.2,"unit":"j"},"l1":"Chez 44 greffés, un gel à base d'Arrabidaea chica a fait cicatriser la mucite deux fois plus vite que le laser basse énergie (5,2 contre 11,2 jours).","l2":"Essai brésilien (UNICAMP, 2022–2024) ouvert, en groupes parallèles. Le laser servait ici de comparateur de référence. 38 patients ont terminé l'étude.\n\nLe gel a raccourci nettement le délai de guérison, avec parfois une légère sensation de brûlure. C'est un des rares essais où la PBM fait moins bien qu'un comparateur actif, mais il est ouvert et petit.","l3":{"points":["Randomisation 22/22 ; 20 et 18 patients analysés.","Délai de cicatrisation : 5,2 ± 1,5 contre 11,2 ± 6,5 jours."],"limites":["Essai ouvert ; paramètres laser non détaillés dans le résumé.","Forte dispersion dans le groupe laser."],"pratique":"Signal à confirmer ; ne remet pas en cause la PBM préventive, mais questionne sa place en curatif face à certains topiques."},"topic":"mucite-orale","title":"Healing Oral Mucositis With Arrabidaea chica Verlot Mucoadhesive Gel in Patients Undergoing Hematopoietic Stem Cell Transplantation: A Randomized Controlled Clinical Trial.","journal":"BioMed research international","journal_abbr":"Biomed Res Int","authors":["de Cássia Almeida Queiroz N","de Oliveira Sousa IM","Júnior RJM","et al."],"date":"2025-12-12","year":2025,"week":"2025-S50","doi":"10.1155/bmri/3700170","pmcid":"PMC12699320","license":"cc by","oa":true,"pdf":"pdfs/41394293.pdf","cited_by":0,"pub_types":["Randomized Controlled Trial"],"abstract":[{"label":"INTRODUCTION","text":"Oral mucositis affects up to 80% of patients undergoing bone marrow transplantation, causing painful ulcers, increased infection risk, and impaired quality of life. Current treatments are mainly palliative, highlighting the need for new therapeutic options. Arrabidaea chica Verlot extract has shown wound healing potential through antioxidant activity and collagen synthesis promotion in vitro and significant wound area reduction in vivo."},{"label":"OBJECTIVE","text":"The objective of the study is to evaluate the wound healing potential of a mucoadhesive gel containing 2.5% A. chica extract for treating oral mucositis in oncohematological patients undergoing bone marrow transplantation."},{"label":"METHODS","text":"This was a randomized, controlled, open-label, parallel-group clinical trial conducted at HC/UNICAMP from October 2022 to November 2024. Forty-four patients were randomized to the intervention group (mucoadhesive A. chica gel, n = 22) or control group (low-intensity laser therapy, n = 22). The primary outcome was the time to healing of oral mucositis. Statistical analysis compared mean healing times between groups."},{"label":"RESULTS","text":"Twenty participants in the intervention group and 18 in the control group completed the study. Mean healing time was 5.2 ± 1.5 days for the intervention group and 11.2 ± 6.5 days for the control group, representing a 2.2-fold faster healing with A. chica gel. Some intervention participants reported a mild burning sensation, without affecting treatment adherence or efficacy."},{"label":"CONCLUSION","text":"The mucoadhesive gel containing A. chica extract proved to be a safe and effective therapeutic option for treating oral mucositis in patients undergoing bone marrow transplantation."},{"label":"TRIAL REGISTRATION","text":"ReBEC number: RBR-5×4397."}],"keywords":["Arrabidaea chica","Fridericia chica","healing","oral mucositis"]},{"pmid":"41657588","title_fr":"La PBM en odontologie : mécanismes, applications cliniques et recommandations","type":"review","level":5,"pop":"mixte","ctx":["mixte"],"aim":"les deux","mode":[],"nm":[],"n":null,"effect":"favorable","theme":"Revue générale","l1":"Courte revue générale : la PBM agit via les chromophores mitochondriaux et l'inflammation ; la mucite fait partie des indications soutenues par les preuves.","l2":"Revue brève des mécanismes (stimulation mitochondriale, régulation des cytokines, facteurs de croissance) et des indications dentaires : mucite, troubles temporo-mandibulaires, parodontite, douleur endodontique, implants, orthodontie.\n\nSans données nouvelles.","l3":{"points":["Revue pédagogique."],"limites":["Résumé très court, méthode non décrite."],"pratique":"Aucune conséquence pratique."},"topic":"mucite-orale","title":"Photobiomodulation in dentistry: Mechanisms, clinical applications and evidence-based guidelines.","journal":"Bioinformation","journal_abbr":"Bioinformation","authors":["Eltahir L","Elgendy M","Ali G","et al."],"date":"2025-11-15","year":2025,"week":"2025-S46","doi":"10.6026/973206300214203","pmcid":"PMC12880127","license":"cc by","oa":true,"pdf":"pdfs/41657588.pdf","cited_by":3,"pub_types":[],"abstract":[{"label":"","text":"Photobiomodulation (PBM), formerly known as low-level laser therapy (LLLT), utilizes red and near-infrared light to activate mitochondrial chromophores, thereby enhancing cellular activity, modulating inflammation and promoting tissue repair. Therefore, it is of interest to the mechanistic pathways of PBM, including mitochondrial stimulation, cytokine regulation and growth factor induction. Clinical evidence supports its efficacy in managing oral mucositis, temporomandibular disorders, periodontitis, postoperative endodontic pain, implant stability and orthodontic treatment outcomes."}],"keywords":["PBM safety","Photobiomodulation","dental implants","endodontics","laser therapy","low-level laser therapy","oral mucositis","orthodontics","pain management","periodontitis"]},{"pmid":"41239052","title_fr":"Soins buccaux structurés incluant la PBM et mucite sous 5-fluorouracile : cohorte rétrospective","type":"cohort","level":4,"pop":"adulte","ctx":["chimio"],"aim":"prévention","mode":[],"nm":[],"n":69,"effect":"favorable","theme":"Vie réelle","l1":"Chez 69 patients sous 5-FU, un programme de soins buccaux avec deux séances de PBM par cycle limite les mucites sévères à 7,2 % ; le bénéfice est marqué sous FOLFIRINOX.","l2":"Cohorte monocentrique brésilienne (2020–2022) de tumeurs solides : bilan dentaire, hygiène, contrôle des infections et PBM les deux premiers jours de chaque cycle.\n\nTous les patients ont eu une mucite, surtout de grade 1 (40,6 %) ou 2 (52,2 %). Deux séances de PBM étaient associées à une apparition plus tardive, une sévérité et une durée moindres, avec un risque divisé par 9,1 sous FOLFIRINOX. Le FOLFOX donnait des mucites plus précoces et plus sévères.","l3":{"points":["Grade 3 : 7,2 % ; aucun grade 4 rapporté.","FOLFIRINOX : risque divisé par 9,1 avec deux séances."],"limites":["Rétrospectif, sans groupe sans PBM ; enregistrement rétrospectif."],"pratique":"Argument pour intégrer la PBM aux soins buccaux dès le début des chimiothérapies à base de 5-FU."},"topic":"mucite-orale","title":"Impact of structured oral care strategies on oral mucositis in patients receiving 5-fluorouracil: a retrospective cohort study.","journal":"Supportive care in cancer : official journal of the Multinational Association of Supportive Care in Cancer","journal_abbr":"Support Care Cancer","authors":["Eduardo FP","Ferreira MH","Gobbi MF","et al."],"date":"2025-11-14","year":2025,"week":"2025-S46","doi":"10.1007/s00520-025-10079-7","pmcid":null,"license":null,"oa":false,"pdf":null,"cited_by":2,"pub_types":[],"abstract":[{"label":"PURPOSE","text":"Oral mucositis (OM) is a frequent and debilitating complication of 5-fluorouracil (5-FU)-based chemotherapy, compromising patients' quality of life and treatment adherence. This study aimed to evaluate the impact of a structured oral care strategy (OCS), including photobiomodulation (PBM), on the severity, onset, and duration of OM in patients with solid tumors receiving 5-FU-based regimens."},{"label":"METHODS","text":"This single-center study included 69 patients treated between 2020 and 2022 and was retrospectively registered (Ethics Committee approval No. 5.089.912/2021). All participants received standardized OCS before and during chemotherapy, consisting of dental examination, oral hygiene instructions, infection control measures, and PBM. PBM was delivered once daily during the first two days of each 5-FU chemotherapy cycle. Associations were analyzed using Chi-square tests and odds ratios."},{"label":"RESULTS","text":"All patients developed OM, predominantly grade 1 (40.6%) or grade 2 (52.2%), while only 7.2% experienced grade 3 lesions. Two PBM sessions were significantly associated with reduced onset, severity, and duration of OM, particularly among patients receiving FOLFIRINOX, for whom the risk of OM decreased 9.1-fold, and among neutropenic patients (p = 0.042). Conversely, the FOLFOX regimen was associated with earlier onset (p = 0.004) and greater severity (p = 0.026); however, two PBM sessions remained beneficial, reducing OM risk 1.35-fold."},{"label":"CONCLUSION","text":"Structured OCS incorporating PBM appears to be an effective, non-invasive approach to mitigate OM severity, onset, and duration in patients undergoing 5-FU-based chemotherapy. These findings support integrating dental care and PBM into routine oncology protocols. Prospective multicenter studies are warranted to validate and standardize this strategy."}],"keywords":["5-fluorouracil","Chemotherapy","Dental care","Oral mucositis","Photobiomodulation"]},{"pmid":"41201561","title_fr":"Du cabinet au canapé : étude pilote d'une PBM à domicile contre la mucite et la radiodermite","type":"cases","level":4,"pop":"adulte","ctx":["ORL"],"aim":"prévention","mode":["domicile","extrabuccal"],"nm":[],"n":20,"effect":"favorable","theme":"PBM à domicile","key":true,"l1":"Une PBM proche infrarouge auto-administrée à domicile est faisable chez les patients ORL irradiés : 60 % des séances prévues réalisées, aucun effet indésirable, 41 % de mucites sévères en fin de radiothérapie.","l2":"Essai monobras (20 inclus, 17 analysés) : deux séances par jour à domicile pendant 9 à 11 semaines, du début de la radiothérapie à 4 semaines après. Le critère principal était l'observance (au moins 50 % des séances).\n\nL'objectif est atteint (67 % des patients). Mucite sévère : 6 % à la semaine 3, 41 % en fin de radiothérapie, 0 % un mois après. Radiodermite sévère : 18 % en fin de traitement. Les patients jugent l'appareil confortable et simple.","l3":{"points":["Enregistrement NCT05176834.","Observance : 60 % des séances attendues réalisées.","Mucite sévère : 41 % en fin de RT ; radiodermite sévère : 18 %."],"limites":["Monobras, sans témoin ; petit effectif.","Le taux de mucite sévère reste élevé : efficacité non démontrée."],"pratique":"Le domicile est faisable ; à réserver pour l'instant à la recherche ou en complément d'une PBM en service."},"topic":"mucite-orale","title":"From clinic to couch: a pilot study of home-use photobiomodulation for radiation-induced oral mucositis and dermatitis.","journal":"Supportive care in cancer : official journal of the Multinational Association of Supportive Care in Cancer","journal_abbr":"Support Care Cancer","authors":["Salman S","Abdalla-Aslan R","Awawdi A","et al."],"date":"2025-11-07","year":2025,"week":"2025-S45","doi":"10.1007/s00520-025-10037-3","pmcid":"PMC12594705","license":"cc by","oa":true,"pdf":"pdfs/41201561.pdf","cited_by":0,"pub_types":["Clinical Trial"],"abstract":[{"label":"PURPOSE","text":"Radiation-induced oral mucositis (OM) and dermatitis (RD) are common debilitating toxicities of radiotherapy (RT) for head and neck cancer (HNC) patients. While photobiomodulation (PBM) has shown promise in reducing severity of these adverse effects, in-clinic treatments pose logistic and financial challenges. This study assessed the feasibility and compliance of a home-use, self-applied near-infrared PBM device for the prevention and treatment of RT-induced OM and RD."},{"label":"METHODS","text":"This prospective, single-arm trial enrolled 20 HNC patients scheduled for intensity-modulated RT (IMRT) with or without concurrent chemotherapy. Seventeen patients were analyzed following exclusions. Participants self-administered PBM at home twice daily for 9-11 weeks, from RT initiation to four weeks post-RT. The primary outcome was compliance (≥ 50% of expected treatments). Secondary outcomes included incidence of severe (grade 3-4) OM and RD."},{"label":"RESULTS","text":"67% of the participants completed at least 50% of the prescribed PBM treatments. Overall, 60% of expected sessions were administered, surpassing the predefined compliance threshold. By week 3, 6% presented with severe OM and none with severe RD. At RT completion, 41% and 18% had severe OM and RD, respectively. Four weeks post-RT, no severe OM or RD were observed, with the majority presenting with no toxicity (grade 0). No device related adverse effects were reported, and patients reported high comfort and ease of use."},{"label":"CONCLUSION","text":"Self-administered, home-use PBM is a feasible and well-tolerated approach that shows promise as pre-emptive treatment for RT induced OM and RD. Large-scale, randomized, placebo-controlled trials are needed to confirm these findings and evaluate long-term benefits."},{"label":"TRIAL REGISTRATION","text":"ClinicalTrials.gov Identifier: NCT05176834. Registration Date: December 13th, 2021."}],"keywords":["Head and neck neoplasms","Intensity-modulated radiotherapy","Low-level laser therapy","Oral mucositis","Photobiomodulation therapy","Radiation-induced dermatitis"]},{"pmid":"41133718","title_fr":"Açaï clarifié et PBM sur les glandes parotides de rats sous chimiothérapie","type":"preclinical","level":5,"pop":"—","ctx":["chimio"],"aim":"prévention","mode":["laser"],"nm":[],"n":54,"effect":"favorable","theme":"Préclinique","l1":"Chez 54 rats avec mucite induite par 5-FU, PBM et açaï protègent les parotides sur le plan biochimique (stress oxydatif) et structurel.","l2":"Cinq groupes : témoin, mucite non traitée, PBM, açaï, PBM + açaï. Analyses à J8 et J10 : capacité antioxydante, peroxydation lipidique, métabolites du NO, histologie.\n\nLes groupes traités gardent une architecture glandulaire proche des animaux sains. Première étape d'un travail poursuivi en 2026 sur la glande submandibulaire.","l3":{"points":["5-FU 60 puis 40 mg/kg, scarification jugale à J3–J4."],"limites":["Modèle animal."],"pratique":"Données mécanistiques uniquement."},"topic":"mucite-orale","title":"Effects of Clarified Açaí Supplementation and Photobiomodulation on the Parotid Glands of Rats Submitted to Chemotherapy.","journal":"Journal of oral pathology & medicine : official publication of the International Association of Oral Pathologists and the American Academy of Oral Pathology","journal_abbr":"J Oral Pathol Med","authors":["Melo WWP","Aragão WAB","Ferreira MKM","et al."],"date":"2025-10-24","year":2025,"week":"2025-S43","doi":"10.1111/jop.70072","pmcid":"PMC12774578","license":"cc by","oa":true,"pdf":"pdfs/41133718.pdf","cited_by":3,"pub_types":[],"abstract":[{"label":"BACKGROUND","text":"The antineoplastic drugs used in anticancer treatment regimens can induce changes in normal tissues, including salivary glands. This study evaluated the effects of clarified açaí and photobiomodulation (PBM) on the oxidative biochemistry and microstructure of the parotid glands of rats with oral mucositis."},{"label":"METHODS","text":"Male rats (n = 54) were divided into five groups: Negative control (no mucositis); Positive control (mucositis without treatment); PBM; Clarified açaí; and PBM + clarified açaí. Oral mucositis was induced by intraperitoneal injection of 5-fluorouracil on days 0 (60 mg/kg) and 2 (40 mg/kg). On days 3 and 4, bilateral scarification of the buccal mucosa was performed. On days 0 (negative controls), 8 and 10 (other groups), parotid glands were collected. To evaluate oxidative biochemistry, the following analyses were performed: Antioxidant capacity test against peroxyl radicals (ACAP), Lipid Peroxidation Assay (LPO), and Nitric oxide metabolite (NOx). Additionally, histopathological, histomorphometric, and histochemical analyses were performed. Statistical analysis was performed using two-way ANOVA and Tukey's post-test (p < 0.05)."},{"label":"RESULTS","text":"Clarified açaí, alone or associated with photobiomodulation, increased antioxidant levels compared to the positive control on days 8 and 10. Regarding lipid peroxidation and nitric oxide metabolite, the positive control showed higher levels than the other groups. The morphological assessment showed that the clarified açaí and photobiomodulation groups maintained similar structures of the parenchyma, stroma, and acini to the negative control."},{"label":"CONCLUSIONS","text":"The study results demonstrated that clarified açaí and photobiomodulation conferred biochemical and structural protection to the parotid glands against chemotherapy-induced damage."}],"keywords":["Euterpe oleracea Mart.","antineoplastic drugs","parotid salivary gland","photobiomodulation"]},{"pmid":"41114890","title_fr":"Prévenir douleur et mucite sous chimiothérapie avec une source lumineuse à haute énergie : essai randomisé","type":"rct","level":2,"pop":"adulte","ctx":["chimio"],"aim":"prévention","mode":["LED","laser"],"nm":[],"n":60,"effect":"neutre","theme":"Comparateurs actifs","l1":"Chez 60 patients sous chimiothérapie, un appareil lumineux à haute énergie (Germinator) prévient la mucite et la douleur aussi bien que le laser rouge classique.","l2":"Essai brésilien en deux groupes de 30 : protocole laser rouge basse intensité contre dispositif Germinator. Douleur (EVA) et mucite (OMS) mesurées avant et après.\n\nScores médians nuls dans les deux groupes, sans différence. Faute de groupe sans PBM et avec des taux de mucite très bas, l'essai ne permet pas de juger l'efficacité de l'un ou l'autre.","l3":{"points":["Douleur médiane 0 (EIQ 0–2) dans les deux groupes ; mucite médiane 0 (0–1)."],"limites":["Pas de bras témoin ; puissance insuffisante pour une équivalence.","Chimiothérapies hétérogènes, peu mucitogènes."],"pratique":"Pas d'argument pour changer de matériel."},"topic":"mucite-orale","title":"Prevention of pain and oral mucositis in patients undergoing chemotherapy through the use of a high-energy light source: A randomized clinical study.","journal":"Lasers in medical science","journal_abbr":"Lasers Med Sci","authors":["Souza MA","De Carli JP","De Conto AB","et al."],"date":"2025-10-20","year":2025,"week":"2025-S43","doi":"10.1007/s10103-025-04711-5","pmcid":null,"license":null,"oa":false,"pdf":null,"cited_by":0,"pub_types":["Randomized Controlled Trial"],"abstract":[{"label":"","text":"Purpose This study aimed to clinically evaluate the high-energy light-emitting device (Germinator) in preventing pain and oral mucositis in patients undergoing chemotherapy. Methods Sixty patients were selected, being randomly divided into two groups(n = 30), according to preventive protocol: G1(Lasertherapy)-conventional low-intensity red laser protocol; G2(Germinator)-protocol with high-energy light-emitting device. Before and after these protocols, the pain and oral mucositis degrees were recorded by visual analog scale(VAS) for pain and World Health Organization(WHO) classification for oral mucositis. The VAS was used to assess pain symptoms, where 0 represented the absence of pain and 10 the highest pain score. The oral mucositis scoring was performed according to the WHO classification: grade 0(none), grade I(oral soreness, erythema), grade II(oral erythema, ulcers, solid and liquid diet tolerated), grade III(oral ulcers, liquid diet only), and grade IV(oral alimentation impossible). Statistical analysis was performed by Mann-Whitney U test(α = 5%), in order to provide the intergroup comparison. Results The median (interquartile range-IQR) of pain score was 0.0(0.0-2.0) for G1 group(Lasertherapy), whereas it was 0.0(0.0-2.0) for G2 group(Germinator), revealing no statistically significant differences (p > 0.05). The median(minimum and maximum value) of the oral mucositis score was 0.0(0.0-1.0) for G1 group(Lasertherapy), whereas it was 0.0(0.0-1.0) for G2 group(Germinator), also revealing no statistically significant differences (p > 0.05). Both treatments proved equally effective in preventing and controlling these symptoms in patients undergoing chemotherapy, recognizing that no statistically significant difference was found between the two preventive modalities. Conclusions The high-energy light-emitting device(Germinator) appears promising in preventing pain and oral mucositis in patients undergoing chemotherapy."}],"keywords":["Cancer","Chemotherapy","Germinator","High-energy light","Oral mucositis","Pain"]},{"pmid":"41087749","title_fr":"PBM prophylactique contre la mucite en ORL : étude observationnelle multicentrique","type":"cohort","level":4,"pop":"adulte","ctx":["ORL"],"aim":"prévention","mode":[],"nm":[],"n":118,"effect":"favorable","theme":"Facteurs de risque","l1":"Sous PBM quotidienne, 118 patients ORL irradiés ont eu 28 % de mucites sévères ; HPV positif, chimioradiothérapie et dénutrition augmentent le risque.","l2":"Étude monobras multicentrique au Brésil : bilan dentaire, nutritionnel (PG-SGA) et statut HPV avant traitement ; mucite cotée chaque jour par un stomatologue (CTCAE v5).\n\nIncidence de mucite tous grades 65,2 %, de grade 3–4 28 %. Facteurs de risque : HPV positif (OR 1,65), chimioradiothérapie (OR 1,86), risque nutritionnel (OR 2,36) ; les cancers du larynx sont moins à risque.","l3":{"points":["Mucite tous grades : 65,2 % (IC95 % 60,0–70,4) ; G3–4 : 28 %."],"limites":["Pas de groupe témoin : l'efficacité affirmée en conclusion n'est pas démontrée par ce plan."],"pratique":"Le dépistage nutritionnel aide à repérer les patients qui feront une mucite sévère malgré la PBM."},"topic":"mucite-orale","title":"Prophylactic photobiomodulation to reduce oral mucositis in head and neck cancer: an observational study.","journal":"Supportive care in cancer : official journal of the Multinational Association of Supportive Care in Cancer","journal_abbr":"Support Care Cancer","authors":["Ferrari R","Felizardo AC","Siqueira L","et al."],"date":"2025-10-14","year":2025,"week":"2025-S42","doi":"10.1007/s00520-025-10020-y","pmcid":null,"license":null,"oa":false,"pdf":null,"cited_by":3,"pub_types":["Observational Study","Multicenter Study"],"abstract":[{"label":"PURPOSE","text":"Oral mucositis (OM) is a common side effect of radiotherapy (RT) and chemoradiotherapy (CRT) in patients with head and neck cancer (HNC). This study investigates the impact of prophylactic photobiomodulation (PBM) on OM incidence in patients with HNC undergoing RT or CRT."},{"label":"METHODS","text":"This single-arm, observational study was conducted across multiple cancer centers in Brazil. Baseline assessments included clinical and radiographic dental evaluations, nutritional risk assessment via the Patient-Generated Subjective Global Assessment (PG-SGA), and HPV status determination. All patients received daily prophylactic PBM during RT. OM incidence and severity were assessed daily by a stomatologist using the Common Terminology Criteria for Adverse Events (CTCAE) Version 5.0. Descriptive statistics summarized patient characteristics, and logistic regression identified potential predictors of severe OM (grade 3+)."},{"label":"RESULTS","text":"A total of 118 patients with HNC were recruited. The cumulative incidence of OM of any grade was 65.2% (95% CI 60.0-70.4%), with severe OM (Grade 3/4) observed in 28.0% of patients. Risk factors significantly associated with OM included HPV-positive tumor status (OR = 1.65, P = 0.02), concurrent CRT (OR = 1.86, P = 0.002), and nutritional risk (OR = 2.36, P = 0.004). Tumor location also influenced OM risk, with laryngeal cancers showing a lower incidence (OR = 0.72, P = 0.02)."},{"label":"CONCLUSION","text":"Prophylactic PBM appears effective in reducing the incidence and severity of OM among patients with HNC undergoing RT or CRT. These findings support its potential role as a preventive strategy in routine clinical care."}],"keywords":["Head and neck cancer","Oral mucositis","Photobiomodulation"]},{"pmid":"41058057","title_fr":"Faisabilité d'un dispositif de laser basse énergie intégré à une gouttière pour réduire la mucite radio-induite sévère","type":"cases","level":4,"pop":"adulte","ctx":["ORL"],"aim":"prévention","mode":["intrabuccal","laser"],"nm":[670,830,910],"n":15,"effect":"favorable","theme":"Nouveaux dispositifs","l1":"Une gouttière intrabuccale émettant à trois longueurs d'onde (670, 830, 910 nm) sur 43 points : 6,6 % de mucites sévères chez 15 patients irradiés, contre 30–40 % attendus.","l2":"Étude de faisabilité monobras multicentrique : séances de 20 minutes, cinq fois par semaine, sans dépendance à l'opérateur. Aucun patient n'a eu de douleur sévère, aucun effet indésirable, et tous ont terminé la radiothérapie comme prévu.\n\nPrometteur pour standardiser la délivrance, mais à confirmer par un essai randomisé.","l3":{"points":["Critère principal : mucite OMS ≥ 3 ; résultat 6,6 % (1 patient sur 15)."],"limites":["15 patients, comparaison à des taux historiques ; étude non enregistrée."],"pratique":"Concept intéressant de délivrance automatisée, pas encore validé."},"topic":"mucite-orale","title":"Feasibility Study of a Novel Stent-Based Low-Level Laser Therapy Device for Reducing Severe Radiation-Induced Oral Mucositis.","journal":"Lasers in surgery and medicine","journal_abbr":"Lasers Surg Med","authors":["Baek JY","Yang K","Lee TH","et al."],"date":"2025-10-07","year":2025,"week":"2025-S41","doi":"10.1002/lsm.70069","pmcid":"PMC12689479","license":"cc by-nc","oa":true,"pdf":"pdfs/41058057.pdf","cited_by":1,"pub_types":["Multicenter Study"],"abstract":[{"label":"OBJECTIVES","text":"Radiation-induced oral mucositis (RIOM) is a severe complication of radiotherapy (RT) for head and neck cancers, impairing quality of life and treatment adherence. Low-level laser therapy (LLLT) shows promise for reducing RIOM severity, but operator-dependent devices limit practicality. This feasibility study assessed the safety and efficacy of a novel intraoral stent-based LLLT device designed for consistent laser delivery."},{"label":"MATERIALS AND METHODS","text":"This prospective, single-arm, multicenter study enrolled 15 patients receiving curative RT for head and neck cancers. The stent-based device delivered laser light to 43 oral cavity points at three wavelengths (670, 830, and 910 nm) during 20-min sessions, five times weekly. The primary end point was the incidence of Grade 3 or higher RIOM (WHO oral mucositis scale). Pain severity was a secondary end point (Numeric Pain Rating Scale [NPRS])."},{"label":"RESULTS","text":"Grade 3 or higher RIOM occurred in 6.6% of patients, significantly lower than historical rates (30%-40%). No patients reported severe pain (NPRS ≥ 8). The device was well-tolerated with no adverse effects, and all patients completed RT as planned."},{"label":"CONCLUSIONS","text":"The intraoral stent-based LLLT device is a safe and potentially effective intervention for reducing severe RIOM. Randomized trials are warranted to confirm these findings."},{"label":"TRIAL REGISTRATION","text":"This study was a prospective, single-arm feasibility trial and was not registered in a clinical trial registry, as it did not meet the criteria for mandatory registration at the time of its initiation."}],"keywords":["feasibility studies","head and neck neoplasms","low‐level light therapy","oral mucositis","radiotherapy"]},{"pmid":"40928526","title_fr":"PBM intrabuccale versus extrabuccale pour la mucite en ORL : essai multicentrique randomisé en simple aveugle","type":"rct","level":2,"pop":"adulte","ctx":["ORL"],"aim":"les deux","mode":["intrabuccal","extrabuccal","laser"],"nm":[660,810,980],"n":58,"effect":"neutre","theme":"Extra vs intrabuccal","key":true,"l1":"Chez 58 patients ORL irradiés, la PBM extrabuccale (810/980 nm) contrôle la mucite aussi bien que l'intrabuccale (660 nm), avec moins de difficultés à avaler et un goût mieux préservé.","l2":"Essai multicentrique brésilien (2021–2023), cancers de la cavité buccale et de l'oropharynx. Intrabuccal : 660 nm, 100 mW, 3 s par point, 10 J/cm². Extrabuccal : laser superpulsé 810/980 nm, 1 W, spot de 4,91 cm², 30 s par point, 6,11 J/cm². Cinq séances par semaine.\n\nTous les patients ont eu une mucite. Pas de différence de grade (OMS p = 0,69 ; NCI p = 0,82) ni de délai d'apparition des ulcérations. Le groupe extrabuccal avalait mieux aux semaines 4 et 6 et perdait moins le goût à la semaine 5. C'est l'essai de référence en ORL sur la question extra/intrabuccal.","l3":{"points":["Enregistrement NCT05242991 ; 58 patients, âge moyen 59 ans, 69 % d'hommes.","Grade de mucite : OMS p = 0,69 ; NCI p = 0,82.","Dysphagie moindre en extrabuccal à J20 (p = 0,02) et J30 (p = 0,04) ; goût mieux préservé à J25 (p = 0,01)."],"limites":["Pas de bras sans PBM ; effectif modeste.","Comparaison qui mélange voie, longueur d'onde et puissance."],"pratique":"En radiothérapie ORL, l'extrabuccal est une option valable, notamment en cas de trismus ou de douleur à l'ouverture buccale."},"topic":"mucite-orale","title":"Intraoral vs. extraoral photobiomodulation therapy for oral mucositis in head and neck cancer patients: a multicenter, randomized, single-blind clinical trial.","journal":"Supportive care in cancer : official journal of the Multinational Association of Supportive Care in Cancer","journal_abbr":"Support Care Cancer","authors":["Klein IP","Pinto MBR","Só BB","et al."],"date":"2025-09-10","year":2025,"week":"2025-S37","doi":"10.1007/s00520-025-09877-w","pmcid":null,"license":null,"oa":false,"pdf":null,"cited_by":7,"pub_types":["Randomized Controlled Trial","Multicenter Study","Comparative Study"],"abstract":[{"label":"PURPOSE","text":"To compare the efficacy of intraoral (IOPBM) and extraoral photobiomodulation (EOPBM) protocols for the prevention and treatment of oral mucositis (OM) in patients with oral or oropharyngeal squamous cell carcinoma (SCC) to submitted radiotherapy (RT)."},{"label":"METHODS","text":"This randomized, blinded, multicenter clinical trial enrolled 58 patients with oral or oropharyngeal SCC, who were allocated into two groups matched by treatment type, clinical stage, and RT modality. Group I (IOPBM) received intraoral photobiomodulation (PBM) with a continuous InGaAlP diode laser (660 nm, 100 mW, 0.03 cm2 spot size, 3 s per point, fluence 10 J/cm2). Group II (EOPBM) received extraoral PBM using a superpulsed diode laser with dual wavelengths (810/980 nm, 1000 mW, 4.91 cm2 spot size, 30 s per point, fluence 6.11 J/cm2). PBM was performed five times per week during RT or until complete resolution of oral mucositis lesions."},{"label":"RESULTS","text":"A total of 58 patients were included from February 2021 to August 2023. The mean age of patients was 59.41 years. Forty (69%) were male, and 18 (31%) were female. All patients experienced some degree of OM during RT. No significant differences were observed between the groups for all OM grades (WHO scale, p = 0.69; NCI scale, p = 0.82) or in the meantime to the development of ulcerated OM (WHO scale, p = 0.63; NCI scale, p = 0.61). On day 20 (week 4) and day 30 (week 6), patients in the EOPBM group reported significantly less difficulty swallowing compared to the IOPBM group (Mann-Whitney test, p = 0.02 and p = 0.04, respectively). On day 25 (week 5), the EOPBM group demonstrated a significantly smaller decline in taste perception compared to the IOPBM group, as determined by the Mann-Whitney test (p = 0.01)."},{"label":"CONCLUSION","text":"The EOPBM protocol was found to be as effective as IOPBM in managing OM, with the added benefit of providing greater patient comfort during application."},{"label":"TRIAL REGISTRATION","text":"Registered with ClinicalTrials.gov (NCT05242991)."}],"keywords":["Head and neck cancer","Oral mucositis","Photobiomodulation","Radiotherapy"]},{"pmid":"41002684","title_fr":"Facteurs de mucite sévère malgré la PBM chez les greffés de cellules souches","type":"cohort","level":4,"pop":"mixte","ctx":["CSH"],"aim":"prévention","mode":[],"nm":[],"n":171,"effect":"—","theme":"Facteurs de risque","l1":"Sur 171 greffés traités par PBM, le conditionnement, le méthotrexate, une culture microbienne positive et des marqueurs biologiques (CRP, transaminases) prédisent une mucite sévère malgré la PBM.","l2":"Analyse rétrospective sur deux ans (Brésil). Les variables étaient relevées au premier jour du conditionnement et au jour de la mucite maximale.\n\nPour les patients cumulant ces facteurs, les auteurs suggèrent d'ajuster la dosimétrie ou d'ajouter des traitements adjuvants. Démarche de PBM personnalisée intéressante, à valider.","l3":{"points":["Prédicteurs dès J1 du conditionnement : type de conditionnement, MTX, cultures positives, ALAT élevées."],"limites":["Rétrospectif ; pas de comparaison sans PBM."],"pratique":"Identifier tôt les greffés à haut risque pour renforcer la prévention."},"topic":"mucite-orale","title":"Determinants of Severe Oral Mucositis Development Despite Photobiomodulation Therapy in Stem Cell Transplant Patients.","journal":"Dentistry journal","journal_abbr":"Dent J (Basel)","authors":["Rezende SB","Campos L","de Souza MC","et al."],"date":"2025-09-08","year":2025,"week":"2025-S37","doi":"10.3390/dj13090411","pmcid":"PMC12468854","license":"cc by","oa":true,"pdf":"pdfs/41002684.pdf","cited_by":1,"pub_types":[],"abstract":[{"label":"","text":"Background/Objectives: Oral mucositis (OM) is an extremely common and debilitating side effect in patients undergoing Hematopoietic Stem Cell Transplantation (HSCT). As such, managing this condition is an obligatory component for their supportive care. Among the available treatment options, photobiomodulation therapy has long since established itself as the most promising approach, with consistent evidence supporting its efficacy. Despite this, the clinical results of this therapy are still influenced by the chosen dosimetry parameters, as well as patient-specific factors. Therefore, this study aimed to assess which factors can continue to influence the severity and onset of OM despite the protective effect provided by the ongoing photobiomodulation therapy. Methods: To achieve this, a retrospective analysis was conducted on the medical records of 171 patients who underwent PBMT during HSCT over a two-year period at the Brazilian Institute of Cancer Control. These files were used to review clinical and laboratorial parameters, such as OM grade, serum CRP, creatinine, urea, OGT, PGT, pain levels, presence of microorganisms in cultures, type of transplant, and conditioning regimens. All of these were evaluated at two different time points: the first day of conditioning and the day of highest OM degree. The statistical analysis was then conducted to evaluate the correlation between the variables and OM grade. Results: Results showed that type of conditioning regimens, use of MTX during conditioning, presence of microorganisms in cultures, and increased serum CRP, PGT, and initial urea levels were associated with severe OM. Conclusions: Among these, the type of conditioning regimens, MTX usage, positive results in cultures, and increased PGT levels on the first day of conditioning could be considered predictive for severe OM and the worst PBMT results. Consequently, in patients exposed to additional risk factors, adjustments in dosimetry paraments of PBMT or employment of adjuvant therapies should be considered to achieve better results."}],"keywords":["Hematopoietic Stem Cell Transplantation","dosimetry","oral mucositis","photobiomodulation therapy","risk factors"]},{"pmid":"40889543","title_fr":"La PBM comme stratégie préventive de la mucite en oncopédiatrie : revue systématique et méta-analyse","type":"ma","level":1,"pop":"pédiatrie","ctx":["chimio"],"aim":"prévention","mode":["laser","LED"],"nm":[],"n":177,"effect":"favorable","theme":"Synthèse des preuves","key":true,"l1":"Cinq essais randomisés (177 enfants, laser ou LED) : la PBM préventive réduit l'incidence et la sévérité de la mucite chez l'enfant atteint d'hémopathie maligne.","l2":"Revue (PICOS) des essais randomisés chez les 0–22 ans recevant une chimiothérapie pour hémopathie maligne (recherche jusqu'en février 2025). 97 enfants ont reçu un laser, 80 une LED.\n\nTrois essais ont pu être méta-analysés : bénéfice significatif (p = 0,04). Le résumé donne un IC95 % de 0,01 à 0,84, sans préciser la mesure d'effet. Ce résultat contraste avec la méta-analyse pédiatrique négative de 2024, qui portait sur un autre ensemble d'essais.","l3":{"points":["5 ECR, 4 mois à 22 ans ; 3 essais méta-analysés (p = 0,04)."],"limites":["Très peu d'essais poolés ; mesure d'effet ambiguë dans le résumé."],"pratique":"Soutient la PBM préventive en oncopédiatrie, à mettre en regard de la méta-analyse In Vivo 2024."},"topic":"mucite-orale","title":"Photobiomodulation as a preventive strategy for oral mucositis in pediatric oncology: a systematic review and meta-analysis.","journal":"Journal of dentistry","journal_abbr":"J Dent","authors":["Marques MT","Arêde LT","Rodrigues JVS","et al."],"date":"2025-08-30","year":2025,"week":"2025-S35","doi":"10.1016/j.jdent.2025.106074","pmcid":null,"license":null,"oa":false,"pdf":null,"cited_by":1,"pub_types":["Systematic Review","Meta-Analysis","Research Support, Non-U.S. Gov't"],"abstract":[{"label":"OBJECTIVES","text":"Oral mucositis (OM) is one of the most frequent and debilitating complications in children undergoing treatment for hematologic malignancies. Current management strategies focus on minimizing OM severity and alleviating symptoms. This systematic review aimed to evaluate the effectiveness of photobiomodulation (PBM) in preventing OM in pediatric patients with hematologic malignancies receiving chemotherapy."},{"label":"DATA/SOURCES","text":"Guided by the PICOS framework, this review addressed the following research question: Is PBM therapy effective in reducing the incidence and severity of oral mucositis in children and adolescents with hematologic malignancies undergoing chemotherapy? A comprehensive search was conducted in PubMed, Scopus, Web of Science, Embase and The Cochrane databases through February 2025. Eligible studies were randomized clinical trials involving patients aged 0-22 years diagnosed with hematologic malignancies treated by means of chemotherapy, and undergoing preventive PBM therapy for OM."},{"label":"STUDY SELECTION","text":"Five randomized controlled trials were included, encompassing patients aged 4 months to 22 years. Of these, 97 received low-level laser therapy (LLLT) and 80 received light-emitting diode therapy (LEDT). PBM was consistently reported as effective in preventing the onset and reducing the severity of OM in these populations. Three studies were eligible for meta-analysis, which demonstrated a statistically significant benefit of PBM therapy (p = 0.04), with a 95% confidence interval ranging from 0.01 to 0.84."},{"label":"CONCLUSIONS","text":"PBM therapy appears to be an effective preventive strategy for oral mucositis in pediatric patients undergoing chemotherapy for hematologic malignancies. Further large-scale trials are warranted to establish standardized protocols and optimize treatment outcomes."},{"label":"CLINICAL SIGNIFICANCE","text":"PBM therapy significantly reduces the incidence and severity of oral mucositis in pediatric patients undergoing chemotherapy for hematologic malignancies. This non-invasive intervention offers an emerging preventive strategy, improving patient comfort and treatment adherence. Standardized PBM protocols may enhance supportive care in pediatric oncology settings."}],"keywords":["Cancer","Photobiomodulation, children, low-level laser therapy, oral mucositis"]},{"pmid":"40852911","title_fr":"« Je n'avais qu'à ouvrir grand la bouche » : acceptabilité de la PBM pour la mucite en soins de support pédiatriques","type":"qualitative","level":0,"pop":"pédiatrie","ctx":["chimio"],"aim":"prévention","mode":["intrabuccal"],"nm":[],"n":27,"effect":"—","theme":"Implantation","l1":"Enfants, parents et soignants britanniques jugent la PBM acceptable ; les freins sont le manque de compréhension du traitement et la charge de travail pour les équipes.","l2":"Entretiens et groupes focaux (27 participants, enfants de 8 à 15 ans) analysés selon le cadre théorique de l'acceptabilité (TFA). Quatre thèmes : attitudes positives, importance de l'autonomie de l'enfant, compréhension insuffisante du traitement, charge pour les équipes.\n\nRecommandations : organiser le service pour limiter la charge et créer des supports adaptés à l'enfant.","l3":{"points":["Plus de la moitié des familles et trois quarts des soignants avaient déjà une expérience de la PBM."],"limites":["Étude qualitative, contexte britannique."],"pratique":"Prévoir des supports explicatifs pour l'enfant et une organisation dédiée."},"topic":"mucite-orale","title":"'All I Had to Do Was Open My Mouth Wide'-A Qualitative Exploration of the Acceptability of Photobiomodulation for Oral Mucositis Management in Paediatric Supportive Care.","journal":"Pediatric blood & cancer","journal_abbr":"Pediatr Blood Cancer","authors":["Heggie C","Chauhan A","Gray-Burrows KA","et al."],"date":"2025-08-25","year":2025,"week":"2025-S35","doi":"10.1002/pbc.31978","pmcid":null,"license":"cc by","oa":false,"pdf":null,"cited_by":1,"pub_types":["Research Support, N.I.H., Extramural"],"abstract":[{"label":"BACKGROUND","text":"Oral mucositis (OM) presents a common and debilitating side effect of chemotherapy for children and young people (CYP). Photobiomodulation is recommended for OM prevention in international guidance; however, the acceptability of photobiomodulation in paediatric cancer care is uncertain. This study explores this acceptability with CYP, their parents, and healthcare professionals (HCPs)."},{"label":"METHODS","text":"Semi-structured interviews with CYP/parent dyads and focus groups with HCPs were audio-recorded and professionally transcribed. Framework analysis was completed utilising the Theoretical Framework of Acceptability (TFA) using an initial deductive approach for theoretical rigour. Over-arching themes within and between constructs and participant groups were developed. Recruitment occurred alongside analysis until there was repetition of data and an absence of novel codes."},{"label":"RESULTS","text":"Twenty-seven participants were interviewed. CYP were aged 8-15 years old; HCPs had diverse professional roles within paediatric oncology and dentistry. Over half of families and three quarters of HCPs had previous photobiomodulation experience. Data were coded for all seven TFA constructs. Four themes, consisting of multiple subthemes, were developed from 42 distinct codes: (i) positive attitudes towards photobiomodulation; (ii) importance of child-centredness and autonomy; (iii) lack of understanding of photobiomodulation treatment; (iv) perceived additional burden to healthcare teams."},{"label":"CONCLUSION","text":"Photobiomodulation for OM prevention is acceptable to CYP, their parents, and HCPs during cancer treatment. Exploration of the theoretical facets of this acceptability supports adaptation of services to overcome highlighted challenges to photobiomodulation acceptance. Photobiomodulation services should be designed to reduce burden on healthcare services, with resources developed to support CYP's autonomy, comprehension and self-efficacy with intra-oral treatment."}],"keywords":["acceptability","low‐level laser","mucositis","oral mucositis","photobiomodulation","qualitative research"]},{"pmid":"40824766","title_fr":"La PBM réduit-elle le nitrite salivaire dans la mucite ? Revue systématique","type":"sr","level":3,"pop":"mixte","ctx":["mixte"],"aim":"les deux","mode":[],"nm":[],"n":null,"effect":"favorable","theme":"Mécanismes","l1":"Trois études concordent : la PBM abaisse le nitrite salivaire, marqueur du stress oxydatif, ce qui pourrait expliquer une partie de son effet sur la mucite.","l2":"Revue (PubMed, Cochrane, jusqu'en juin 2025) avec évaluation RoB 2.0. Les protocoles et les traitements anticancéreux diffèrent, mais le sens de l'effet est constant.\n\nPreuve mécanistique préliminaire, fondée sur trois études seulement.","l3":{"points":["3 études incluses."],"limites":["Très peu d'études ; hétérogénéité des protocoles."],"pratique":"Pas de conséquence pratique."},"topic":"mucite-orale","title":"Can Photobiomodulation Therapy Reduce Salivary Nitrite in Oral Mucositis? A Systematic Review.","journal":"Photobiomodulation, photomedicine, and laser surgery","journal_abbr":"Photobiomodul Photomed Laser Surg","authors":["Khalil M","Soliman G","Hasan A","et al."],"date":"2025-08-15","year":2025,"week":"2025-S33","doi":"10.1177/25785478251370450","pmcid":null,"license":null,"oa":false,"pdf":null,"cited_by":0,"pub_types":["Systematic Review"],"abstract":[{"label":"","text":"Background: Oral mucositis (OM) is a frequent and debilitating adverse effect of cancer treatments, primarily mediated by oxidative stress and inflammatory pathways involving reactive oxygen species (ROS) and nitric oxide. Photobiomodulation (PBM) therapy has emerged as a potential intervention to mitigate OM severity by regulating these mechanisms. This review examines the influence of PBM on salivary nitrite concentrations-a key biomarker of oxidative stress-in patients with OM. Methods: A systematic literature search was conducted across PubMed and the Cochrane Library, encompassing clinical trials published up to June 2025. Included studies investigated the effects of PBM on salivary nitrite levels in patients receiving anticancer therapy. Methodological quality was assessed using the Revised Cochrane Risk of Bias tool (RoB 2.0). Results: Three studies satisfied the inclusion criteria. Although variations existed among the studies regarding anticancer treatment modalities and PBM irradiation parameters, all consistently reported a reduction in salivary nitrite levels following PBM therapy. Conclusion: PBM therapy appears effective in alleviating OM severity, potentially attributable to its capacity to decrease salivary nitrite concentrations. Additional research is warranted to validate these preliminary findings and optimize PBM protocols for clinical implementation."}],"keywords":["nitrite","oral mucositis","photobiomodulation"]},{"pmid":"40640452","title_fr":"Mucite chez les greffés de cellules souches sous PBM prophylactique : cohorte rétrospective","type":"cohort","level":4,"pop":"adulte","ctx":["CSH"],"aim":"prévention","mode":[],"nm":[],"n":68,"effect":"favorable","theme":"Vie réelle","l1":"Chez 68 greffés sous PBM prophylactique, 54 % ont eu une mucite mais aucune de grade 3 ou 4 ; le conditionnement MEL100 multiplie le risque par 14.","l2":"Cohorte brésilienne d'un centre de référence. Associations : âge jeune et mucite de grade 1–2 ; conditionnement FluBuMel/Cy et grade 2 ; mucite pharyngée et grade 1.\n\nL'absence totale de forme sévère est remarquable, mais l'étude n'a pas de groupe sans PBM.","l3":{"points":["Âge médian 40 ans ; 54,4 % de femmes.","MEL100 : risque multiplié par 14,36."],"limites":["Rétrospectif, sans comparateur."],"pratique":"Surveiller de près les conditionnements à base de melphalan, même sous PBM."},"topic":"mucite-orale","title":"Oral mucositis in patients undergoing hematopoietic stem cell transplantation with prophylactic photobiomodulation: A retrospective cohort study.","journal":"Supportive care in cancer : official journal of the Multinational Association of Supportive Care in Cancer","journal_abbr":"Support Care Cancer","authors":["Pereira TBF","Lima BMF","Potter GS","et al."],"date":"2025-07-10","year":2025,"week":"2025-S28","doi":"10.1007/s00520-025-09750-w","pmcid":null,"license":null,"oa":false,"pdf":null,"cited_by":0,"pub_types":[],"abstract":[{"label":"PURPOSE","text":"To analyze the incidence, severity, and factors associated with the development of oral mucositis (OM) in patients undergoing hematopoietic stem cell transplantation (HSCT) who are receiving prophylactic photobiomodulation therapy (PBMT)."},{"label":"METHODS","text":"Data, including sex, age, grade of OM, and conditioning regimen, were collected from medical records of patients undergoing prophylactic PBMT at a reference hospital in Brazil. Pearson's chi-square test or Fisher's exact test were applied to examine the associations between variables. Kaplan-Meier method was used to calculate disease-free survival time."},{"label":"RESULTS","text":"A total of 68 patients were included in the study, 54.4% of whom were female, with a median age of 40 years. OM occurred in 37 patients (54.4%). Most OMs were classified as Grade 0 (45.5%). No cases of Grade 3 or 4 OM were observed. A statistically significant association was found between Grade 1 and 2 OM and younger age (p = 0.017). FluBuMel/Cy conditioning regimen was associated with the occurrence of Grade 2 OM (p = 0.006), while pharyngeal mucositis was associated with Grade 1 (p < 0.0001). Age, pharyngeal mucositis and some types of conditioning regimen had a significant impact on the disease-free survival rate for OM. Patients undergoing the MEL100 conditioning protocol had a 14.36-fold increased risk of developing this complication."},{"label":"CONCLUSION","text":"In HSCT patients undergoing prophylactic use of PBMT, factors such as age, specific chemotherapy conditioning protocols, and the presence of pharyngeal mucositis were found to be associated with OM development."}],"keywords":["Cohort studies","Hematopoietic stem cell transplantation","Oral mucositis"]},{"pmid":"40549226","title_fr":"PBM combinée extra- et intrabuccale pour prévenir et traiter mucite et xérostomie en ORL","type":"rct","level":2,"pop":"adulte","ctx":["ORL"],"aim":"les deux","mode":["intrabuccal","extrabuccal","laser"],"nm":[],"n":75,"effect":"favorable","theme":"Extra vs intrabuccal","key":true,"l1":"Chez 75 patients ORL, ajouter une PBM extrabuccale (sur les glandes salivaires) à l'intrabuccale réduit la mucite dès la 2e semaine, améliore le débit salivaire et la qualité de vie.","l2":"Essai randomisé brésilien avec simulation : PBM intrabuccale + extrabuccale simulée (n = 38) contre PBM intrabuccale + extrabuccale réelle (n = 37). Mesures : qualité de vie (OHIP-14, PROMs, XeQOLS), grade de mucite, sialométrie.\n\nLa combinaison fait mieux sur tous les critères (p < 0,001), dont le débit salivaire de repos et stimulé. Contrairement aux essais qui opposent les deux voies, celui-ci teste leur addition.","l3":{"points":["Enregistrement ReBEC RBR-5p2xfzd.","Grade de mucite inférieur dès la semaine 2 (p < 0,001).","Sialométrie de repos et stimulée améliorée (p < 0,001)."],"limites":["Paramètres détaillés seulement dans le texte intégral ; analyse par Kruskal-Wallis pour deux groupes.","Monocentrique."],"pratique":"Ajouter une PBM extrabuccale en regard des glandes salivaires pendant la radiothérapie ORL semble apporter un bénéfice supplémentaire."},"topic":"mucite-orale","title":"Combined extraoral and intraoral photobiomodulation for the prevention and management of oral mucositis and Xerostomia caused by the treatment of head and neck cancers.","journal":"Lasers in medical science","journal_abbr":"Lasers Med Sci","authors":["Borghesi Brunelli G","Pinheiro SL"],"date":"2025-06-23","year":2025,"week":"2025-S26","doi":"10.1007/s10103-025-04539-z","pmcid":null,"license":null,"oa":false,"pdf":null,"cited_by":8,"pub_types":["Randomized Controlled Trial"],"abstract":[{"label":"PURPOSE","text":"To evaluate the effectiveness of combined intraoral and extraoral photobiomodulation (PBM) in the management of oral mucositis (OM) and xerostomia in patients undergoing head and neck cancer (HNC) treatment."},{"label":"METHODS","text":"Seventy-five patients with HNC were included and randomly assigned to 2 groups: those receiving intraoral PBM and a simulated extraoral PBM application (PBMI-SE group, n = 38); and those receiving combined intraoral and extraoral PBM (PBMIE group, n = 37). Quality of life related to OM and xerostomia, OM symptoms, OM grade, and salivary flow rate were assessed. Results were analyzed using the Kruskal-Wallis test (Student-Newman-Keuls)."},{"label":"RESULTS","text":"Combined intraoral and extraoral PBM led to a significant improvement in quality of life (OHIP-14, PROMS, and XeQOLS scores) compared with intraoral PBM alone (p < 0.001). From the second week onward, the PBMIE group experienced significantly lower OM grades (p < 0.001) than the PBMI-SE group. Additionally, the PBMIE group showed a significant improvement in both resting and stimulated sialometry from the second collection onward compared with the PBMI-SE group (p < 0.001)."},{"label":"CONCLUSION","text":"Combined extraoral and intraoral PBM improved the quality of life of patients undergoing HNC treatment, particularly in relation to OM and xerostomia. This combined approach effectively controlled OM severity and improved salivary flow, offering a potential treatment option for these patients."},{"label":"CLINICAL TRIAL REGISTRATION","text":"This study was registered in Brazilian Registry of Clinical Trials (ReBEC) (RBR-5p2xfzd). First Posted: October 03, 2023."}],"keywords":["Cancer","Cancer treatment","Oral mucositis","Photobiomodulation","Xerostomia"]},{"pmid":"41040611","title_fr":"Effet potentiel de la PBM sur la mucite des radio-chimiothérapies ORL : revue de la littérature","type":"review","level":5,"pop":"mixte","ctx":["ORL"],"aim":"les deux","mode":[],"nm":[],"n":null,"effect":"favorable","theme":"Revue générale","l1":"Revue des essais récents : la PBM réduit sévérité et durée de la mucite en ORL ; protocoles courants de 630 à 980 nm, 2 à 6 J/cm², 10 à 125 s par point.","l2":"Revue narrative taïwanaise centrée sur les essais randomisés chez l'adulte et l'enfant. Elle décrit les mécanismes et les obstacles à l'implantation, en premier lieu le manque de standardisation.\n\nUtile surtout pour ses fourchettes de paramètres.","l3":{"points":["Paramètres usuels : 630–980 nm ; 2–6 J/cm² ; 10–125 s/point."],"limites":["Revue narrative."],"pratique":"Repères de dosimétrie cohérents avec MASCC/ISOO."},"topic":"mucite-orale","title":"The potential effect of photobiomodulation on oral mucositis induced by head and neck radio-chemotherapy: A literature review.","journal":"Journal of dental sciences","journal_abbr":"J Dent Sci","authors":["Bahrami R","Eftekharian S","Jafarieh R","et al."],"date":"2025-06-18","year":2025,"week":"2025-S25","doi":"10.1016/j.jds.2025.06.002","pmcid":"PMC12485458","license":"cc by-nc-nd","oa":true,"pdf":"pdfs/41040611.pdf","cited_by":2,"pub_types":["Review"],"abstract":[{"label":"","text":"Oral mucositis (OM) is a common side effect of head and neck radio-chemotherapy. It manifests as painful, ulcerative lesions in the oral mucosa, significantly impairing essential functions such as eating, speaking, and maintaining oral hygiene. It finally has adverse effects on the patient's quality of life. Photobiomodulation therapy (PBMT) has emerged as a promising non-invasive modality for preventing and treating OM. Due to controversy among the results of studies, this literature review synthesizes findings from recent randomized controlled trials that evaluated the use of PBMT in adult and pediatric cancer patients. This review also highlights the mechanisms, clinical efficacy, and future challenges of integrating PBMT into routine supportive cancer care. The evidence shows that PBMT significantly reduces the severity and duration of OM, improved patient-reported outcomes, and minimized treatment-related morbidity. By utilizing low-intensity light sources-typically lasers or LEDs-PBMT enhances cellular metabolism, reduces inflammation, alleviates pain, and promotes mucosal healing. Despite its clinical potential, widespread implementation of PBMT is hindered by variability in treatment protocols and limited standardization; in brief, laser protocols - wavelengths (630-980 nm), energy densities (2-6 J/cm2), and application durations (10-125 s/point) are commonly used for preventing and treating OM induced by head and neck radio-chemotherapy. However, future studies with long-term follow-ups will be necessary to standardize PBMT protocols, as standardization is essential to integrate PBMT into routine cancer care."}],"keywords":["Low-level laser","Oral mucositis","Photobiomodulation","Radio-chemotherapy"]},{"pmid":"40645840","title_fr":"Voies juridiques et jurisprudence pour l'accès à la PBM contre la mucite au Brésil","type":"survey","level":0,"pop":"—","ctx":["mixte"],"aim":"—","mode":[],"nm":[],"n":50,"effect":"—","theme":"Implantation","l1":"Au Brésil, 50 décisions de justice (2005–2024) ont toutes accordé, au moins en partie, l'accès à la PBM contre la mucite, surtout face aux assurances santé.","l2":"Étude transversale des bases de données des tribunaux de tous les États, de la Cour supérieure et de la Cour suprême. 82 % des demandes ont été entièrement accordées, 18 % partiellement (dommages moraux refusés). 94 % visaient des assureurs privés.\n\nDélai moyen jusqu'à la décision provisoire : 13 jours. Illustration originale de la reconnaissance de la PBM comme soin standard.","l3":{"points":["180 décisions trouvées, 50 éligibles."],"limites":["Contexte juridique propre au Brésil."],"pratique":"Montre que la PBM est considérée comme un standard de soins opposable au Brésil."},"topic":"mucite-orale","title":"Legal pathways and case law on photobiomodulation therapy for oral mucositis in cancer care: a retrospective cross-sectional study.","journal":"Oral surgery, oral medicine, oral pathology and oral radiology","journal_abbr":"Oral Surg Oral Med Oral Pathol Oral Radiol","authors":["Startari DM","Esteves-Pereira TC","Wolkart EN","et al."],"date":"2025-05-24","year":2025,"week":"2025-S21","doi":"10.1016/j.oooo.2025.05.006","pmcid":null,"license":null,"oa":false,"pdf":null,"cited_by":0,"pub_types":["Research Support, Non-U.S. Gov't"],"abstract":[{"label":"OBJECTIVE","text":"Oral mucositis (OM) is a common and debilitating complication of cancer therapy that can be prevented with photobiomodulation therapy (PBMT), a light-based intervention recommended by international guidelines. This study aimed to identify the legal pathways to patient access to PBMT for OM in Brazilian cancer care."},{"label":"STUDY DESIGN","text":"This retrospective cross-sectional study assessed electronic databases of the Courts of Justice from all Brazilian states, the Superior Court of Justice, and the Federal Supreme Court, yielding 180 court rulings for \"mucositis\"."},{"label":"RESULTS","text":"A total of 50 court rulings, adjudicated between 2005 and 2024, met the eligibility criteria. Of these, 41 (82%) were fully granted and 9 (18%) partially granted, typically denying compensation for moral damages. In 47 cases (94%), the rulings were directed to health insurance providers, while 3 (6%) involved the public healthcare system. The mean time from filing to interim relief was 13.3 days (SD = 28.3; range: 1-115). Chemotherapy was the most common cancer treatment, followed by radiotherapy and hematopoietic stem cell transplantation."},{"label":"CONCLUSIONS","text":"This novel study reveals a consistent legal framework supporting access to PBMT for OM in Brazil, reflecting a progressive judicial stance with potential implications for health policy and supportive cancer care."}],"keywords":[]},{"pmid":"40340568","title_fr":"Effet de la PBM sur le nitrite salivaire dans la mucite chimio-induite : essai randomisé","type":"rct","level":2,"pop":"adulte","ctx":["chimio"],"aim":"prévention","mode":["intrabuccal","extrabuccal","laser"],"nm":[650,980],"n":45,"effect":"favorable","theme":"Mécanismes","l1":"Chez 45 patients sous chimiothérapie, la PBM (intrabuccale 650 nm, seule ou avec extrabuccale 980 nm) réduit la sévérité de la mucite et le nitrite salivaire par rapport aux seuls conseils d'hygiène.","l2":"Essai en double aveugle à trois groupes appariés : conseils seuls, conseils + PBM intrabuccale, conseils + PBM intra- et extrabuccale. Mesures à 1 et 2 semaines.\n\nLes deux groupes laser font mieux que le témoin, sans différence entre eux. Même équipe et même schéma que l'essai sur le préconditionnement publié en février 2025 : il s'agit probablement de la même population.","l3":{"points":["Nitrite dosé par réactif de Griess.","Différences significatives entre groupes à S1 et S2 (p < 0,001)."],"limites":["Recouvrement probable avec l'essai PMID 39910516 : ne pas compter deux fois ces patients.","15 patients par groupe."],"pratique":"Pas de bénéfice de l'ajout extrabuccal en chimiothérapie dans cette étude."},"topic":"mucite-orale","title":"Effect of Photobiomodulation on Salivary Nitrite in Patients with Chemotherapy-Induced Oral Mucositis: A Randomized Clinical Trial.","journal":"Photobiomodulation, photomedicine, and laser surgery","journal_abbr":"Photobiomodul Photomed Laser Surg","authors":["Khalil M","Hamadah O","Saifo M"],"date":"2025-05-09","year":2025,"week":"2025-S19","doi":"10.1089/photob.2024.0151","pmcid":null,"license":null,"oa":false,"pdf":null,"cited_by":0,"pub_types":["Randomized Controlled Trial"],"abstract":[{"label":"","text":"Background: Oral mucositis (OM) is a severe inflammatory and ulcerative condition of the oral mucosa commonly induced by chemotherapy. Photobiomodulation (PBM) therapy has been proposed for preventing and treating OM. However, the understanding of light interaction with biological tissues and the variability in light sources and protocols limit its widespread application. This study aimed to evaluate the impact of PBM on salivary nitrite levels, a marker of oxidative stress associated with inflammation and tissue damage. Materials and Methods: This prospective, randomized, double-blind clinical trial included 45 patients, evenly divided into three age- and sex-matched groups. Group 1 received basic oral care instructions prior to chemotherapy. Group 2 received these instructions plus PBM using a 650 nm intraoral diode laser. Group 3 received basic oral care instructions combined with PBM using both a 650 nm intraoral diode laser and a 980 nm extraoral diode laser. OM severity was assessed using World Health Organization criteria, and salivary nitrite levels were measured using the Griess reagent kit (Biotium®) according to the manufacturer's instructions 1 and 2 weeks after the first chemotherapy session. Results: Our study included 45 patients who were evenly distributed into three groups, matched for age, sex, tumor type, and type of chemotherapy. Significant differences in OM severity were observed among the groups at both 1 and 2 weeks (p = 0.000). Salivary nitrite levels also showed significant differences between groups at these time points (p = 0.00). Significant differences were found between the control group and both laser treatment groups, but no significant difference was noted between the two laser treatment groups. Conclusions: PBM effectively reduces OM severity, whether used intraorally alone or combined with extraoral application. This effect is likely due to PBM's ability to lower salivary nitrite levels, indicating reduced oxidative stress and inflammation."}],"keywords":["chemotherapy","nitrite","oral mucositis","photobiomodulation"]},{"pmid":"40316940","title_fr":"PBM pour prévenir et thérapie photodynamique pour traiter la mucite sous méthotrexate chez l'enfant : essai randomisé","type":"rct","level":2,"pop":"pédiatrie","ctx":["chimio"],"aim":"les deux","mode":["laser","PDT"],"nm":[],"n":null,"effect":"favorable","theme":"Associations","metric":{"kind":"rates","outcome":"Incidence de la mucite (bras prévention)","a_label":"Cycle avec PBM","a":6.67,"b_label":"Cycle sans PBM","b":66.67},"l1":"En Iran, chez l'enfant sous méthotrexate, la PBM fait passer la mucite de 66,7 % à 6,7 % ; en curatif, la thérapie photodynamique au vert d'indocyanine accélère l'amélioration contre placebo.","l2":"Essai à deux bras. Prévention : chaque enfant sert de témoin, un cycle sans laser puis un cycle avec PBM (comparaison intra-individuelle, non randomisée). Traitement : les enfants avec mucite sont randomisés entre laser simulé et PDT (vert d'indocyanine + laser basse énergie).\n\nLa PDT réduit les scores OMS, NCI et WCCNR à J3 et J5. Résultat préventif spectaculaire, mais obtenu avec un plan avant-après.","l3":{"points":["Enregistrement IRCT20120101008585N16.","Prévention : 66,67 % puis 6,67 % (p < 0,05).","Traitement : PDT supérieure au placebo à J3 et J5."],"limites":["Bras prévention non randomisé (séquence fixe).","Effectifs non donnés dans le résumé."],"pratique":"La PDT au vert d'indocyanine est une piste curative ; la PBM préventive reste le standard."},"topic":"mucite-orale","title":"Efficacy of photobiomodulation for the prevention and treatment of chemotherapy-induced oral mucositis in pediatric patients with hematologic cancers: a randomized clinical trial.","journal":"BMC cancer","journal_abbr":"BMC Cancer","authors":["Lavaee F","Rezazadeh F","Amanati A","et al."],"date":"2025-05-02","year":2025,"week":"2025-S18","doi":"10.1186/s12885-025-14213-w","pmcid":"PMC12049046","license":"cc by-nc-nd","oa":true,"pdf":"pdfs/40316940.pdf","cited_by":1,"pub_types":["Randomized Controlled Trial"],"abstract":[{"label":"BACKGROUND","text":"Oral mucositis (OM) is a debilitating treatment-related complication that affects most children undergoing chemotherapy. This dual-arm clinical trial aimed to evaluate the efficacy of photobiomodulation (PBM) for prevention and photodynamic therapy (PDT) using indocyanine green (ICG) and low-level laser for treating methotrexate (MTX)-induced OM in pediatric patients with hematologic cancers."},{"label":"METHODS","text":"In the prevention arm, patients were initially assigned to a control group (received no laser) and subsequently reassigned to a PBM group in their next cycle, allowing intra-patient comparison under similar chemotherapy protocols. In the treatment arm, patients diagnosed with OM were randomly assigned to either a control group (sham laser) or a PDT group."},{"label":"RESULTS","text":"In the prevention arm, PBM led to a significant reduction in the incidence of OM from 66.67% to 6.67% (p < 0.05). In the treatment arm, the PDT group showed a significant reduction in the scores of all scales (WHO, NCI, and WCCNR) at both 3 days and 5 days after starting the treatment compared to the control group (p < 0.05). Moreover, the reduction in the NCI score from the third to the fifth days after the starting treatment is higher in the PDT group compared to the control group (p < 0.05)."},{"label":"CONCLUSION","text":"PBM appears to be a safe and effective approach for the prevention of MTX-induced OM in pediatric patients with hematologic cancers. Additionally, PDT using ICG and a low-level laser demonstrates promising results in treating OM lesions. Therefore, these findings highlight the efficacy of PBM for managing MTX-induced OM in this patient group."},{"label":"TRIAL REGISTRATION","text":"The study has been registered on irct.behdasht.gov.ir on 4 December 2022 (Trial Registration Number: IRCT20120101008585N16)."}],"keywords":["Hematology","Indocyanine Green","Laser Therapy","Mucositis","Neoplasms","Pediatrics","Photobiomodulation","Photodynamic Therapy"]},{"pmid":"40295318","title_fr":"L-glutamine et acide hyaluronique associés à la PBM contre la mucite : essai randomisé","type":"rct","level":2,"pop":"adulte","ctx":["ORL"],"aim":"prévention","mode":[],"nm":[],"n":48,"effect":"favorable","theme":"Associations","l1":"Chez 48 patients ORL sous radiochimiothérapie, ajouter L-glutamine et acide hyaluronique à la PBM réduit la mucite sévère à la 30e séance (échelle OMS) et améliore la qualité de vie.","l2":"Essai brésilien : PBM + protocole de soins buccaux (n = 24) contre la même chose plus L-glutamine et acide hyaluronique (n = 24). Évaluations aux 1re, 7e, 14e, 21e et 30e séances.\n\nLe bénéfice apparaît sur l'échelle OMS mais pas sur l'échelle NCI, ce qui fragilise le résultat. Les patients du groupe combiné rapportent moins de symptômes à tous les temps.","l3":{"points":["Enregistrement ReBEC RBR-6ybmgcv.","Mucite sévère moindre à la 30e séance (OMS) ; pas de différence NCI."],"limites":["Discordance entre échelles ; petit effectif."],"pratique":"Association prometteuse, insuffisante pour une recommandation."},"topic":"mucite-orale","title":"Effects of L-glutamine and hyaluronic acid associated with photobiomodulation on oral mucositis: a randomized clinical trial.","journal":"Supportive care in cancer : official journal of the Multinational Association of Supportive Care in Cancer","journal_abbr":"Support Care Cancer","authors":["Dos Santos RW","Martins AFL","Sousa-Neto SS","et al."],"date":"2025-04-28","year":2025,"week":"2025-S18","doi":"10.1007/s00520-025-09480-z","pmcid":null,"license":null,"oa":false,"pdf":null,"cited_by":1,"pub_types":["Randomized Controlled Trial"],"abstract":[{"label":"OBJECTIVE","text":"To investigate whether the application of L-glutamine (L-G) and hyaluronic acid (HA) associated with photobiomodulation (PBM) and the Preventive Oral Care Protocol (POCP) would present similar efficacy as the exclusive use of PBM, associated with POCP, in the prevention and control of severe oral mucositis (OM)."},{"label":"STUDY DESIGN","text":"Patients with head and neck cancer undergoing radiochemotherapy (RCT) were randomized into two groups: group A, patients that received PBM + POCP (n = 24), and group B, patients that received PBM + POCP + L-G + HA (n = 24). OM was assessed using the National Cancer Institute (NCI) and World Health Organization (WHO) scales. The impact of oral health on patients' quality of life and perception of OM symptoms was evaluated. Assessments were made at the 1st, 7th, 14th, 21st, and 30th radiotherapy (RT) sessions."},{"label":"RESULTS","text":"When the severity of OM was assessed according to the WHO scale in the 30th RT, group B showed lower rates of severe OM than group A. There were no differences when using the NCI scale. The impact of oral health on quality of life and the symptoms reported by patients were lower in group B at all times evaluated."},{"label":"CONCLUSION","text":"The combination of PBM + POCP + L-G + HA resulted in a significantly lower severity of OM than PBM + POCP alone, according to the WHO scale at the 30th RT."},{"label":"TRIAL REGISTRATION","text":"Brazilian Clinical Trials Registry-REBEC no. RBR-6ybmgcv. Registration March 27, 2022."}],"keywords":["Glutamine","Head and neck cancer","Hyaluronic acid","Oral mucositis","Photobiomodulation therapy","Radiotherapy"]},{"pmid":"40414733","title_fr":"Photothérapie LED pour la mucite en cancérologie : revue systématique avec analyse GRADE","type":"sr","level":3,"pop":"mixte","ctx":["mixte"],"aim":"les deux","mode":["LED"],"nm":[],"n":256,"effect":"favorable","theme":"Synthèse des preuves","l1":"Cinq études (256 patients) sur les LED : amélioration de la sévérité et de la douleur dans quatre d'entre elles, mais certitude GRADE faible.","l2":"Revue avec RoB-2 pour les essais et ROBINS-I pour les études non randomisées. Hétérogénéité trop grande pour une méta-analyse ; risque de biais modéré.\n\nLes LED réduiraient douleur et durée de la mucite. L'essai américain contre simulation publié en 2026 apporte depuis une preuve randomisée plus solide.","l3":{"points":["5 études, 256 patients ; GRADE : faible."],"limites":["Pas de méta-analyse."],"pratique":"Les LED sont une alternative crédible au laser ; voir l'essai multicentrique de 2026."},"topic":"mucite-orale","title":"LED-based low-level light therapy for oral mucositis in cancer patients: a systematic review and GRADE analysis.","journal":"Oral surgery, oral medicine, oral pathology and oral radiology","journal_abbr":"Oral Surg Oral Med Oral Pathol Oral Radiol","authors":["Joseph B","Mauramo M","Sorsa T","et al."],"date":"2025-04-22","year":2025,"week":"2025-S17","doi":"10.1016/j.oooo.2025.04.095","pmcid":null,"license":"cc by","oa":false,"pdf":null,"cited_by":1,"pub_types":["Systematic Review"],"abstract":[{"label":"OBJECTIVE","text":"This systematic review aimed to evaluate the efficacy of light-emitting diode (LED) therapy in reducing oral mucositis (OM) severity, pain, and healing time in cancer patients and compare it to standard care or sham light."},{"label":"MATERIALS AND METHODS","text":"A comprehensive search of major databases was conducted using relevant MeSH terms and keywords. Two authors independently screened studies meeting the eligibility criteria. The Cochrane Risk-of-Bias tool for randomized controlled trials (RoB-2) and the Risk of Bias in Non-Randomized Studies - of Interventions (ROBINS-I) were used to assess the methodological quality of included studies. The certainty of evidence was evaluated using the Grading of Recommendations, Assessment, Development, and Evaluation (GRADE) approach."},{"label":"RESULTS","text":"The review included five studies (256 participants). Four studies reported significant improvements in OM severity and pain with LED therapy while only one study suggested improved healing time. The risk of bias was moderate among the included studies. Due to heterogeneous data, a meta-analysis could not be performed. The GRADE assessment indicated low certainty of evidence, highlighting the need for further clinical research."},{"label":"CONCLUSION","text":"LED therapy may effectively manage cancer therapy-induced OM by reducing patient-reported pain and OM duration. However, the low certainty of evidence warrants more well-designed, longitudinal studies with larger sample sizes to confirm these findings and establish optimal treatment protocols."}],"keywords":[]},{"pmid":"40259459","title_fr":"Des mécanismes moléculaires aux applications cliniques : la PBM en cancérologie","type":"review","level":5,"pop":"—","ctx":["mixte"],"aim":"—","mode":[],"nm":[],"n":null,"effect":"—","theme":"Sécurité oncologique","l1":"Revue générale de la PBM en cancérologie : effets sur mucite, radiodermite, lymphœdème, neuropathie et entérite radique, et discussion de la sécurité vis-à-vis des cellules tumorales.","l2":"Revue narrative chinoise sur les mécanismes, les toxicités traitées et la question de l'effet de la lumière sur les cellules cancéreuses. Les auteurs citent des travaux où la PBM inhibe la prolifération ou induit l'apoptose.\n\nÀ lire avec les études précliniques de 2024 (mélanome, Xenopus) qui montrent au contraire des signaux de stimulation.","l3":{"points":["Indications : mucite, radiodermite, lymphœdème, neuropathie, entérite radique."],"limites":["Revue narrative ; vision plutôt optimiste de la sécurité."],"pratique":"Pas de conséquence directe."},"topic":"mucite-orale","title":"From molecular mechanisms to clinical applications: A comprehensive review of photobiomodulation in cancer treatment.","journal":"Photochemistry and photobiology","journal_abbr":"Photochem Photobiol","authors":["Wang Q","Oh PS","Jeong HJ"],"date":"2025-04-21","year":2025,"week":"2025-S17","doi":"10.1111/php.14107","pmcid":null,"license":null,"oa":false,"pdf":null,"cited_by":3,"pub_types":["Review"],"abstract":[{"label":"","text":"Photobiomodulation (PBM) is a non-invasive therapeutic technique that regulates biological processes using primarily low-power lasers or light-emitting diodes (LEDs) to achieve therapeutic effects. Its application has expanded significantly, particularly in the field of cancer therapy. This review provides a comprehensive overview of PBM, elucidating its underlying mechanisms of action and its potential applications in cancer therapy. It highlights the benefits of PBM in reducing side effects of cancer treatments such as acute oral mucositis, radiation dermatitis, lymphedema, neuropathic pain, and radiation enteropathy. Furthermore, the ability of PBM to inhibit cancer cell proliferation and induce apoptosis, and discusses safety concerns of PBM in clinical applications, presenting existing research that emphasizes its significant potential in cancer therapy was summarized. PBM therapy may offer promising new clinical options for managing cancer and mitigating the side effects associated with conventional cancer therapies."}],"keywords":["cancer","clinical treatment","photobiomodulation","phototherapy"]},{"pmid":"40238654","title_fr":"Effets de la PBM sur l'épithélium embryonnaire de Xenopus laevis","type":"preclinical","level":5,"pop":"—","ctx":[],"aim":"—","mode":["laser"],"nm":[660],"n":null,"effect":"défavorable","theme":"Sécurité oncologique","l1":"Chez le têtard de xénope, trois séances de PBM à 660 nm font apparaître des structures épithéliales anormales (30,6 % des têtards contre 3,8 %) : un signal de sécurité à explorer.","l2":"Laser 660 nm, 2,08 J/cm², 0,208 W/cm², trois séances à 24 h d'intervalle. Les zones anormales correspondent à une perte de polarité et d'organisation cellulaire au bord de la queue.\n\nCe modèle animal ne se transpose pas directement à la muqueuse humaine, mais il justifie des études de sécurité à long terme.","l3":{"points":["Surface anormale : 0,03 % (témoins) contre 0,37 % (PBM), p < 0,0001.","Têtards atteints : 3,8 % contre 30,6 % (p < 0,001)."],"limites":["Modèle embryonnaire amphibien, très éloigné de la clinique."],"pratique":"Respecter les doses recommandées ; rester attentif aux données de sécurité à long terme."},"topic":"mucite-orale","title":"The Effects of Photobiomodulation Therapy on Xenopus laevis Embryonic Epithelium.","journal":"Photobiomodulation, photomedicine, and laser surgery","journal_abbr":"Photobiomodul Photomed Laser Surg","authors":["Lloyd K","Pitstick L","Gao F","et al."],"date":"2025-04-16","year":2025,"week":"2025-S16","doi":"10.1089/photob.2024.0158","pmcid":null,"license":null,"oa":false,"pdf":null,"cited_by":0,"pub_types":[],"abstract":[{"label":"","text":"Background: Photobiomodulation therapy (PBMT) is widely used in clinical settings, particularly for treating oral mucositis. Although PBMT has shown promise in aiding tissue healing, its safety and potential effects on tumorigenesis remain controversial. Objective: Xenopus laevis (X. laevis) tadpoles have proven to be an effective in vivo model system to study how different therapies affect epithelial biology. Because of this, the goal of this study was to investigate how PBMT influences epithelial tissue in X. laevis tadpoles. Methods: X. laevis tadpoles were treated with PBMT three times, every 24 h, using a 660 nm low-level laser at a fluence of 2.08 J/cm2 and an irradiance of 0.208 W/cm2, resulting in a cumulative dose of 2.25 J. Tadpole tails were then fixed, and the abnormal tissue area was analyzed using fluorescent microscopy. Results: PBMT induced the formation of abnormal tissue structures along the epithelial edges. The altered tissue increased from 0.03% in control tadpoles to 0.37% in PBMT-treated tadpoles (p < 0.0001; Mann-Whitney nonparametric test), and the number of tadpoles displaying this abnormal phenotype increased from 3.8% of control tadpoles to 30.6% of PBMT-treated tadpoles (p < 0.001; chi-squared test). Conclusion: This study demonstrates that PBMT can have significant effects on the epithelial tissue of X. laevis. The PBMT-induced abnormal tissue structures represent a loss of tissue polarization and cellular organization along the tail edge, both of which are essential to maintaining tissue homeostasis. For the first time, we show that X. laevis may provide an in vivo model system for examining the effects and mechanisms of PBMT."}],"keywords":["Xenopus","development","photobiomodulation"]},{"pmid":"40184883","title_fr":"PBM pour prévenir les effets indésirables buccaux des traitements ORL : essai randomisé en simple aveugle","type":"rct","level":2,"pop":"adulte","ctx":["ORL"],"aim":"prévention","mode":[],"nm":[],"n":53,"effect":"favorable","theme":"Efficacité vs soins standards","l1":"Chez 53 patients ORL, la PBM ralentit la progression de la mucite, préserve la fonction salivaire et réduit douleur et dysgueusie, sans effet sur xérostomie, trismus, antalgiques ni candidose.","l2":"Essai espagnol monocentrique (CONSORT, enregistré) comparant PBM et traitement standard. Les critères couvraient l'ensemble des effets buccaux : mucite, douleur, dysgueusie, hyposialie, bouche sèche, trismus, antalgiques et infections fongiques.\n\nBénéfices sur la mucite, l'hyposialie, la douleur et le goût aux moments critiques ; pas d'effet sur les autres critères.","l3":{"points":["Bénéfices : progression de la mucite, fonction salivaire, douleur, dysgueusie.","Sans effet : xérostomie, trismus, consommation d'antalgiques, candidose."],"limites":["Simple aveugle, monocentrique ; paramètres non donnés dans le résumé."],"pratique":"Confirme l'effet sur la mucite ; ne pas promettre d'effet sur le trismus ou la xérostomie."},"topic":"mucite-orale","title":"Photobiomodulation for the prevention of oral side effects secondary to head and neck cancer therapy: results of a randomised, single-blind clinical trial.","journal":"Oral oncology","journal_abbr":"Oral Oncol","authors":["Vianna Camolesi GC","Prado-Pena IB","Gómez-Caamaño A","et al."],"date":"2025-04-03","year":2025,"week":"2025-S14","doi":"10.1016/j.oraloncology.2025.107266","pmcid":null,"license":"cc by","oa":false,"pdf":null,"cited_by":5,"pub_types":["Randomized Controlled Trial"],"abstract":[{"label":"INTRODUCTION","text":"A significant increase of 61.6 % in new cases of head and neck cancer (HNC) worldwide is projected by 2050. Multimodal treatments for HNC often result in debilitating oral side effects like oral mucositis (OM). Photobiomodulation (PBM) therapy shows promise in managing these side effects, yet standardized protocols are lacking. This randomized clinical trial aims to evaluate PBM's effectiveness in preventing and treating oral side effects and related symptoms in HNC patients."},{"label":"METHODS","text":"We compared PBM with standard treatment in HNC patients at a single centre. PBM efficacy was evaluated on OM, pain, dysgeusia, hyposalivation, dry mouth, and trismus. Additionally, we controlled for analgesic use and fungal infection presence. The study adhered to the CONSORT checklist, is registered on the ClinicalTrials platform, and statistical analysis was performed using SPSS."},{"label":"RESULTS","text":"The study included 53 patients. The PBM group experienced a significant reduction in OM progression, better salivary function preservation, and lower severity of pain and dysgeusia by the end of treatment. However, no significant differences were found between the PBM and standard treatment groups regarding xerostomia, trismus, analgesic use, or oral candidiasis incidence."},{"label":"CONCLUSION","text":"PBM showed effectiveness in delaying onset and reducing the severity of oral mucositis and hyposalivation, as well as alleviating pain and dysgeusia at critical moments. However, it had no significant impact on xerostomia, trismus, analgesic use, or oral candidiasis."}],"keywords":["Dysgeusia","Hyposalivation","Oral mucositis","Photobiomodulation therapy","Supportive cancer care","Toxicity"]},{"pmid":"40217880","title_fr":"Étude pilote comparant PBM intrabuccale et transcutanée pour la mucite en ORL","type":"protocol","level":0,"pop":"adulte","ctx":["ORL"],"aim":"les deux","mode":["intrabuccal","extrabuccal","LED"],"nm":[650],"n":null,"effect":"—","theme":"Extra vs intrabuccal","l1":"Protocole français d'essai pilote randomisé comparant un dispositif intrabuccal (CareMin650) et un dispositif transcutané (ATP38) pendant la radiothérapie ORL.","l2":"Étude monocentrique (NCT06458517) dans les cancers de la cavité buccale et de l'oropharynx. Critères : incidence et sévérité de la mucite, interruptions de traitement, douleur et qualité de vie.\n\nPas encore de résultats. Utile car elle porte sur deux dispositifs LED disponibles en France.","l3":{"points":["Dispositifs : CareMin650 (intrabuccal) contre ATP38 (transcutané)."],"limites":["Protocole sans résultats."],"pratique":"À suivre pour le choix du matériel en France."},"topic":"mucite-orale","title":"A Pilot Study Comparing Intraoral and Transcutaneous Photobiomodulation for Oral Mucositis in Head and Neck Cancer Patients Undergoing Radiotherapy or Chemoradiotherapy.","journal":"Journal of clinical medicine","journal_abbr":"J Clin Med","authors":["Eber J","Schohn A","Carinato H","et al."],"date":"2025-04-02","year":2025,"week":"2025-S14","doi":"10.3390/jcm14072430","pmcid":"PMC11989608","license":"cc by","oa":true,"pdf":"pdfs/40217880.pdf","cited_by":3,"pub_types":[],"abstract":[{"label":"","text":"Background: Photobiomodulation (PBM) therapy has shown potential in managing oral mucositis (OM), a frequent and painful side effect of radiotherapy or chemoradiotherapy in head and neck cancer patients. Although PBM is increasingly used in clinical settings, the optimal delivery method-transcutaneous or intraoral-remains undetermined. Methods: This prospective, single-center, randomized pilot study (clinicaltrials.gov NCT06458517) aims to compare the efficacy of transcutaneous versus intraoral PBM in preventing and managing OM in patients undergoing radiotherapy or chemoradiotherapy for cancers of the oral cavity or oropharynx. Participants will be randomized into two groups: one receiving intraoral PBM with the CareMin650™ device, and the other receiving transcutaneous PBM with the ATP38® device. Results: Primary and secondary outcomes will include the incidence and severity of OM, treatment interruptions, patient-reported pain levels, and quality of life, assessed using validated tools. Conclusions: This study will provide comparative data on two PBM modalities, contributing to the development of standardized PBM protocols in supportive oncology care and informing future multicenter trials aimed at improving patient outcomes during radiotherapy for head and neck cancer."}],"keywords":["drug therapy","head and neck neoplasms","low-level laser therapy","palliative care","radiotherapy","stomatitis"]},{"pmid":"40172371","title_fr":"Mécanismes de la PBM dans le traitement et la prévention de la mucite : revue systématique","type":"sr","level":3,"pop":"mixte","ctx":["mixte"],"aim":"les deux","mode":[],"nm":[],"n":null,"effect":"favorable","theme":"Mécanismes","l1":"Sept essais cliniques : la PBM diminue les cytokines pro-inflammatoires (IL-6, TNF-α), augmente les anti-inflammatoires (IL-4, IL-10) et la superoxyde dismutase.","l2":"Revue PRISMA (Medline, LILACS, SciELO, BBO) des essais des dix dernières années, avec RoB 2.0 et ROBINS-I. Les essais randomisés présentent un faible risque de biais.\n\nLe mécanisme proposé associe modulation inflammatoire, équilibre des espèces réactives de l'oxygène et expression de gènes de différenciation des kératinocytes.","l3":{"points":["355 études repérées, 7 incluses."],"limites":["Peu d'études, marqueurs différents d'une étude à l'autre."],"pratique":"Pas de conséquence pratique."},"topic":"mucite-orale","title":"Mechanisms of photobiomodulation therapy in treating and preventing antineoplastic-induced oral mucositis: a systematic review.","journal":"Acta cirurgica brasileira","journal_abbr":"Acta Cir Bras","authors":["Sales PVA","Godói IPD","Brito GAC","et al."],"date":"2025-03-31","year":2025,"week":"2025-S14","doi":"10.1590/acb403125","pmcid":"PMC11960598","license":"cc by","oa":true,"pdf":"pdfs/40172371.pdf","cited_by":6,"pub_types":["Systematic Review"],"abstract":[{"label":"PURPOSE","text":"To conduct a systematic review of the mechanisms of photobiomodulation therapy (PBMT) for treating or preventing oral mucositis (OM) caused by antineoplastic therapy."},{"label":"METHODS","text":"Following PRISMA 2020 guidelines, a search was conducted in Medline, Latin American and Caribbean Health Sciences Literature (LILACS), Scientific Electronic Library Online (SciELO), and Bibliografia Brasileira de Odontologia from August to September 2023 using descriptors related to OM and laser therapy. Studies on the mechanisms of photobiomodulation in OM were included. Randomized (RCTs) or non-randomized trials from the past 10 years were reviewed. Risk of bias was assessed using RoB 2.0 and ROBINS-I tools."},{"label":"RESULTS","text":"A total of 355 studies was identified. After the screening, seven met the eligibility criteria. The RCTs showed a low risk of bias. PBMT reduced OM incidence in patients undergoing chemotherapy/radiotherapy. PBMT decreased pro-inflammatory cytokines (interleukin-6, tumor necrosis factor-α) and increased anti-inflammatory cytokines (interleukin-4, interleukin-10). It also modulated inflammatory mediators, enhancing the antioxidant enzyme superoxide dismutase and overexpressing genes for keratinocyte differentiation, aiding injury repair."},{"label":"CONCLUSION","text":"The findings suggested that the mechanism of action of PBMT in OM involves modulation of the inflammatory response, balancing oxygen reactive species generation, and expression of factors related to healing or repair. Further studies are needed to elucidate these mechanisms and optimize treatment protocols."}],"keywords":[]},{"pmid":"42238110","title_fr":"La thérapie par photobiomodulation (revue de l'American Dental Association)","type":"review","level":5,"pop":"mixte","ctx":["mixte"],"aim":"les deux","mode":[],"nm":[],"n":null,"effect":"favorable","theme":"Revue générale","l1":"Revue de référence de l'ADA (rapport technique n° 189) : terminologie, mécanismes, dispositifs, sécurité, réglementation et logique de prescription de la dose.","l2":"Revue narrative sur l'état de l'art : histoire, terminologie, mécanismes, matériel, sécurité, réglementation et politiques de santé. L'accent est mis sur les modèles de dose et sur la formation des praticiens aux interactions lumière-tissu.\n\nRéférence utile pour la formation des équipes.","l3":{"points":["Version courte : ADA Technical Report n° 189."],"limites":["Revue narrative."],"pratique":"Support de formation recommandé."},"topic":"mucite-orale","title":"Photobiomodulation therapy.","journal":"JADA foundational science","journal_abbr":"JADA Found Sci","authors":["Arany PR"],"date":"2025-03-26","year":2025,"week":"2025-S13","doi":"10.1016/j.jfscie.2025.100045","pmcid":"PMC13229060","license":"cc by","oa":true,"pdf":"pdfs/42238110.pdf","cited_by":14,"pub_types":["Review"],"abstract":[{"label":"OBJECTIVES","text":"The use of low doses of photons (light) in biological tissues to modulate (inhibit or stimulate) responses is termed photobiomodulation (PBM). This nonthermal, noninvasive, and nonsurgical light treatment has been reported to reduce pain or inflammation, modulate immune responses, and promote tissue healing and regeneration. These fundamental pathophysiological responses underlie several oral and dental diseases, highlighting the broad scope of PBM interventions such as alleviating pain, discomfort, and swelling postsurgical procedures, including third-molar extractions, managing oncotherapy-associated mucositis and temporomandibular joint disorders, and promoting accelerated orthodontic tooth movements and implant osseointegration."},{"label":"SEARCH STRATEGY CITATION SOURCES AND DATA ELEMENTS","text":"This narrative review provides the state-of-the-art in the PBM field, including history, terminology, mechanisms, devices, safety, regulations, and policy. The primary emphasis of this work is to outline the advances in mechanistic insights and clinical dosing paradigms that enable the safe and effective use of this therapy."},{"label":"OVERALL CONCLUSIONS","text":"The importance of fundamental PBM education and training concepts focusing on light-tissue interactions, target tissue composition, evoked therapeutic biological responses, clinical diagnosis, and rationalized dose prescriptions is emphasized. Furthermore, several issues and logistical concerns should be addressed to enable the routine use of this innovative nonpharmacological treatment. A succinct version of this article is available as the American Dental Association Technical Report no. 189 Standards Committee on Dental Products Working Group 6.58."}],"keywords":["Photobiomodulation","TGF-β1","low-level light/laser therapy"]},{"pmid":"40881977","title_fr":"PBM préventive et auto-administrée contre la mucite : cas à haut risque","type":"cases","level":4,"pop":"adulte","ctx":["ORL"],"aim":"prévention","mode":["laser","domicile"],"nm":[600,904],"n":1,"effect":"favorable","theme":"PBM à domicile","l1":"Un patient sous chimioradiothérapie pour un cancer du nasopharynx, traité par laser 904 nm en séance et par un laser portable auto-appliqué le week-end, n'a eu qu'une mucite légère à modérée, sans opioïdes ni gastrostomie.","l2":"Cas australien : cisplatine hebdomadaire et 70 Gy. Protocole « lymphatique » 904 nm (3 J/point, 200 mW). Les scores se dégradant le lundi, un laser portable a été ajouté pour le week-end à partir de J21.\n\nRésultats : OMAS 6/45, OHIP-14 19/56, ni antalgiques réguliers ni sonde. Un seul cas, avec un intérêt commercial possible (appareil de marque).","l3":{"points":["OMAS 6/45 ; PROMS 28/100 ; OHIP-14 19/56."],"limites":["Cas unique."],"pratique":"Idée à retenir : la pause du week-end peut faire rebondir la mucite."},"topic":"mucite-orale","title":"Preemptive, Self-Applied Photobiomodulation in the Treatment of Oral Mucositis: A High-Risk Case Study.","journal":"Case reports in oncology","journal_abbr":"Case Rep Oncol","authors":["Ryan J","Chong LN","Dunn RJ"],"date":"2025-03-10","year":2025,"week":"2025-S11","doi":"10.1159/000541966","pmcid":"PMC11893146","license":"cc by-nc","oa":true,"pdf":"pdfs/40881977.pdf","cited_by":1,"pub_types":["Case Reports"],"abstract":[{"label":"INTRODUCTION","text":"Oral mucositis (OM) is a prevalent and debilitating complication of antineoplastic therapy and hematopoietic stem cell transplantation. Effective OM treatment has important economic, quality of life (QoL), and healthcare implications. Photobiomodulation (PBM) emerges as an innovative addition to the treatment paradigm, using red and near-infrared light to relieve pain and stimulate tissue repair without significant toxicity. Although the role of PBM is well established in international guidelines, optimal timing and delivery of therapy remain unclear. Several studies have reported encouraging data, advocating for a preemptive and self-applied approach to PBM."},{"label":"CASE PRESENTATION","text":"We describe a high-risk case of locally advanced (T2N1M0, stage II) nasopharyngeal squamous cell carcinoma. The patient was treated with primary chemoradiation consisting of weekly cisplatin and a radiation dose of 70 Gy in 35 fractions to the nasopharynx, lymph nodes, and surrounding soft tissues. As per the 904 nm laser lymphatic protocol, 3J/point was delivered over 30 s at an output power of 200 mW in the continuous mode, targeting the tongue, gingiva, buccal mucosa, and hard palate. Various clinical outcome measures were documented including self-reported health status (PROMS), oral health-related quality of life (OHIP-14), and objective OM severity (OMAS). Owing to raised PROMS and OHIP-14 scores on Mondays reflecting symptom exacerbation over the weekend, the study protocol was adjusted at day 21 to include a portable SYMBYX 600 nm laser for the patient to self-apply over the weekend. In contrast to expected outcomes for concurrent chemoradiation, the patient developed only mild to moderate OM: PROMS (28/100), OHIP-14 (19/56), and OMAS (6/45). Further, the patient did not require regular analgesia, opioid analgesics, or gastrostomy tube placement."},{"label":"CONCLUSION","text":"Preemptive PBM using the 904 nm laser lymphatic protocol, supplemented by a self-applied SYMBYX 600 nm laser to facilitate continued treatment over the weekends, shows promise as an effective therapy for reducing OM severity in high-risk patients. Further studies are required to clarify optimal timing and delivery of PBM within the OM treatment paradigm."}],"keywords":["Case report","Head and neck cancer","Oral mucositis","Photobiomodulation","Stomatitis"]},{"pmid":"39910516","title_fr":"Préconditionnement par PBM pour prévenir la mucite et améliorer la qualité de vie sous chimiothérapie : essai randomisé","type":"rct","level":2,"pop":"adulte","ctx":["chimio"],"aim":"prévention","mode":["intrabuccal","extrabuccal","laser"],"nm":[650,980],"n":45,"effect":"favorable","theme":"Efficacité vs soins standards","metric":{"kind":"rates","outcome":"Mucite à 2 semaines","a_label":"PBM intrabuccale","a":20,"b_label":"Conseils seuls","b":93.3},"l1":"Une PBM débutée avant la première chimiothérapie évite la mucite chez 80 à 87 % des patients à 2 semaines, contre 7 % avec les seuls conseils d'hygiène ; la qualité de vie est meilleure.","l2":"Essai syrien en double aveugle à trois groupes de 15 patients : conseils seuls, + PBM intrabuccale 650 nm, + PBM intrabuccale 650 nm et extrabuccale 980 nm. Patients recevant une chimiothérapie seule pour la première fois.\n\nÀ 2 semaines, 93,3 % des témoins avaient une mucite contre 20 % et 13,3 % dans les groupes laser, avec seulement de l'érythème. Les différences d'ulcération (13,3 % contre 0 %) ne sont pas significatives. OHIP-14 et LENT-SOMA sont meilleurs sous PBM.","l3":{"points":["Enregistrement ISRCTN70634383.","Sans mucite à S2 : 80 % (IO) et 86,7 % (IO + EO) contre 6,7 % (témoin).","Ulcérations : 0 % contre 13,3 % (p = 0,129)."],"limites":["15 patients par groupe ; chimiothérapies variées.","Même population probable que l'essai sur le nitrite salivaire (PMID 40340568)."],"pratique":"La PBM avant la première cure de chimiothérapie est une stratégie simple et prometteuse."},"topic":"mucite-orale","title":"Photobiomodulation preconditioning for oral mucositis prevention and quality of life improvement in chemotherapy patients: a randomized clinical trial.","journal":"BMC oral health","journal_abbr":"BMC Oral Health","authors":["Khalil M","Hamadah O","Saifo M"],"date":"2025-02-05","year":2025,"week":"2025-S06","doi":"10.1186/s12903-025-05579-1","pmcid":"PMC11800611","license":"cc by-nc-nd","oa":true,"pdf":"pdfs/39910516.pdf","cited_by":7,"pub_types":["Randomized Controlled Trial","Comparative Study"],"abstract":[{"label":"BACKGROUND","text":"Given the suffering experienced by cancer patients, effective solutions must be found to prevent painful and debilitating side effects of anti-cancer treatment. This trial aims to study the effect of preconditioning with photobiomodulation in preventing oral mucositis and xerostomia in cancer patients undergoing chemotherapy alone for the first time, and to examine its role in improving patients' quality of life."},{"label":"MATERIALS AND METHODS","text":"This is a prospective, randomized, double-blind clinical trial including 45 patients divided into three age- and sex-matched groups. Group 1 received basic oral care instructions before undergoing chemotherapy. Group 2 received basic oral care instructions plus photobiomodulation using an intraoral 650 nm diode laser. Group 3 received basic oral care instructions plus photobiomodulation using a 650 nm diode laser intraorally and a 980 nm diode laser extraorally."},{"label":"RESULTS","text":"After one week of follow-up, 73.3% of patients in Group 2 and 80% in Group 3 did not develop oral mucositis. The remaining patients in both groups experienced only mild erythema. In contrast, all patients in Group 1 developed oral mucositis, ranging from mild erythema to ulceration greater than 3 cm². After two weeks of follow-up, 80% of patients in Group 2 and 86.7% in Group 3 remained free of oral mucositis. The remaining patients in both groups experienced only mild erythema. In contrast, 93.3% of patients in Group 1 developed oral mucositis, with cases ranging from mild erythema to ulcers larger than 3 cm². There were statistically significant differences between the three groups in oral mucositis assessment scale after one week and after two weeks (p < 0.001). Specifically, ulcerative mucositis was not observed in both laser groups, while it was observed in 13.3% of patients in Group 1. However, these differences were not statistically significant (p = 0.129). Regarding Late Effects in Normal Tissue (LENT-SOMA) scale, there was a statistically significant difference between the three groups studied (p = 0.037). There was also a statistically significant difference in the Oral Health Impact Profile (OHIP-14) between the three groups studied (p = 0.003 after one week, p = 0.023 after two weeks)."},{"label":"CONCLUSION","text":"Preconditioning before starting chemotherapy, whether with the intraoral red laser alone or in combination with the extraoral infrared laser, has shown significant results in preventing oral mucositis and dry mouth, and it has also played a major role in improving patients' quality of life."},{"label":"TRIAL REGISTRATION","text":"This trial was registered in ISRCTN registry under no ISRCTN70634383 ( https://doi.org/10.1186/ISRCTN70634383 ) on 24/07/2023."}],"keywords":["Oral mucositis","Photobiomodulation","Preconditioning","Quality of life","Xerostomia"]},{"pmid":"39889324","title_fr":"Efficacité de la PBM (laser basse énergie) pour traiter la mucite chimioradio-induite en ORL","type":"rct","level":2,"pop":"adulte","ctx":["ORL"],"aim":"traitement","mode":["laser"],"nm":[810],"n":36,"effect":"favorable","theme":"Traitement curatif","l1":"Chez 36 patients ORL avec mucite de grade 3–4, quatre jours de laser 810 nm soulagent mieux la douleur et améliorent plus la qualité de vie qu'un bain de bouche, avec un effet sur la sévérité limité à la première semaine.","l2":"Essai iranien en simple aveugle : laser portable 810 nm, 200 mW, 6 J/cm² pendant 4 jours (n = 18), contre bain de bouche diphénhydramine + hydroxyde d'aluminium-magnésium toutes les 6 h (n = 18).\n\nLa douleur baisse nettement sous laser. La sévérité est réduite à une semaine mais plus à un mois (p = 0,158).","l3":{"points":["Évaluations avant traitement, à 1 semaine et à 1 mois."],"limites":["Petit effectif ; simple aveugle ; comparateur actif peu standardisé."],"pratique":"La PBM curative soulage vite la mucite sévère ; l'effet sur la cicatrisation à long terme reste incertain."},"topic":"mucite-orale","title":"Effectiveness of Photobiomodulation (low-level laser therapy) on treatment of oral mucositis (OM) induced by chemoradiotherapy in head and neck cancer patients.","journal":"Journal of photochemistry and photobiology. B, Biology","journal_abbr":"J Photochem Photobiol B","authors":["Barati S","Motevasseli S","Saedi HS","et al."],"date":"2025-01-26","year":2025,"week":"2025-S04","doi":"10.1016/j.jphotobiol.2025.113115","pmcid":null,"license":null,"oa":false,"pdf":null,"cited_by":11,"pub_types":["Randomized Controlled Trial"],"abstract":[{"label":"PURPOSE","text":"Mucositis is a common and debilitating side effect of cancer treatment, causing significant pain and discomfort. This study aimed to evaluate the effectiveness of Photobiomodulation in treating mucositis induced by chemoradiotherapy in cancer patients."},{"label":"METHODS","text":"This randomized, single-blind clinical trial involved 36 patients with grade 3-4 oral mucositis. The test group (n = 18) received diode portable laser therapy (810 nm wavelength, 200 mW power, 6 J/cm2 energy density) for 4 days. The control group (n = 18) used a Diphenhydramine + Almgs mouthwash (50-50% mixture, 15 ml, every 6 h for 4 days). Lesions were assessed before treatment, one week after, and again at one month."},{"label":"RESULTS","text":"Pain scores in the laser group decreased significantly over time (P < 0.05). Patients in the laser group reported better quality of life and reduced oral discomfort compared to the control group (P < 0.05). The severity of mucositis significantly decreased in the laser group within the first week (P < 0.05), though no significant difference was observed at one month (P = 0.158)."},{"label":"CONCLUSION","text":"The study demonstrates that Photobiomodulation is a more effective and efficient method for reducing pain, tingling, and improving quality of life in both the short and long term. It also reduces the severity of oral mucositis in the short term for patients with head and neck cancer undergoing chemoradiotherapy."}],"keywords":["Drug therapy","Head and neck neoplasms","Low-level light therapy","Mucositis","Radiotherapy"]},{"pmid":"40002682","title_fr":"PBM préventive de la mucite chimio-induite : revue systématique d'essais randomisés","type":"sr","level":1,"pop":"mixte","ctx":["chimio"],"aim":"prévention","mode":[],"nm":[],"n":828,"effect":"favorable","theme":"Synthèse des preuves","l1":"Treize essais randomisés (828 patients sous chimiothérapie, sans radiothérapie) : 128 mucites dans les groupes PBM contre 211 dans les groupes témoins.","l2":"Revue PRISMA enregistrée (PROSPERO) sur sept bases, recherche en août 2024, outil JBI pour le biais. Patients de 1 à 84 ans, sans prédisposition liée au sexe ou à l'âge.\n\n41 % des patients ont eu une mucite au total ; 85 % des études ont un faible risque de biais. Le résumé ne donne pas d'estimation poolée.","l3":{"points":["13 ECR, 828 patients.","Mucite : 128 (PBM) contre 211 (témoins)."],"limites":["Pas de méta-analyse ; effectifs de chaque bras non précisés."],"pratique":"Soutient la PBM préventive en chimiothérapie seule."},"topic":"mucite-orale","title":"Preventive Photobiomodulation for Chemotherapy-Induced Oral Mucositis: A Systematic Review of Randomized Clinical Trials.","journal":"Biomedicines","journal_abbr":"Biomedicines","authors":["Parra-Rojas S","Velázquez-Cayón RT","Ciortan-Pop ME","et al."],"date":"2025-01-22","year":2025,"week":"2025-S04","doi":"10.3390/biomedicines13020268","pmcid":"PMC11852607","license":"cc by","oa":true,"pdf":"pdfs/40002682.pdf","cited_by":12,"pub_types":["Review"],"abstract":[{"label":"","text":"Background/Objectives: Oral mucositis (OM) is the most common acute complication among cancer patients. It initially manifests as an inflammatory process, beginning with erythema and edema of the oral mucosa, progressing to erosive lesions, and ultimately leading to highly painful ulcers. This systematic review seeks to evaluate the efficacy of preventive PBM protocols in mitigating chemotherapy-induced OM. Methods: The PRISMA guidelines were followed. The search was conducted in August 2024 in the following databases: Pubmed/Medline, Scopus, WoS, Cochrane, SciELO, BDTD, and BVS/IBECS. Only randomized clinical trials that utilized preventive photobiomodulation protocols in chemotherapy patients were included. All studies involving patients previously treated with radiation therapy were excluded. The Joanna Briggs Institute tool was employed for risk of bias analysis. Results: The total sample size consisted of 828 patients aged between 1 and 84 years. There was no predisposition based on gender or age. When the patients were evaluated under preventive protocols, some cases of mucositis manifested in a total of 339 cases. Of the total number of patients in the 13 selected studies (n = 828), 40.94% developed oral mucositis over the course of chemotherapy cycles. Comparing the experimental and control groups, 211 patients who did not receive preventive laser treatment developed oral mucositis; in contrast, only 128 in the experimental group did. Eighty-five percent of the studies exhibited a low risk of bias. Conclusions: Preventively applied photobiomodulation proves effective in minimizing or even preventing the manifestation of oral mucositis and reducing the severity of lesions that arise during oncological treatment. Registration PROSPERO (CRD42023465329)."}],"keywords":["chemotherapy","oral mucositis","photobiomodulation","preventive protocols","radiotherapy"]},{"pmid":"39799794","title_fr":"Stratégies de prise en charge de la mucite chimio- et radio-induite","type":"review","level":5,"pop":"mixte","ctx":["mixte"],"aim":"les deux","mode":[],"nm":[],"n":null,"effect":"favorable","theme":"Revue générale","l1":"Revue de Cancer Treatment Reviews : PBM et cryothérapie buccale sont les deux approches les plus fortement recommandées quand elles sont réalisables.","l2":"La revue fait le point sur les options nouvelles et repositionnées : mélatonine, clonidine, pentoxifylline, SAMITAL, IL-11, probiotique S. salivarius K12, curcuma. Les études de haut niveau des deux dernières années renforcent la PBM et la cryothérapie.\n\nLes auteurs rappellent de tenir compte de l'accessibilité et du rapport coût-efficacité.","l3":{"points":["Recommandations fortes : PBM, cryothérapie.","Options à valider : clonidine topique, mélatonine, pentoxifylline, SAMITAL, rhIL-11, SsK12, curcuma."],"limites":["Revue narrative."],"pratique":"Bonne carte de l'arsenal thérapeutique en 2025."},"topic":"mucite-orale","title":"Managing strategies of chemotherapy and radiotherapy-induced oral mucositis.","journal":"Cancer treatment reviews","journal_abbr":"Cancer Treat Rev","authors":["Wu Y","Shi W","Li C","et al."],"date":"2025-01-07","year":2025,"week":"2025-S02","doi":"10.1016/j.ctrv.2025.102883","pmcid":null,"license":null,"oa":false,"pdf":null,"cited_by":9,"pub_types":["Review"],"abstract":[{"label":"","text":"Radiotherapy and chemotherapy are widely employed as primary non-surgical cancer treatments; however, their non-selective cytotoxicity often leads to adverse events such as oral mucositis (OM), particularly in head and neck cancer therapies. International guidelines provide recommendations for managing chemoradiotherapy-induced OM in various clinical contexts. Subsequently, emerging researches have introduced evidence supporting novel approaches or existing regimens for OM prevention and treatment. The repurposing of established drugs has garnered significant interest due to its shorter development timeline, improved safety profiles, and lower costs compared to new drug development. For example, clinical trials assessing established drugs such as melatonin, clonidine, and pentoxifylline indicate promising potential for managing OM. Additionally, several emerging pharmacological interventions have demonstrated considerable efficacy; SAMITAL and rhIL-11 are supported by phase II clinical trials and prospective studies, while probiotics like Streptococcus salivarius K12 and curcumin have shown effectiveness in randomized clinical trials. Furthermore, recent high-level studies have reinforced the efficacy of non-pharmacological interventions, such as photobiomodulation (PBM) and cryotherapy, over the past two years. In all, given the evidence supporting different strategies, PBM and oral cryotherapy are highly recommended for managing OM when feasible. Topical clonidine, melatonin, oral pentoxifylline, topical SAMITAL or rhIL-11, oral SsK12, and curcumin may also be utilized but would benefit from validation in larger trials. Besides, Verbascoside, Palifermin, Amifostine, and Avasopasem manganese can be suggested for OM management, while the side effects should be monitored. The accessibility and cost/effectiveness of specific managing strategies of OM should be considered when selecting appropriate options."}],"keywords":[]},{"pmid":"39794168","title_fr":"Les preuves du laser basse énergie contre la mucite en radiothérapie ORL (éditorial)","type":"letter","level":0,"pop":"adulte","ctx":["ORL"],"aim":"—","mode":[],"nm":[],"n":null,"effect":"—","theme":"Correspondance","l1":"Éditorial de Clinical Oncology sur le niveau de preuve du laser basse énergie en radiothérapie ORL. Pas de résumé disponible.","l2":"Éditorial publié par la revue du Royal College of Radiologists. Le titre indique une mise au point sur les preuves en radiothérapie ORL, un sujet discuté au Royaume-Uni où la PBM est encore peu déployée.\n\nContenu non indexé sur PubMed.","l3":{"points":["Éditorial, sans données originales."],"limites":["Résumé indisponible."],"pratique":"—"},"topic":"mucite-orale","title":"The Evidence for Low-level Laser Therapy for Oral Mucositis in Head and Neck Cancer Radiotherapy.","journal":"Clinical oncology (Royal College of Radiologists (Great Britain))","journal_abbr":"Clin Oncol (R Coll Radiol)","authors":["O'Hara J","Iqbal MS"],"date":"2024-12-14","year":2024,"week":"2024-S50","doi":"10.1016/j.clon.2024.103731","pmcid":null,"license":null,"oa":false,"pdf":null,"cited_by":0,"pub_types":["Editorial"],"abstract":[],"keywords":[]},{"pmid":"39625051","title_fr":"Interventions pour prévenir la mucite chez les personnes traitées pour un cancer : photobiomodulation (protocole Cochrane)","type":"protocol","level":0,"pop":"mixte","ctx":["mixte"],"aim":"prévention","mode":[],"nm":[],"n":null,"effect":"—","theme":"Synthèse des preuves","key":true,"l1":"Protocole de la future revue Cochrane dédiée à la PBM pour prévenir la mucite (cancers ORL, autres tumeurs solides, hémopathies). Revue très attendue.","l2":"Cochrane annonce une revue spécifique à la PBM, qui détaillera ses effets selon le type de cancer et de traitement.\n\nLorsqu'elle paraîtra, elle deviendra probablement la référence méthodologique. À surveiller dans la veille.","l3":{"points":["Populations : ORL, tumeurs solides, hémopathies."],"limites":["Protocole sans résultats."],"pratique":"Surveiller la publication de la revue complète."},"topic":"mucite-orale","title":"Interventions for preventing oral mucositis in people receiving cancer treatment: photobiomodulation.","journal":"The Cochrane database of systematic reviews","journal_abbr":"Cochrane Database Syst Rev","authors":["Lewis SR","Riley P","Deligianni E","et al."],"date":"2024-12-03","year":2024,"week":"2024-S49","doi":"10.1002/14651858.CD016068","pmcid":"PMC11613434","license":null,"oa":false,"pdf":null,"cited_by":1,"pub_types":[],"abstract":[{"label":"","text":"This is a protocol for a Cochrane Review (intervention). The objectives are as follows: To assess the effects of photobiomodulation for the prevention of oral mucositis in people undergoing treatment for head and neck cancers, other solid cancers, and haematological cancers."}],"keywords":[]},{"pmid":"39615560","title_fr":"Thérapie photodynamique pour la mucite en cancérologie : revue systématique et méta-analyse","type":"ma","level":3,"pop":"mixte","ctx":["chimio"],"aim":"traitement","mode":["PDT"],"nm":[],"n":null,"effect":"favorable","theme":"Associations","l1":"Sept études : associer thérapie photodynamique et PBM accélère la cicatrisation et réduit la douleur mieux que la PBM seule (DMS −0,51).","l2":"Revue d'essais randomisés et non randomisés (2000–mai 2024). Méta-analyse sur 4 études : différence moyenne standardisée −0,51 (IC95 % −0,88 à −0,15), toutes les estimations allant dans le même sens.\n\nPiste curative, à confirmer sur des effectifs plus larges.","l3":{"points":["DMS −0,5138 (−0,8799 à −0,1477), k = 4."],"limites":["Études non randomisées incluses ; petit nombre d'études."],"pratique":"Combinaison PDT + PBM à envisager pour les mucites installées, dans un cadre de recherche."},"topic":"mucite-orale","title":"Photodynamic therapy for oral mucositis in cancer patients- a systematic review and meta-analysis.","journal":"Photodiagnosis and photodynamic therapy","journal_abbr":"Photodiagnosis Photodyn Ther","authors":["Joseph B","Mauramo M","Vijayakumary BK","et al."],"date":"2024-11-28","year":2024,"week":"2024-S48","doi":"10.1016/j.pdpdt.2024.104424","pmcid":null,"license":"cc by","oa":false,"pdf":null,"cited_by":4,"pub_types":["Meta-Analysis","Systematic Review"],"abstract":[{"label":"AIM","text":"This systematic review aimed to evaluate the efficacy of photodynamic therapy (PDT) in reducing the severity of chemotherapy-induced oral mucositis, and associated symptoms in cancer patients compared to standard care or other interventions."},{"label":"MATERIALS AND METHODS","text":"A comprehensive literature search identified seven relevant studies (randomized controlled trials and non-randomized clinical trials) published between January 1, 2000, and May 31, 2024. The data analysis was carried out using the standardized mean difference as the outcome measure. A random-effects model was fitted to the data. The rank correlation test and the regression test, using the standard error of the observed outcomes as predictors, were used to check for funnel plot asymmetry."},{"label":"RESULTS","text":"Studies combining PDT with photobiomodulation therapy (PBMT) consistently showed superior therapeutic outcomes compared to PBMT alone, particularly in accelerating healing and reducing pain. In the meta-analysis (k = 4), the observed standardized mean differences ranged from -0.9605 to -0.0223, with most estimates being negative (100 %). The estimated average standardized mean difference based on the random-effects model was -0.5138 (95% CI: -0.8799 to -0.1477)."},{"label":"CONCLUSION","text":"PDT, especially when combined with PBMT, shows promise in reducing the severity of oral mucositis and associated symptoms in cancer patients."}],"keywords":["Antimicrobial photodynamic therapy","Cancer therapy","Meta-analysis","Oral mucositis","Photodynamic therapy","Systematic review"]},{"pmid":"39926956","title_fr":"Revue générale du laser basse énergie en odontologie","type":"review","level":5,"pop":"mixte","ctx":["mixte"],"aim":"les deux","mode":[],"nm":[],"n":null,"effect":"favorable","theme":"Revue générale","l1":"Panorama des usages du laser basse énergie en odontologie, dont la mucite. Sans méthode systématique.","l2":"Revue courte des effets anti-inflammatoires, antalgiques et biostimulants : douleur postopératoire, cicatrisation, troubles temporo-mandibulaires, mucite, orthodontie.\n\nPas de données nouvelles.","l3":{"points":["Revue pédagogique."],"limites":["Méthode non décrite."],"pratique":"—"},"topic":"mucite-orale","title":"Comprehensive Review on Low-Level Laser Therapy in Dentistry.","journal":"Journal of pharmacy & bioallied sciences","journal_abbr":"J Pharm Bioallied Sci","authors":["Singh S","Chakraborty A","Saju AR","et al."],"date":"2024-11-27","year":2024,"week":"2024-S48","doi":"10.4103/jpbs.jpbs_1261_24","pmcid":"PMC11805236","license":"cc by-nc-sa","oa":true,"pdf":"pdfs/39926956.pdf","cited_by":2,"pub_types":["Review"],"abstract":[{"label":"","text":"Low-level laser therapy (LLLT) has broad applications in dentistry, enhancing patient outcomes through its anti-inflammatory, analgesic, and biostimulatory effects. It is used for reducing pain and swelling, accelerating wound healing, and promoting tissue regeneration. LLLT is effective in managing conditions, such as temporomandibular disorders (TMDs), oral mucositis, and postoperative discomfort. Additionally, it aids in orthodontics by accelerating tooth movement and mitigating associated pain. This non-invasive treatment offers a safe, efficient, and clinically proven approach, integrating well into various dental procedures to improve overall patient care and recovery."}],"keywords":["Dentistry","LLLT","low-level laser therapy"]},{"pmid":"39523254","title_fr":"PBM et mucite chez l'enfant : revue systématique","type":"sr","level":3,"pop":"pédiatrie","ctx":["mixte"],"aim":"les deux","mode":[],"nm":[],"n":null,"effect":"favorable","theme":"Synthèse des preuves","l1":"Vingt études pédiatriques : aucun effet indésirable et une seule étude sans bénéfice ; la PBM soulage la douleur et accélère la cicatrisation.","l2":"Revue enregistrée (PROSPERO) sur PubMed, Embase, Cochrane et la littérature grise. L'effet antalgique est le plus constant ; la réduction de la sévérité, de la durée voire de l'incidence est aussi rapportée.\n\nL'effet préventif et les protocoles optimaux restent à préciser par des études de meilleure qualité.","l3":{"points":["20 études, designs variés."],"limites":["Qualité des études hétérogène, pas de méta-analyse."],"pratique":"Encourage l'usage de la PBM en pédiatrie, surtout pour la douleur."},"topic":"mucite-orale","title":"Photobiomodulation for oral mucositis management in pediatric patients: a systematic review.","journal":"Lasers in medical science","journal_abbr":"Lasers Med Sci","authors":["Calarga CC","Cotomácio CC","Simões A"],"date":"2024-11-10","year":2024,"week":"2024-S45","doi":"10.1007/s10103-024-04221-w","pmcid":null,"license":null,"oa":false,"pdf":null,"cited_by":3,"pub_types":["Systematic Review"],"abstract":[{"label":"","text":"This review aimed to determine whether photobiomodulation therapy (PBMT) is a safe and effective alternative for treating and preventing OM in children. The database search was carried out on PubMed, Embase, and Cochrane. Gray literature was also consulted through Google Scholar. Among 20 studies included, no adverse effect was reported, and only one study did not show any evidence of benefit from the use of PBMT. The analgesic effect of PBMT was emphasized and its action in accelerating the healing process and reducing the severity, duration and even incidence of OM was also noted. Evidence was found to encourage the use of PBMT to treat OM in pediatric patients. Higher quality studies must be developed in order to clarify the PBMT prophylactic effect and the best protocols for each clinical situation.The protocol of this review was registered on PROSPERO (CRD42023418109)."}],"keywords":["Child","Low-level light therapy","Neoplasms","Stomatitis","Systematic review"]},{"pmid":"39501907","title_fr":"PBM pour prévenir la mucite chimio-induite chez l'enfant atteint de leucémie aiguë myéloïde : essai randomisé en double aveugle","type":"rct","level":2,"pop":"pédiatrie","ctx":["chimio"],"aim":"prévention","mode":["laser"],"nm":[660],"n":42,"effect":"favorable","theme":"Efficacité vs placebo","l1":"Chez 42 enfants atteints de LAM (Le Caire), cinq jours de PBM à 660 nm réduisent l'incidence et la sévérité de la mucite à J12, J19 et J30 par rapport à la simulation.","l2":"Essai en double aveugle contre simulation, en plus du protocole préventif standard. Laser diode 660 nm, 10 s par point, J1 à J5 de la chimiothérapie. Mucite cotée OMS et NCI à J5, J12, J19 et J30.\n\nEffets importants (tailles d'effet 0,58 à 0,80) en faveur de la PBM à chaque temps.","l3":{"points":["Tailles d'effet : 0,796 (J12), 0,670 (J19), 0,576 (J30), p < 0,001."],"limites":["Monocentrique ; puissance annoncée (« 15 watt ») probablement erronée dans le résumé."],"pratique":"Soutient la PBM préventive en LAM pédiatrique."},"topic":"mucite-orale","title":"Photobiomodulation Therapy in the Prevention of Chemotherapy Induced Oral Mucositis in Children with Acute Myeloid Leukemia: A Randomized, Double-blind, Clinical trial.","journal":"Pediatric blood & cancer","journal_abbr":"Pediatr Blood Cancer","authors":["Elkady RE","Khedr R","Hassan M","et al."],"date":"2024-11-06","year":2024,"week":"2024-S45","doi":"10.1002/pbc.31400","pmcid":null,"license":null,"oa":false,"pdf":null,"cited_by":5,"pub_types":["Randomized Controlled Trial"],"abstract":[{"label":"BACKGROUND","text":"Many preventive modalities have been advocated to reduce the incidence and severity of chemotherapy-induced Oral Mucositis (OM). Photobiomodulation, also known as Low-Level laser. Therapy, has shown promising results. This clinical trial was performed to evaluate the effectiveness of Photobiomodulation (PBM) for the prevention of chemotherapy-induced oral mucositis (OM) in children with Acute Myeloid Leukemia (AML)."},{"label":"PATIENTS AND METHODS","text":"A double-blinded randomized clinical trial was performed, including forty-two children diagnosed with Acute Myeloid Leukemia (AML) admitted to receiving chemotherapy (CT) at Children's Cancer Hospital 57357, Cairo, Egypt. Patients were randomly assigned to either the control or test group. Both groups followed the standard preventive protocol for oral mucositis. In the control group I sham therapy, while in the Laser group I had Photobiomodulation sessions for five consecutive days once a day performed by Diode laser (Sirrolaser Blue ™, USA) 660nm, 15watt, 10 s. Laser sessions began on day 1 of chemotherapy and were repeated on days 2,3, 4, and 5. The response to both groups was evaluated according to the development of oral mucositis; graded using the classification criteria of the World Health Organization (WHO) and the National Cancer Institute scale (NCI - Common Terminology Criteria for Adverse Events, version 4.0). Incidence of oral mucositis was evaluated from baseline to 5, 12, 19, and 30 days after treatment."},{"label":"RESULTS","text":"A clinical significant difference between the laser and control group was observed. The laser group showed a higher prevalence of no oral mucositis with decreased incidence of OM, while on day 12, the control group showed a higher prevalence of grades I and II (P-value <0.001, effect size = 0.796). However, the laser group showed higher prevalence of mucosa free of OM; on day 19, the control group showed higher rates of grades II and III, (P-value <0.001, effect size = 0.670). while on day 30 there was higher rates of grades I and II (P-value <0.001, effect size = 0.576) with higher rates of healthy oral mucosa in the laser group."},{"label":"CONCLUSION","text":"Photobiomodulation effectively reduces the incidence and severity of chemotherapy-induced oral mucositis in pediatric patients diagnosed with AML."}],"keywords":["Acute myeloid Leukemia","Low‐Level Laser Therapy","Oral mucositis","Pediatric Cancer","Photobiomodulation"]},{"pmid":"39491499","title_fr":"Laser haute puissance contre la mucite chimio-induite de l'enfant : réponse au Dr Morankar","type":"letter","level":0,"pop":"pédiatrie","ctx":["chimio"],"aim":"traitement","mode":["haute puissance"],"nm":[],"n":null,"effect":"—","theme":"Correspondance","l1":"Échange de correspondance sur un article évaluant le laser haute puissance en oncopédiatrie. Pas de résumé.","l2":"Fait partie d'une série de trois lettres (Dr Morankar, Dr Jin, correspondance de juin 2024) dans l'International Journal of Paediatric Dentistry, qui discutent la méthodologie d'un article sur la PBM haute puissance chez l'enfant.\n\nLe débat porte sur une modalité encore peu validée.","l3":{"points":["Correspondance."],"limites":["Contenu non indexé."],"pratique":"—"},"topic":"mucite-orale","title":"Evaluation of high-power laser therapy as treatment of chemotherapy-induced oral mucositis in paediatric patients with oncohematological diseases-Dr Morankar.","journal":"International journal of paediatric dentistry","journal_abbr":"Int J Paediatr Dent","authors":["Ludovichetti FS","Costa G","Signoriello AG","et al."],"date":"2024-11-03","year":2024,"week":"2024-S44","doi":"10.1111/ipd.13280","pmcid":null,"license":null,"oa":false,"pdf":null,"cited_by":0,"pub_types":["Letter"],"abstract":[],"keywords":[]},{"pmid":"39480546","title_fr":"PBM pour prévenir et traiter la mucite en greffe de cellules souches : évaluation de la qualité des soins","type":"cohort","level":4,"pop":"adulte","ctx":["CSH"],"aim":"les deux","mode":[],"nm":[],"n":35,"effect":"favorable","theme":"Implantation","l1":"Chez 35 greffés, des indicateurs qualité (SQUIRE 2.0) montrent 100 % de conformité du protocole PBM et des mucites surtout de grade 1.","l2":"PBM pendant le conditionnement puis à J1, J5 et J10 post-greffe. 66,6 % des patients ont eu une mucite à au moins un moment, majoritairement de grade 1 ; quatre patients (16,7 %) ont eu des douleurs.\n\nÉtude surtout utile pour sa méthode d'évaluation de la qualité d'un programme de PBM.","l3":{"points":["4 indicateurs de processus, 13 indicateurs de résultat."],"limites":["Sans témoin, petit effectif."],"pratique":"Modèle d'indicateurs pour auditer un programme de PBM."},"topic":"mucite-orale","title":"Photobiomodulation for the prevention and treatment of oral mucositis in patients submitted to hematopoietic stem cell transplantation: health quality evaluation.","journal":"Supportive care in cancer : official journal of the Multinational Association of Supportive Care in Cancer","journal_abbr":"Support Care Cancer","authors":["Marinho LCN","Freire GCB","de Araújo Silva DN","et al."],"date":"2024-10-31","year":2024,"week":"2024-S44","doi":"10.1007/s00520-024-08952-y","pmcid":null,"license":null,"oa":false,"pdf":null,"cited_by":2,"pub_types":[],"abstract":[{"label":"OBJECTIVE","text":"To evaluate the quality of oral health care through indicators in patients undergoing hematopoietic stem cell transplantation for the management of oral mucositis."},{"label":"METHODS","text":"Thirty-five patients were evaluated. Photobiomodulation was performed during the conditioning regimen, 1 day, 5 days, and 10 days after transplantation. Four process indicators and 13 outcome indicators were used to evaluate the effectiveness of the intervention, according to SQUIRE 2.0."},{"label":"RESULTS","text":"All process indicators demonstrated a compliance rate of 100% to the desired standard. Outcome indicators revealed that 66.6% of patients experienced mucositis during at least one follow-up period. A statistically significant increase was observed between periods of 1 and 5 days post-transplant, as well as between 1 and 10 days post-transplant (p < 0.05), with a predominance of grade I mucositis (p = 0.014). Four patients (16.7%) reported feeling pain, occurring between 5 and 10 days after transplantation, with moderate pain being the most prevalent. Oral mucositis did not show a statistically significant association with pain, associated treatments, leukopenia, comorbidities, or type of transplant."},{"label":"CONCLUSIONS","text":"The indicators demonstrated their suitability for evaluating oral health in both the prevention and treatment of oral mucositis in these patients. Furthermore, the effectiveness of photobiomodulation in improving the quality of oral health in the patients studied was confirmed."}],"keywords":["Health care quality assessment","Hematopoietic stem cell transplantation","Low-level light therapy","Oral mucositis","Photobiomodulation therapy","Quality of health care"]},{"pmid":"39482275","title_fr":"Laser haute puissance contre la mucite chimio-induite de l'enfant : réponse au Dr Jin","type":"letter","level":0,"pop":"pédiatrie","ctx":["chimio"],"aim":"traitement","mode":["haute puissance"],"nm":[],"n":null,"effect":"—","theme":"Correspondance","l1":"Suite de l'échange sur le laser haute puissance en oncopédiatrie. Pas de résumé.","l2":"Réponse des auteurs à une lettre critique. Voir aussi les lettres du 3 juin et du 3 novembre 2024.","l3":{"points":["Correspondance."],"limites":["Contenu non indexé."],"pratique":"—"},"topic":"mucite-orale","title":"Evaluating high power laser therapy (HPLT) as treatment for chemotherapy-induced oral mucositis in paediatric patients with oncohematological diseases- Dr Jin.","journal":"International journal of paediatric dentistry","journal_abbr":"Int J Paediatr Dent","authors":["Ludovichetti FS","Costa G","Signoriello AG","et al."],"date":"2024-10-31","year":2024,"week":"2024-S44","doi":"10.1111/ipd.13281","pmcid":null,"license":null,"oa":false,"pdf":null,"cited_by":0,"pub_types":["Letter"],"abstract":[],"keywords":[]},{"pmid":"39461860","title_fr":"PBM pour prévenir la mucite et l'altération fonctionnelle chez l'adulte greffé : protocole d'essai randomisé (POMFITT)","type":"protocol","level":0,"pop":"adulte","ctx":["CSH"],"aim":"prévention","mode":["laser"],"nm":[],"n":30,"effect":"—","theme":"Efficacité vs soins standards","l1":"Protocole de l'essai chilien POMFITT (30 greffés) ; les résultats sont parus en juillet 2026 (0 % de mucite sévère sous PBM contre 26,6 %).","l2":"Essai randomisé avec évaluateur et statisticien en aveugle, PBM trois fois par semaine du conditionnement à J+3. Critères : mucite (OMS), test de marche de 2 min, force de préhension, lever de chaise, FACT-BMT, acceptabilité.\n\nExemple de suivi d'un essai du protocole à la publication.","l3":{"points":["NCT06260111 ; résultats : PMID 42399564."],"limites":["Protocole."],"pratique":"Voir la fiche des résultats 2026."},"topic":"mucite-orale","title":"Photobiomodulation therapy to prevent oral mucositis and functional impairment in adult patients with haematological cancer undergoing haematopoietic stem cell transplantation: randomised trial protocol.","journal":"BMJ open","journal_abbr":"BMJ Open","authors":["López-Espinoza T","Sacomori C","Araya-Castro P","et al."],"date":"2024-10-26","year":2024,"week":"2024-S43","doi":"10.1136/bmjopen-2024-088073","pmcid":"PMC11529764","license":"cc by-nc","oa":true,"pdf":"pdfs/39461860.pdf","cited_by":2,"pub_types":["Clinical Trial Protocol"],"abstract":[{"label":"INTRODUCTION","text":"Oral mucositis is a highly prevalent condition in individuals treated for haematological neoplasms, primarily during haematopoietic stem cell transplantation (HSCT). The condition is known to delay recovery processes, increasing the risk of infection, the number of interventions and the length of hospital stays. The proposed Photobiomodulation Therapy for Oral Mucositis and Functional Impairment Transplantation Trial aims to assess the effectiveness and acceptability of using photobiomodulation in the oral cavity to prevent oral mucositis and functional impairment in adult patients undergoing HSCT."},{"label":"METHODS AND ANALYSIS","text":"This is an assessor-blinded and statistician-blinded, parallel-group randomised controlled clinical trial (photobiomodulation vs control group)."},{"label":"PARTICIPANTS AND SETTING","text":"30 patients, aged 18-65 years, with haematological neoplasms undergoing HSCT at the Clínica Dávila Oncology and Bone Marrow Transplant Unit."},{"label":"PRIMARY OUTCOME MEASURES","text":"oral mucositis will be assessed daily using the WHO grading scale, beginning on the day of transplant through day 20 post-transplant. Researchers will assess functional capacity using a 2 min step test, handgrip strength with the Jamar digital dynamometer, lower limb strength using a 30' sit-to-stand test and quality of life with the Functional Assessment of Cancer Therapy-Bone Marrow Transplantation Questionnaire. Acceptability will be assessed by logging treatment adherence and using a Visual Analogue Scale. Assessments will occur at two time points (1): on admission to the transplant unit, before starting the conditioning regimen and (2) on the day of discharge."},{"label":"INTERVENTION","text":"three times per week photobiomodulation therapy using a diode laser device will begin the first day of conditioning and continue through day 3 post-transplant."},{"label":"ETHICS AND DISSEMINATION","text":"The Clínica Dávila and Universidad del Desarrollo Clinical Research Ethics Committees approved this study in accordance with the Helsinki Declaration. Patients' informed consent will be required. The dissemination strategy includes publication in scientific journals as well as presentations in the media and at conferences."},{"label":"TRIAL REGISTRATION NUMBER","text":"NCT06260111."}],"keywords":["Laser Therapy","Physical Therapy Modalities","Transplant Medicine"]},{"pmid":"39422592","title_fr":"Domaines peu explorés de la PBM en oncologie buccale : analyse d'experts","type":"review","level":5,"pop":"mixte","ctx":["mixte"],"aim":"les deux","mode":["extrabuccal"],"nm":[],"n":null,"effect":"favorable","theme":"Nouvelles indications","l1":"Panel d'experts : résultats prometteurs pour la xérostomie, la dysgueusie, l'odynophagie, le trismus et la mucite (extrabuccale, pédiatrie), mais études petites et sécurité à surveiller.","l2":"Revue narrative où chaque thème est confié à deux spécialistes : justification, preuves, rapport bénéfice/risque, pistes de recherche.\n\nConclusion : impression globalement positive, mais il faut de grands essais multicentriques avant une généralisation.","l3":{"points":["Thèmes : mucite, xérostomie, dysgueusie, dysphagie, trismus/fibrose."],"limites":["Avis d'experts."],"pratique":"Feuille de route des indications émergentes."},"topic":"mucite-orale","title":"Underexplored Areas of Photobiomodulation in Oral Oncology: An Expert Analysis.","journal":"Photobiomodulation, photomedicine, and laser surgery","journal_abbr":"Photobiomodul Photomed Laser Surg","authors":["Gueiros LA","Gobbo M","Santos-Silva AR","et al."],"date":"2024-10-18","year":2024,"week":"2024-S42","doi":"10.1089/photob.2023.0155","pmcid":null,"license":null,"oa":false,"pdf":null,"cited_by":3,"pub_types":["Review"],"abstract":[{"label":"","text":"Objective: This study aimed to review the current body of literature on underexplored areas of photobiomodulation (PBM) for preventing and/or treating oral adverse events. Background: Recent studies suggest that PBM may offer potential benefits in managing cancer-related toxicities other than oral mucositis. Nevertheless, further research to establish conclusive evidence is still missing. Methods: A panel of specialists conducted a narrative review to evaluate the evidence on PBM therapy for oral mucositis, xerostomia, dysgeusia, dysphagia, and trismus/fibrosis. Each topic was reviewed by two specialists who discussed treatment rationale, summarized current evidence, evaluated risk/benefit ratio, and identified future research directions. Results: The current evidence suggests promising outcomes in nonroutine uses of PBM for xerostomia, dysgeusia, odynophagia, oral mucositis (extraoral PBM and the pediatric population), and trismus/fibrosis. However, the primary studies are often small and may have biases that require further evaluation, particularly regarding treatment safety. Conclusion: Despite the overall positive impression of PBM therapy for oral adverse events of cancer treatment, robust evidence from large multicentered studies is necessary to support its widespread clinical use."}],"keywords":["dysgeusia","odynophagia","oral mucositis","photobiomodulation","trismus","xerostomia"]},{"pmid":"39457676","title_fr":"Rapport coût-efficacité de la PBM pour prévenir et traiter la mucite : revue systématique","type":"sr","level":1,"pop":"adulte","ctx":["ORL"],"aim":"les deux","mode":[],"nm":[],"n":229,"effect":"favorable","theme":"Médico-économie","key":true,"l1":"Trois essais (229 patients ORL) : la PBM coûte un peu plus à court terme mais fait économiser à moyen et long terme en évitant les mucites sévères. Certitude GRADE modérée.","l2":"Revue PRISMA 2020 d'essais randomisés PBM contre placebo chez des patients ORL. Le surcoût initial est compensé par la réduction des complications : hospitalisations, nutrition, antalgiques.\n\nArgument utile pour convaincre une direction d'établissement, même si la base reste étroite (trois essais).","l3":{"points":["Certitude GRADE : modérée.","Enregistrement CRD42024498825."],"limites":["Trois essais seulement, contextes de coûts différents."],"pratique":"Argument médico-économique pour financer un programme de PBM."},"topic":"mucite-orale","title":"Assessing the Cost-Effectiveness of Photobiomodulation for Oral Mucositis Prevention and Treatment: A Systematic Review.","journal":"Biomedicines","journal_abbr":"Biomedicines","authors":["Parra-Rojas S","Cassol Spanemberg J","Del Mar Díaz-Robayna N","et al."],"date":"2024-10-16","year":2024,"week":"2024-S42","doi":"10.3390/biomedicines12102366","pmcid":"PMC11505555","license":"cc by","oa":true,"pdf":"pdfs/39457676.pdf","cited_by":9,"pub_types":["Review"],"abstract":[{"label":"BACKGROUND","text":"We report on the cost-effectiveness of photobiomodulation (PBM) for the prevention and treatment of oral mucositis (OM) derived from the cytotoxic effects of antineoplastic therapy."},{"label":"METHODS","text":"This review followed the PRISMA 2020 guidelines. A search was conducted in PubMed, Scopus, Web of Science, Embase, and OpenGrey. Articles published before 23 July 2024, were included. Randomized controlled trials (RCTs) that included patients with head and neck cancer undergoing chemotherapy and/or radiotherapy and a placebo group compared to an intervention group (PBM) were selected. The risk of bias was evaluated using the Joanna Briggs Institute tools. The certainty of the evidence was assessed using the Grading of Recommendations Assessment, Development and Evaluation (GRADE) approach and was rated as moderate."},{"label":"RESULTS","text":"A total of 3 RCTs and 229 patients were included. PBM may represent an additional cost in the short term, but the incremental expenses derived from the cytotoxic effects of antineoplastic therapy are greater in the medium-long term. The intervention group (PBM) showed a lower incidence of severe OM compared to the control group (placebo)."},{"label":"CONCLUSIONS","text":"PBM is a cost-effective long-term treatment, effective in preventing severe OM and improving the quality of life of cancer patients. More RCTs following the same standardized protocols are needed (registration CDR42024498825)."}],"keywords":["chemotherapy","head and neck cancer","oral mucositis","photobiomodulation","radiotherapy"]},{"pmid":"39670136","title_fr":"La PBM, une nouvelle ère pour des soins de support personnalisés en cancérologie","type":"review","level":5,"pop":"mixte","ctx":["mixte"],"aim":"les deux","mode":[],"nm":[],"n":null,"effect":"favorable","theme":"Revue générale","l1":"Courte revue narrative : la PBM est désormais recommandée en routine contre la mucite en ORL, en chimiothérapie et en greffe ; elle insiste sur l'harmonisation des doses.","l2":"Rappel des mécanismes moléculaires et des preuves cliniques, avec plaidoyer pour des schémas de dose harmonisés.\n\nSans données nouvelles.","l3":{"points":["Revue narrative."],"limites":["Vision plutôt promotionnelle."],"pratique":"—"},"topic":"mucite-orale","title":"Photobiomodulation therapy: Ushering in a new era in personalized supportive cancer care.","journal":"Journal of oral and maxillofacial pathology : JOMFP","journal_abbr":"J Oral Maxillofac Pathol","authors":["Varsani R","Khan M","Nannuri P","et al."],"date":"2024-10-15","year":2024,"week":"2024-S42","doi":"10.4103/jomfp.jomfp_193_24","pmcid":"PMC11633914","license":"cc by-nc-sa","oa":true,"pdf":"pdfs/39670136.pdf","cited_by":0,"pub_types":["Review"],"abstract":[{"label":"","text":"The human body can utilize light for a broad range of pathophysiological responses, such as circadian rhythm, Vitamin D metabolism, and vision, among others. The therapeutic use of light has spanned many ancient health practices. Recent advances in using low-dose light therapy, termed photobiomodulation (PBM), have made tremendous progress in unravelling precise biological mechanisms and clinical dosimetry, enabling this treatment modality's clinical safety and effectiveness. The evidence for PBM has received its strongest endorsement via recent systematic reviews and meta-analyses recommending its routine use to address various acute and chronic side effects associated with cancer treatment. Carefully done studies have noted unequivocal evidence demonstrating its effectiveness in managing oral mucositis in patients undergoing radiotherapy for head and neck cancer, chemotherapy, or hematopoietic stem cell transplantation. This brief narrative review will explore the therapeutic benefits of PBM therapy, supported by recent research findings, to provide a comprehensive understanding of its potential in clinical settings. Additionally, the review will highlight our current understanding of molecular mechanisms underlying PBM and the importance of novel harmonized dosing that enables its optimal clinical implementation and utilization."}],"keywords":["Cancer care","light therapy","photobiomodulation","photonic fluence"]},{"pmid":"39439950","title_fr":"Efficacité et sécurité de la PBM en traitement oncologique : revue","type":"sr","level":5,"pop":"—","ctx":["mixte"],"aim":"—","mode":["laser","LED"],"nm":[],"n":null,"effect":"mitigé","theme":"Sécurité oncologique","key":true,"l1":"Vingt-trois études surtout précliniques : la PBM peut tuer certaines cellules tumorales, mais la lumière rouge a aussi augmenté l'invasivité de cellules de cancer buccal. La sécurité dépend des paramètres.","l2":"Revue (PRISMA) de 14 études laser et 9 études LED. In vitro, le laser peut induire apoptose et chimiosensibilisation, mais certaines longueurs d'onde stimulent la prolifération in vitro tout en freinant la croissance des xénogreffes.\n\nLa lumière bleue freine mélanome et pancréas ; la lumière rouge LED a suscité une inquiétude sur l'invasivité du cancer buccal. Cliniquement, les deux modalités réduisent mucite, xérostomie et neuropathie.","l3":{"points":["Résultats discordants entre in vitro et in vivo.","Signal d'alerte : lumière rouge et invasivité du carcinome buccal."],"limites":["Majoritairement préclinique."],"pratique":"Garder des doses dans les fourchettes recommandées et informer les patients ; la question de la sécurité tumorale reste ouverte."},"topic":"mucite-orale","title":"Reviewing the efficiency of photobiomodulation therapy in oncological treatment.","journal":"Frontiers in oncology","journal_abbr":"Front Oncol","authors":["Luitel B","Duggisani T","Luitel A","et al."],"date":"2024-10-08","year":2024,"week":"2024-S41","doi":"10.3389/fonc.2024.1447653","pmcid":"PMC11493773","license":"cc by","oa":true,"pdf":"pdfs/39439950.pdf","cited_by":4,"pub_types":["Review"],"abstract":[{"label":"","text":"The aim of the present systematic review is to evaluate the effects of different photobiomodulation therapy (PBMT) approaches in oncological treatment practices. The review follows the PRISMA guidelines. Specifically, the review is composed of laser PBMT and LED PBMT. A total of 23 studies were included, 14 investigating laser PBMT and 9 examining LED PBMT. In vitro studies demonstrated laser PBMT's potential to induce apoptosis and cytotoxicity in various cancer cell lines while enhancing sensitivity to chemotherapeutics and natural compounds. However, some studies highlighted divergent effects between in vitro (promoted proliferation) and in vivo xenograft models (slowed tumor growth) for certain laser wavelengths. LED PBMT studies showed blue light inhibited melanoma and pancreatic cancer cell growth, potentially via ROS generation, while red light raised concerns about enhancing oral cancer invasiveness. Both modalities mitigated treatment side effects like oral mucositis, xerostomia, peripheral neuropathy, and improved quality of life. While promising, the outcomes varied based on light parameters, cancer type, and experimental setting, necessitating further optimization of PBMT protocols through well-designed studies to establish long-term safety and efficacy across clinical scenarios."}],"keywords":["low level light therapy (LLLT)","oncology","photobiomoduation","photobiomodulation therapy","radiation therapy"]},{"pmid":"39294629","title_fr":"PBM et affections buccales chimio- et radio-induites : revue parapluie","type":"umbrella","level":1,"pop":"mixte","ctx":["mixte"],"aim":"les deux","mode":[],"nm":[],"n":null,"effect":"mitigé","theme":"Synthèse des preuves","key":true,"l1":"21 revues systématiques et 40 méta-analyses : 87,5 % concluent à un effet significatif, mais les revues sont de qualité faible à très faible et une seule estimation atteint le niveau « fortement suggestif ».","l2":"Revue parapluie (PROSPERO) des revues publiées jusqu'en novembre 2023. Selon AMSTAR 2, 71 % des revues sont de qualité critiquement faible et 29 % de qualité faible.\n\nSur 40 estimations méta-analytiques, 33 sont significatives, mais une seule est classée « fortement suggestive » et les autres « faibles ». Le recouvrement des études entre revues est modéré. C'est le contrepoint critique indispensable aux méta-analyses très favorables.","l3":{"points":["21 revues ; AMSTAR 2 : 15 critiquement faibles, 6 faibles.","40 estimations : 33 significatives ; 1 « fortement suggestive ».","Recouvrement : CA 12,1 %, CCA 7,75 %."],"limites":["Analyse des revues existantes, sans nouvelle méta-analyse."],"pratique":"L'effet de la PBM est constant, mais sa démonstration repose sur des revues fragiles : d'où l'intérêt de grands essais et de la future revue Cochrane."},"topic":"mucite-orale","title":"Photobiomodulation therapy on chemo- and radiotherapy induced oral conditions: an umbrella review.","journal":"BMC oral health","journal_abbr":"BMC Oral Health","authors":["Rodrigues I","Machado V","Lopes LB","et al."],"date":"2024-09-18","year":2024,"week":"2024-S38","doi":"10.1186/s12903-024-04793-7","pmcid":"PMC11411753","license":"cc by-nc-nd","oa":true,"pdf":"pdfs/39294629.pdf","cited_by":5,"pub_types":["Systematic Review"],"abstract":[{"label":"OBJECTIVES","text":"Photobiomodulation (PBM) is a laser-based therapy used to promote tissue repair, reduce inflammation and pain, and has been extensively studied in chemo- and radiotherapy-induced oral mucositis (OM). This review examines the level of evidence of systematic reviews (SRs) that have investigated PBM in such cases of OM."},{"label":"MATERIALS AND METHODS","text":"SRs evaluating PBM for both the treatment and prevention of OM in patients undergoing chemotherapy and/or radiotherapy and published before November 30, 2023, on PubMed, Cochrane, Embase, Web of Science, LILACS, TRIP and Open Grey databases were eligible for inclusion. We assessed the level of methodological and meta-analytic procedures."},{"label":"RESULTS","text":"Of the 1201 SRs, 21 that met the inclusion criteria were included. The quality of evidence was assessed using the Assessing the Measurement Tool to Assess Systematic Reviews (AMSTAR2), and the majority was of critically low quality (n = 15, 71.4%) with only 28.5% of low quality. A total of 40 meta-analytic estimates were obtained and analyzed. Approximately 87.5% of the meta-analysis were significant (n = 33), but only one meta-analyses had a strength of \"highly suggestive\", while the rest were classified as \"weak\". When analyzing the overlap values, the covered area was 12.14% and the corrected covered area was 7.75%, indicating a moderate overlap. Only 4 SRs had a very high overlap and one had a high overlap."},{"label":"CONCLUSION","text":"The efficacy of PBM in the treatment of chemotherapy-induced OM is supported by low to critically low quality SRs and meta-analysis of low strength. This review highlights important areas that need to be addressed in future research on this topic."},{"label":"REGISTRATION","text":"CRD42023484013 (PROSPERO)."}],"keywords":["Chemotherapy","Oral mucositis","Photobiomodulation","Radiotherapy"]},{"pmid":"39223301","title_fr":"PBM associée au phytomédicament FITOPROT (Bidens pilosa et curcuma) contre la mucite radio-induite : essai randomisé","type":"rct","level":2,"pop":"adulte","ctx":["ORL"],"aim":"prévention","mode":["laser"],"nm":[660],"n":52,"effect":"neutre","theme":"Associations","l1":"Chez 52 patients ORL, ajouter le gel FITOPROT à la PBM (660 nm, 25 mW) ne réduit pas davantage la mucite que la PBM seule, malgré un meilleur équilibre des cytokines salivaires.","l2":"Essai brésilien : FITOPROT + PBM (n = 25) contre PBM seule (n = 27), avec programme préventif de soins buccaux pour tous. PBM quotidienne à 0,25 J par point.\n\nPas de différence de grade de mucite ; la dose de radiothérapie était le facteur déterminant. Les auteurs évoquent un effet sur le nitrite et les cytokines.","l3":{"points":["Enregistrement ReBEC RBR-9vddmr.","Cytokines mesurées : IL-1, TNF-α, IL-6, IL-8, IL-12p70, IL-10."],"limites":["Effectif modeste."],"pratique":"Pas d'intérêt démontré de l'ajout de FITOPROT."},"topic":"mucite-orale","title":"Oral mucositis management with photobiomodulation, Bidens pilosa L. (Asteraceae) and Curcuma longa L. (Zingiberaceae), the FITOPROT herbal medicine, and its influence on inflammatory cytokine levels: a randomized clinical trial.","journal":"Supportive care in cancer : official journal of the Multinational Association of Supportive Care in Cancer","journal_abbr":"Support Care Cancer","authors":["Pereira CH","Martins AFL","Morais MO","et al."],"date":"2024-09-02","year":2024,"week":"2024-S36","doi":"10.1007/s00520-024-08842-3","pmcid":null,"license":null,"oa":false,"pdf":null,"cited_by":4,"pub_types":["Randomized Controlled Trial"],"abstract":[{"label":"PURPOSE","text":"This randomized clinical trial aimed to compare the effects of a mucoadhesive formula, containing curcuminoids from Curcuma longa L. and glycerinated extract of Bidens pilosa L. (FITOPROT), associated with photobiomodulation (PBM), and of PBM exclusively, on the incidence of oral mucositis (OM)-induced by radiotherapy (RT) in the head and neck region, and the salivary expression of inflammatory cytokines, in patients with head neck cancer."},{"label":"METHODS","text":"Patients were randomly assigned into two intervention groups-FITOPROT + PBM (n = 25) or PBM (n = 27). PBM protocol comprised a wavelength of 660 nm, 25 mW, 0.25 J/point, and daily irradiation from the first until the last day of RT. FITOPROT was gargled twice a day. All patients underwent a preventive oral care program throughout the study. OM degree, salivary concentration of nitrite, and inflammatory (IL-1, TNFα, IL-6, IL-8, and IL-12p70), and anti-inflammatory (IL-10) cytokines were assessed at baseline, and at the 7th, 14th, 21st, and 30th RT sessions."},{"label":"RESULTS","text":"There were no differences in the OM degree between groups, but the RT dose significantly affected the OM. The RT significantly affected the salivary nitrite, TNFα, IL-1β, and IL-10 concentrations."},{"label":"CONCLUSION","text":"FITOPROT associated with PBM showed limited effects on preventing the incidence of severe OM compared to PBM alone. However, FITOPROT + PBM may be associated with nitrite and cytokine balance, which may contribute to the occurrence of fewer cases of severe OM."},{"label":"TRIAL REGISTRATION","text":"Brazilian Clinical Trials database (ReBEC; RBR-9vddmr), registered UTN code: U1111-1193-2066, registered in August 8th, 2017."}],"keywords":["Curcumin","FITOPROT","Head and neck cancer","Oral mucositis","Photobiomodulation"]},{"pmid":"39164917","title_fr":"Réponse : le laser haute puissance peut-il soulager la douleur de la mucite ?","type":"letter","level":0,"pop":"adulte","ctx":["mixte"],"aim":"traitement","mode":["haute puissance"],"nm":[],"n":null,"effect":"—","theme":"Correspondance","l1":"Réponse des auteurs à une lettre sur leur étude du laser haute puissance contre la mucite réfractaire. Pas de résumé.","l2":"Échange dans Oral Diseases autour de l'étude publiée en 2023–2024 (PMID 37203784). Voir aussi l'étude 2026 de la même équipe sur l'odynophagie.","l3":{"points":["Correspondance."],"limites":["Contenu non indexé."],"pratique":"—"},"topic":"mucite-orale","title":"Reply to: Can high-power laser photobiomodulation therapy alleviate the pain of oral mucositis?","journal":"Oral diseases","journal_abbr":"Oral Dis","authors":["Zadik Y","Finfter O","Cohen R","et al."],"date":"2024-08-20","year":2024,"week":"2024-S34","doi":"10.1111/odi.15115","pmcid":null,"license":null,"oa":false,"pdf":null,"cited_by":0,"pub_types":["Letter"],"abstract":[],"keywords":[]},{"pmid":"39164930","title_fr":"Le laser haute puissance peut-il soulager la douleur de la mucite réfractaire ?","type":"letter","level":0,"pop":"adulte","ctx":["mixte"],"aim":"traitement","mode":["haute puissance"],"nm":[],"n":null,"effect":"—","theme":"Correspondance","l1":"Lettre interrogeant les résultats de l'étude sur le laser haute puissance en mucite réfractaire. Pas de résumé.","l2":"Commentaire critique dans Oral Diseases. Le débat porte sur l'absence de groupe témoin et sur la sécurité des fortes puissances.","l3":{"points":["Correspondance."],"limites":["Contenu non indexé."],"pratique":"—"},"topic":"mucite-orale","title":"Can high-power lasers photobiomodulation therapy alleviate the pain of refractory oral mucositis?","journal":"Oral diseases","journal_abbr":"Oral Dis","authors":["Wang Q","Jin J"],"date":"2024-08-20","year":2024,"week":"2024-S34","doi":"10.1111/odi.15112","pmcid":null,"license":null,"oa":false,"pdf":null,"cited_by":0,"pub_types":["Letter"],"abstract":[],"keywords":[]},{"pmid":"39032055","title_fr":"Effet de différents paramètres de laser basse énergie, utilisés contre la mucite, sur des cellules de carcinome épidermoïde buccal","type":"preclinical","level":5,"pop":"—","ctx":[],"aim":"—","mode":["laser"],"nm":[660],"n":null,"effect":"—","theme":"Sécurité oncologique","l1":"In vitro, un laser 660 nm à 20 mW réduit la viabilité de cellules de cancer buccal ; à 40 et 80 mW, pas d'effet sur la viabilité. Plaide pour la dose la plus faible possible.","l2":"Cellules Cal-27 irradiées à 20, 40 et 80 mW (2 J/cm², 30 s). Viabilité par MTT, expression de Bax et Bcl-2 par RT-PCR.\n\nSeule la puissance de 20 mW réduit la viabilité ; Bax baisse à 40 et 80 mW, Bcl-2 ne change pas. Résultat rassurant à faible puissance.","l3":{"points":["Viabilité réduite à 20 mW (p = 0,001)."],"limites":["Une seule lignée cellulaire, in vitro."],"pratique":"Va dans le sens des recommandations de faible dose chez les patients avec tumeur en place."},"topic":"mucite-orale","title":"The effect of different parameters of low-level laser used in the treatment of oral mucositis, on the viability and apoptosis of oral squamous cell carcinoma cells: In vitro study.","journal":"Photochemistry and photobiology","journal_abbr":"Photochem Photobiol","authors":["Shirazian S","Mohseni A","Pourshahidi S","et al."],"date":"2024-07-19","year":2024,"week":"2024-S29","doi":"10.1111/php.13997","pmcid":null,"license":null,"oa":false,"pdf":null,"cited_by":1,"pub_types":[],"abstract":[{"label":"","text":"Oral mucositis is a complication of chemo/radiotherapy. To assess the impact of various power levels of diode-laser on the survival and expression of apoptosis-related genes in oral cancer cells, it is crucial to consider the potential existence of malignant cells within the treatment region and the reliance of laser effectiveness on its specific characteristics. Cal-27 cells were cultivated and exposed to a 660 nm-diode-laser at power levels of 20, 40, and 80 mW, alongside non-irradiated control cells. Viability and expression of Bax and Bcl-2 mRNA were assessed with Methyl Thiazolyl Tetrazolium (MTT) and Real-time Polymerase Chain Reaction (RT-PCR), respectively. The results were analyzed using one-way ANOVA and Tukey post-hoc test (p < 0.05). A significant reduction in viability was found only in the 20 mW group compared to controls (p = 0.001). Cell survival was significantly lower in cells receiving 20 mW laser than those treated with 40 and 80 mW (p < 0.05). None of the laser groups showed significant changes in BcL-2, but Bax was significantly lower in cells receiving 40 and 80 mW (p < 0.05), compared to controls. Laser irradiation at 660 nm (2 J/cm2, 30 s) significantly reduced the viability of oral cancer cells when using 20 mW power. These specifications align with the recommendation that the lowest possible laser dose should be applied for treating cancer patients. The exact mechanism of cell death following laser therapy with these specifications requires further investigation."}],"keywords":["apoptosis","low‐level laser","oral mucositis","photobiomodulation","squamous cell carcinoma"]},{"pmid":"39032372","title_fr":"PBM dépendante de la longueur d'onde : prolifération et angiogenèse du mélanome in vitro et in vivo","type":"preclinical","level":5,"pop":"—","ctx":[],"aim":"—","mode":["laser"],"nm":[405,532,635,808],"n":null,"effect":"défavorable","theme":"Sécurité oncologique","key":true,"l1":"Chez la souris, une PBM à 808 nm a favorisé la croissance et la vascularisation d'un mélanome (B16F10), contrairement à 405, 532 et 635 nm : un signal de sécurité à prendre au sérieux.","l2":"Quatre longueurs d'onde testées. À 808 nm, prolifération et migration augmentent in vitro ; in vivo, le volume tumoral augmente avec une angiogenèse accrue (VEGF, HIF-1α) et l'activation de voies de prolifération.\n\nCe modèle (mélanome murin) est éloigné de la muqueuse buccale, mais il rappelle que l'infrarouge n'est pas neutre sur des cellules tumorales.","l3":{"points":["808 nm : augmentation du volume tumoral relatif et surexpression de VEGF et HIF-1α."],"limites":["Mélanome murin ; doses non transposables directement."],"pratique":"Éviter d'irradier une tumeur en place ; respecter les recommandations de sécurité (voir Bulletin du Cancer 2024)."},"topic":"mucite-orale","title":"Wavelength-dependent photobiomodulation (PBM) for proliferation and angiogenesis of melanoma tumor in vitro and in vivo.","journal":"Journal of photochemistry and photobiology. B, Biology","journal_abbr":"J Photochem Photobiol B","authors":["Kang M","Lee Y","Lee Y","et al."],"date":"2024-07-19","year":2024,"week":"2024-S29","doi":"10.1016/j.jphotobiol.2024.112990","pmcid":null,"license":null,"oa":false,"pdf":null,"cited_by":5,"pub_types":[],"abstract":[{"label":"","text":"Photobiomodulation (PBM) has widely been used to effectively treat complications associated with cancer treatment, including oral mucositis, radiation dermatitis, and surgical wounds. However, the safety of PBM against cancer still needs to be validated as the effects of PBM on cancer cells and their mechanisms are unclear. The current study investigated the wavelength-dependent PBM effects by examining four different laser wavelengths (405, 532, 635, and 808 nm) on B16F10 melanoma tumor cells. In vitro tests showed that PBM with 808 nm promoted both proliferation and migration of B16F10 cells. In vivo results demonstrated that PBM with 808 nm significantly increased the relative tumor volume and promoted angiogenesis with overexpression of VEGF and HIF-1α. In addition, PBM induced the phosphorylation of factors closely related to cancer cell proliferation and tumor growth and upregulated the related gene expression. The current result showed that compared to the other wavelengths, 808 nm yielded a significant tumor-stimulating effect the malignant melanoma cancer. Further studies will investigate the in-depth molecular mechanism of PBM on tumor stimulation in order to warrant the safety of PBM for clinical cancer treatment."}],"keywords":["Malignant melanoma","Oncologic safety","Photobiomodulation","Tumorigenesis","Wavelength-dependent effect"]},{"pmid":"38992294","title_fr":"La PBM modifie-t-elle la fréquence des mucites sévères et des candidoses ? Analyse rétrospective en ORL","type":"cohort","level":4,"pop":"adulte","ctx":["ORL"],"aim":"prévention","mode":["intrabuccal","laser"],"nm":[660],"n":74,"effect":"mitigé","theme":"Facteurs de risque","l1":"Chez 74 patients ORL, la PBM n'a pas changé la fréquence globale des mucites ni des candidoses, mais a réduit les formes sévères ; les tumeurs de la cavité buccale font plus de mucites.","l2":"Dossiers 2016–2019 d'un service public brésilien. Protocole : 660 nm, 2 J par point en 3 s, 28 points (56 J), trois fois par semaine pendant 5 semaines.\n\nLes grades 1–2 dominaient, avec ou sans PBM. Les candidoses étaient plus fréquentes après la 12e séance. La localisation buccale et la limite d'irradiation des zones tumorales expliqueraient les moins bons résultats dans ces tumeurs.","l3":{"points":["Dosimétrie détaillée : 28 points, 2 J/point, 56 J/séance."],"limites":["Rétrospectif, petit effectif."],"pratique":"La PBM n'empêche pas les candidoses : continuer la surveillance fongique."},"topic":"mucite-orale","title":"Might photobiomodulation therapy interfere with the frequency of severe mucositis and oral candidiasis? A retrospective analysis in patients with head and neck carcinoma.","journal":"Supportive care in cancer : official journal of the Multinational Association of Supportive Care in Cancer","journal_abbr":"Support Care Cancer","authors":["Nascimento RB","Neri BP","Moutinho MM","et al."],"date":"2024-07-11","year":2024,"week":"2024-S28","doi":"10.1007/s00520-024-08724-8","pmcid":null,"license":null,"oa":false,"pdf":null,"cited_by":1,"pub_types":[],"abstract":[{"label":"PURPOSE","text":"Considering the tumor in the oral cavity or the oropharynx and nasopharynx region might be an aggravating factor for oral mucositis (OM) manifestation, the present study aimed to evaluate whether the location of the tumor and the use of photobiomodulation therapy (PBMT) might affect the frequency of oral candidiasis (OC) during radiotherapy (RT) and/or chemotherapy (CT) treatments."},{"label":"METHODS","text":"The medial records of seventy-four patients with head and neck cancer treated in a public service from 2016 to 2019 were evaluated. All these patients were submitted to RT in an accumulated dose of 48 to 70 Gy of radiation. Data about OM and OC were collected and presented according to the application of a therapeutic protocol with laser photobiomodulation (PBMT) to control oral mucositis, or not (No-PBM), and the location of tumor (head and neck or oral cavity). In the PBMT group patients, a low-power laser device composed of InGaAlP diode (maximum output power of 86.7 mW, active tip area of 0.1256 cm2, and continuous wavelength of 660 nm), was applied to the lips (three points each), right and left jugal mucosa (three points each), the limit between hard and soft palate (three points), buccal floor/sublingual gland (one point), lateral edge of the tongue (three points on each side), and back of the tongue (six points), three times weekly, for 5 weeks. The dosimetry used in each application was 2 J for 3 s, thus totaling 56 J. The correlation between clinical characteristics such as age, tumor size (T), metastatic lymph node (N), number of RT and CT sessions, candidiasis, and OM were analyzed."},{"label":"RESULTS","text":"Mucositis grades 1 and 2 were the most common among all patients, especially before the 12th radiotherapy session, regardless of the treatment with PBM (p > 0.05). Additionally, no difference in the grade of OM and OC was significantly observed when comparing the two laser therapy groups. OC was more frequent after the 12th radiotherapy session in all groups. Nonetheless, OM and OC had a different correlation regarding to tumor location (head and neck and oral cavity) being PBMT a positive therapy to delay OM. It was observed a positive and statistically significant correlation between tumors at oral cavity and OM, regardless PBMT (R = 0.84, p < 0.05 to PBMT and R = 0.13, p < 0.05 to No-PBM). Otherwise, OC was positively correlated to local metastasis in patients with oral tumors undergoing PBMT (R = 0.84, p < 0.05)."},{"label":"CONCLUSION","text":"Patients with oral cavity tumor presented more OM, especially high grades, then patients with tumors in other regions of the head and neck, which seems to be related to the irradiation parameters of radiotherapy and/or with the limitation of conduction of PBMT in tumor areas. OM and OC were not changed by PBMT, although it helped to reduce the incidence of severe cases of OM."}],"keywords":["Chemotherapy","Head and neck cancer","Oral candidiasis","Oral mucositis","Photobiomodulation therapy","Radiotherapy"]},{"pmid":"38944404","title_fr":"Survie à long terme des patients ORL ayant reçu une PBM contre la mucite","type":"cohort","level":4,"pop":"adulte","ctx":["ORL"],"aim":"les deux","mode":[],"nm":[],"n":751,"effect":"favorable","theme":"Sécurité oncologique","key":true,"l1":"Sur 751 patients ORL (2000–2016), ceux qui ont reçu une PBM ont vécu plus longtemps (médiane 1,66 contre 1,07 an). Rassurant pour la sécurité, mais très exposé aux biais.","l2":"Analyse rétrospective des dossiers d'un hôpital brésilien, PBM réalisée par un seul opérateur. Plus il y avait de séances, meilleure était la survie, notamment aux stades III–IV.\n\nCe lien s'explique très probablement par des biais : les patients qui vivent plus longtemps ont plus d'occasions de recevoir des séances (biais de temps immortel), et les plus fragiles arrêtent plus tôt. Le message utile est l'absence de signal de surmortalité.","l3":{"points":["Survie médiane : 1,07 an (sans PBM) contre 1,66 an (avec).","Stades III–IV, radiochimiothérapie, plus de 5 séances : 1,83 an."],"limites":["Biais de temps immortel et de sélection majeurs.","Rétrospectif, mono-opérateur."],"pratique":"Absence de signal de surmortalité ; ne pas en conclure que la PBM prolonge la survie."},"topic":"mucite-orale","title":"Long-term survival of cancer patients after photobiomodulation therapy for prevention and treatment of oral mucositis.","journal":"Photodiagnosis and photodynamic therapy","journal_abbr":"Photodiagnosis Photodyn Ther","authors":["Paiva DL","Oliveira VR","Bagnato VS","et al."],"date":"2024-06-27","year":2024,"week":"2024-S26","doi":"10.1016/j.pdpdt.2024.104248","pmcid":null,"license":"cc by-nc-nd","oa":false,"pdf":null,"cited_by":4,"pub_types":[],"abstract":[{"label":"BACKGROUND","text":"Photobiomodulation therapy (PBMT) has been employed for the prevention and treatment of oral mucositis in patients with head and neck cancer undergoing treatment. However, information about the survival of patients receiving PBMT is still lacking. Therefore, the aim of this study was to correlate the survival of patients who either received or did not receive PBMT, combined with cancer treatments, and to assess whether the number of PBMT sessions had a positive impact on survival."},{"label":"METHODS","text":"The records of 751 patients seen at Hospital de Caridade São Vicente de Paulo diagnosed with head and neck cancer, treated by D.L.P., the sole operator of PBMT, between 2000 and 2016, were analyzed. The median survival rates were compared by analyzing diagnostic staging, the combination of cancer treatments, and the number of PBMT sessions. Correlation analyses were performed to assess the interactions between the number of PBMT sessions and survival."},{"label":"RESULTS","text":"PBMT increased survival by 55.14 %, from 1.07 (Without PBMT) to 1.66 years (With PBMT) for the total population and more significantly in Group 41 (stages III and IV, radiation therapy/chemotherapy, with more than five PBMT sessions), the median survival value of 1.83 years, which represents a 66.36 % higher survival rate. In all the analyzed populations, the larger number of sessions was associated with better survival. In terms of survival, advanced stages III and IV almost equated with stages I and II in the absence of PBMT."},{"label":"CONCLUSIONS","text":"PBMT seems to interfere with the survival of patients with head and neck cancer, corroborating its recommendation for the treatment of mucositis, but further research is needed to investigate possible synergistic effects between PBMT and cancer treatments."}],"keywords":["Cancer treatments","Laser","Low level light","Oral medicine","Oral mucositis","Photobiomodulation therapy","Survival"]},{"pmid":"38840131","title_fr":"Interventions non médicamenteuses et nutrition, complications et qualité de vie en radiothérapie ORL : méta-analyse","type":"ma","level":1,"pop":"adulte","ctx":["ORL"],"aim":"prévention","mode":[],"nm":[],"n":2814,"effect":"favorable","theme":"Synthèse des preuves","metric":{"kind":"rr","outcome":"Mucite (toutes interventions non médicamenteuses)","rr":0.54,"lo":0.37,"hi":0.8,"k":27},"l1":"27 essais (2 814 patients ORL) : les interventions non médicamenteuses, dont le laser basse énergie, limitent la perte de poids et réduisent la mucite (RR 0,54).","l2":"Méta-analyse de cinq familles d'interventions : nutrition, exercice, rééducation de la déglutition, soutien psychologique et laser basse énergie. En sous-groupes, le laser contribue à limiter la perte de poids.\n\nL'estimation sur la mucite mélange plusieurs interventions et ne reflète pas la PBM seule.","l3":{"points":["Perte de poids : DM 1,66 kg ; mucite : RR 0,54 (0,37–0,80) ; dénutrition : RR 0,76."],"limites":["Estimation globale sur des interventions différentes."],"pratique":"La PBM aide aussi à préserver le statut nutritionnel."},"topic":"mucite-orale","title":"Effect of nonpharmacological interventions on nutrition status, complications and quality of life in head and neck cancer patients undergoing radiotherapy: A systematic review and meta-analysis.","journal":"International journal of nursing practice","journal_abbr":"Int J Nurs Pract","authors":["Jin X","Fan Y","Guo C","et al."],"date":"2024-06-05","year":2024,"week":"2024-S23","doi":"10.1111/ijn.13277","pmcid":null,"license":null,"oa":false,"pdf":null,"cited_by":1,"pub_types":["Systematic Review","Meta-Analysis","Research Support, N.I.H., Extramural"],"abstract":[{"label":"PURPOSE","text":"To evaluate the effect of nonpharmacological therapies on nutrition status, complications and quality of life in head and neck cancer patients and to provide a basis for clinical practice."},{"label":"METHODS","text":"This systematic review was reported in accordance with the Preferred Reporting Items for Systematic Review and Meta-analysis statement. Ten databases were systematically searched for all available articles from construction to November 2023. Two researchers independently conducted literature screening, data extraction and quality evaluation. Cochrane Review Manager 5.3 was used for meta-analysis."},{"label":"RESULTS","text":"Finally, 27 RCT studies including 2814 patients with head and neck cancer were included. Five categories of interventions were used: nutritional support, exercise, swallowing function training, psychological intervention and low-level laser therapy. Nonpharmacological interventions can improve body weight loss in patients with HNC at the end of treatment (MD: 1.66 kg; 95% CI: 0.80 to 2.51), and subgroup analysis showed that nutritional support, psychological intervention and low-level laser therapy were effective. Nonpharmacological interventions can also ameliorate decreases in BMI (MD: 0.71; 95% CI: 0.16 to 1.26) and reduce the incidence of malnutrition (RR: 0.76; 95% CI: 0.67 to 0.86), oral mucositis (RR: 0.54; 95% CI: 0.37 to 0.80) and gastrointestinal complications (RR: 0.61; 95% CI: 0.38 to 0.96) during radiotherapy; however, no significant differences were found in other complications and quality of life."},{"label":"CONCLUSION","text":"Nonpharmacological interventions can improve the nutrition status of patients with head and neck cancer and reduce the incidence of severe oral mucositis and gastrointestinal complications during radiotherapy but have no significant impact on quality of life."}],"keywords":["complications","head and neck cancer","meta‐analysis","nonpharmacological interventions","nutrition","quality of life","radiotherapy"]},{"pmid":"38831515","title_fr":"Correspondance sur l'évaluation du laser haute puissance contre la mucite de l'enfant","type":"letter","level":0,"pop":"pédiatrie","ctx":["chimio"],"aim":"traitement","mode":["haute puissance"],"nm":[],"n":null,"effect":"—","theme":"Correspondance","l1":"Première lettre du débat sur le laser haute puissance en oncopédiatrie (Int J Paediatr Dent). Pas de résumé.","l2":"Voir aussi les réponses des 31 octobre et 3 novembre 2024.","l3":{"points":["Correspondance."],"limites":["Contenu non indexé."],"pratique":"—"},"topic":"mucite-orale","title":"Correspondence to a recently published research article \"Evaluating high-power laser therapy (HPLT) as a treatment for chemotherapy-induced oral mucositis in paediatric patients with oncohematological diseases\".","journal":"International journal of paediatric dentistry","journal_abbr":"Int J Paediatr Dent","authors":["Monica M","Garima J","Rahul M","et al."],"date":"2024-06-03","year":2024,"week":"2024-S23","doi":"10.1111/ipd.13222","pmcid":null,"license":null,"oa":false,"pdf":null,"cited_by":0,"pub_types":["Letter"],"abstract":[],"keywords":[]},{"pmid":"38792366","title_fr":"Effet de la PBM sur les cytokines salivaires des patients ORL avec mucite : revue systématique","type":"sr","level":3,"pop":"adulte","ctx":["ORL"],"aim":"les deux","mode":["laser"],"nm":[660],"n":null,"effect":"favorable","theme":"Mécanismes","l1":"Quatre études brésiliennes (660 nm) : la PBM réduit la sévérité de la mucite ; l'IL-6 est la cytokine la plus liée à la mucite.","l2":"Première revue sur la PBM et les cytokines salivaires (recherche jusqu'en février 2024). Cytokines étudiées : IL-12p70, TNF-α, IL-6, IL-8, IL-10, CXCL8, IL-1β.\n\nRésultats variables selon les traitements anticancéreux et le recueil de salive.","l3":{"points":["4 études, toutes InGaAlP 660 nm."],"limites":["Très peu d'études, toutes d'un même pays."],"pratique":"L'IL-6 salivaire pourrait servir de biomarqueur dans les essais."},"topic":"mucite-orale","title":"Effect of Photobiomodulation on Salivary Cytokines in Head and Neck Cancer Patients with Oral Mucositis: A Systematic Review.","journal":"Journal of clinical medicine","journal_abbr":"J Clin Med","authors":["Khalil M","Hamadah O","Saifo M","et al."],"date":"2024-05-10","year":2024,"week":"2024-S19","doi":"10.3390/jcm13102822","pmcid":"PMC11122140","license":"cc by","oa":true,"pdf":"pdfs/38792366.pdf","cited_by":8,"pub_types":["Review"],"abstract":[{"label":"","text":"Background: Oral mucositis is a common and distressing side effect of head and neck oncology treatment. Photobiomodulation therapy can be utilized to prevent and treat oral mucositis. Its impact on salivary cytokines has yet to be thoroughly investigated. This is the first systematic review aiming to evaluate the effect of photobiomodulation on salivary cytokines in patients undergoing anticancer treatment. Methods: Numerous data resources, from the Web of Science, Embase, ScienceDirect, PubMed, Cochrane Library, and Scopus were sought. Articles published up until February 2024 were included if they met the following inclusion criteria: clinical trials reporting the effect on salivary cytokines in patients undergoing anticancer therapy. The methodological quality was assessed using several appraisal tools. Results: Four studies were deemed eligible for inclusion. All the studies were conducted in Brazil and used an InGaAlP diode laser with a wavelength of 660 nm. The included studies had a relatively low risk of bias. The head and neck cancer patients' salivary cytokines that were assessed by the studies, along with photobiomodulation therapy, included IL-12p70, TNF-α, IL-6, IL-8, IL-10, CXCL8, and IL-1β. The results varied among the studies. Conclusions: Our results show that photobiomodulation demonstrated positive results for reducing the severity of OM in all the included studies. Among the examined salivary cytokines, IL-6 is the most relevant cytokine for oral mucositis development and severity. A variation in the cytokine levels between the studies was noted due to differences in the type of anticancer treatment and saliva sampling."}],"keywords":["low-level laser","oral mucositis","photobiomodulation","salivary cytokines"]},{"pmid":"39051003","title_fr":"Effet in vitro du laser basse énergie sur des colonies de Candida albicans de patients irradiés","type":"preclinical","level":5,"pop":"—","ctx":["ORL"],"aim":"—","mode":["laser"],"nm":[976],"n":11,"effect":"neutre","theme":"Préclinique","l1":"In vitro, un laser 976 nm ne réduit pas significativement Candida albicans (−7 à −11 %), contrairement à la nystatine.","l2":"Onze souches isolées de la salive de patients ORL irradiés (plus de 30 Gy). Laser 976 nm à 5 ou 10 J/cm², 50 s.\n\nLa PBM seule n'est pas antifongique.","l3":{"points":["Réduction 7,01 % et 10,94 % (NS)."],"limites":["In vitro."],"pratique":"Ne pas compter sur la PBM pour traiter une candidose."},"topic":"mucite-orale","title":"In Vitro Effect of Low-level Laser Therapy on Candida albicans Colonies Isolated From Patients Undergoing Radiotherapy for Head and Neck Cancer.","journal":"Journal of lasers in medical sciences","journal_abbr":"J Lasers Med Sci","authors":["Hasanah NT","Sufiawati I","Kusumadjati A","et al."],"date":"2024-05-08","year":2024,"week":"2024-S19","doi":"10.34172/jlms.2024.11","pmcid":"PMC11267099","license":null,"oa":false,"pdf":null,"cited_by":4,"pub_types":[],"abstract":[{"label":"","text":"Introduction: Studies on head and neck cancer (HNC) patients undergoing radiotherapy have revealed increased numbers of Candida spp., leading to oral candidiasis and oral mucositis. The effects of laser therapy on Candida spp. have been studied with varied results. This study aimed to investigate the effect of low-level laser therapy (LLLT) on C. albicans colonies isolated from HNC patients undergoing radiotherapy. Methods: This study included a treated group, wherein 11 C. albicans isolates were obtained from the saliva of HNC patients undergoing radiotherapy at a dose exceeding 30 Gy. The control groups consisted of an untreated negative control and a positive control treated with nystatin. The treatment groups were subjected to LLLT in continuous mode for 50 seconds at a wavelength of 976 nm, 0.1 W at a dose of 5 J/cm2 for Laser 1 (L1) group and 0.2 W at a dose of 10 J/cm2 for Laser 2 (L2) group. The tests were conducted using C. albicans inoculum equivalent to a 0.5-McFarland suspension on 96-well plates. All test group inoculums were then cultured on Sabouraud dextrose agar (SDA), and the colony count was assessed at 104 CFU/mL. Results: LLLT at 5 and 10 J/ cm2 reduced C. albicans colonies by 7.01% and 10.94%, respectively, but the reductions were not statistically significant (P>0.05). Nystatin eliminated all the colonies. Conclusion: LLLT shows potential for reducing C. albicans colonies in HNC patients undergoing radiotherapy. However, further studies with varied parameters and methods are necessary."}],"keywords":["Candida","Head and neck cancer","Laser","Radiotherapy"]},{"pmid":"37203784","title_fr":"PBM laser haute puissance pour soulager immédiatement la douleur de la mucite réfractaire","type":"cases","level":4,"pop":"adulte","ctx":["mixte"],"aim":"traitement","mode":["intrabuccal","haute puissance"],"nm":[],"n":25,"effect":"favorable","theme":"Douleur","l1":"Chez 25 patients avec mucite résistante aux traitements de première ligne, une PBM haute puissance soulage immédiatement la douleur dans 94 % des séances (−60 à −72 %), pendant environ 6 jours.","l2":"Étude rétrospective israélienne : laser diode InGaAsP intrabuccal (1,4 W/cm²), douleur mesurée juste avant et après la séance. 16 patients sous chimiothérapie, 9 sous radiothérapie.\n\nLa douleur disparaît complètement dans 35 % des séances. Une sensation de brûlure transitoire a été rapportée une fois. Publiée en ligne en 2023, parue dans le numéro de 2024.","l3":{"points":["79 séances ; baisse immédiate dans 94 %, plus de 50 % de baisse dans 61 %.","Baisse moyenne : 4,8 ± 2,5 (chimiothérapie) et 4,5 ± 2,8 (radiothérapie) ; effet de 6,0 ± 5,1 jours."],"limites":["Rétrospectif, sans témoin."],"pratique":"Option de recours pour les douleurs réfractaires, dans un centre formé à la haute puissance."},"topic":"mucite-orale","title":"High-power laser photobiomodulation therapy for immediate pain relief of refractory oral mucositis.","journal":"Oral diseases","journal_abbr":"Oral Dis","authors":["Finfter O","Cohen R","Hanut A","et al."],"date":"2024-05-01","year":2024,"week":"2024-S18","doi":"10.1111/odi.14618","pmcid":null,"license":"cc by-nc-nd","oa":false,"pdf":null,"cited_by":7,"pub_types":["Research Support, Non-U.S. Gov't"],"abstract":[{"label":"PURPOSE","text":"We aimed to evaluate the efficacy of high-power (class IV) laser photobiomodulation (PBM) therapy for immediate pain relief due to oral mucositis (OM) refractory to recommended first-line therapy."},{"label":"METHODS","text":"This retrospective study included 25 cancer patients with refractory chemotherapy- or radiotherapy-induced OM (16 and 9 patients, respectively) that were treated for pain relief with an intraoral InGaAsP diode laser (power density = 1.4 W/cm2). Pain was self-assessed immediately before and after laser treatment using a 0-to-10 numeric rating scale ([NRS], \"0\" = no pain, \"10\" = intolerable pain)."},{"label":"RESULTS","text":"Patients reported an immediate decrease in pain following 94% (74 of 79) of the PBM sessions, in 61% (48 sessions) the pain reduction was over 50%, and in 35% (28 sessions) the initial pain was completely eliminated. There were no reports of increased pain post-PBM. For chemotherapy and radiotherapy-treated patients, mean reduction in pain NRS post-PBM was 4.8 ± 2.5 (p < 0.001) and 4.5 ± 2.8 (p = 0.001), indicating a post-PBM reduction of 72% and 60% of the initial pain level, respectively. The analgesic benefit of PBM remained for a mean of 6.0 ± 5.1 days. One patient reported a transient burning sensation after one PBM session."},{"label":"CONCLUSIONS","text":"High-power laser PBM may provide nonpharmacologic, patient-friendly, long-lasting, rapid pain relief for refractory OM."}],"keywords":["chemotherapy","low‐level laser","oral complications","oral ulcer","palliative therapy","radiotherapy","supportive treatment"]},{"pmid":"38688628","title_fr":"Prévention et traitement de la mucite chez l'enfant : méta-analyse d'essais randomisés","type":"ma","level":1,"pop":"pédiatrie","ctx":["mixte"],"aim":"les deux","mode":["laser"],"nm":[],"n":null,"effect":"neutre","theme":"Synthèse des preuves","key":true,"metric":{"kind":"rr","outcome":"Mucite grade ≥ 2 (prévention, enfant)","rr":0.99,"lo":null,"hi":null,"k":null},"l1":"Seule méta-analyse discordante de la période : chez l'enfant, le laser basse énergie ne réduit pas la mucite de grade ≥ 2 (RR 0,99), alors que le miel raccourcit l'hospitalisation.","l2":"Revue de 34 essais randomisés pédiatriques (MEDLINE et Scopus, 2000–2023) ; cinq essais méta-analysables pour le laser ou le miel.\n\nEn prévention, laser : RR 0,99 (p = 0,99) ; en curatif : RR 0,48 (p = 0,58), non significatif. Ce résultat repose sur très peu d'essais et contraste avec la méta-analyse pédiatrique de 2025 (J Dent) et avec les recommandations du Children's Oncology Group.","l3":{"points":["34 ECR repérés, 5 méta-analysés.","Laser préventif : RR 0,99 ; curatif : RR 0,48 (NS).","Miel : hospitalisation raccourcie (DM −4,33 jours)."],"limites":["Deux bases de données seulement ; très peu d'essais par comparaison ; IC non rapportés dans le résumé."],"pratique":"À connaître comme contrepoint ; l'ensemble des preuves pédiatriques reste plutôt favorable à la PBM."},"topic":"mucite-orale","title":"Prevention and Treatment of Oral Mucositis in Pediatric Patients: Systematic Review and Meta-Analysis of Randomized Controlled Trials.","journal":"In vivo (Athens, Greece)","journal_abbr":"In Vivo","authors":["Andriakopoulou CS","Yapijakis C","Koutelekos I","et al."],"date":"2024-04-30","year":2024,"week":"2024-S18","doi":"10.21873/invivo.13535","pmcid":"PMC11059879","license":null,"oa":false,"pdf":null,"cited_by":1,"pub_types":["Meta-Analysis","Systematic Review"],"abstract":[{"label":"BACKGROUND/AIM","text":"Oral mucositis (OM) is a common and serious side effect of cancer treatment. The incidence of chemotherapy-induced OM in pediatric patients can reach up to 91.5% and has a major impact on patients' quality of life. The aim of the study was to assess the efficacy of current interventions and agents for the management of OM in children undergoing chemo/radiotherapy or hematopoietic stem cell transplantation (HSCT)."},{"label":"MATERIALS AND METHODS","text":"A systematic search of randomized controlled trials (RCTs) was conducted in the MEDLINE and Scopus databases from January 2000 until March 2023. Thirty-four randomized studies meeting the inclusion criteria were identified and five RCTs investigating the efficacy of Low Level Laser Therapy (LLLT) intervention or the agent honey were included in the meta-analysis."},{"label":"RESULTS","text":"The meta-analysis of two RCTs indicated that topical application of honey on oral mucosa was effective in shortening the mean duration of hospital stay in children with severe OM (MD=-4.33, p=0.002). However, LLLT was not found to be effective for the prevention or treatment of OM grade ≥II (RR=0.99, p=0.99). Moreover, the therapeutic application of LLLT did not show significant benefit for lower risk of OM grade ≥II (RR=0.48, p=0.58)."},{"label":"CONCLUSION","text":"Various interventions and agents were examined in the present study for the management of OM. Honey could be a promising candidate for the treatment of OM in pediatric patients. Further high-quality RCTs are required to enhance our findings."}],"keywords":["Oral mucositis","cancer","children","meta-analysis","review"]},{"pmid":"38672630","title_fr":"Prise en charge de la mucite chez l'enfant sous traitement anticancéreux : revue systématique","type":"sr","level":3,"pop":"pédiatrie","ctx":["mixte"],"aim":"les deux","mode":[],"nm":[],"n":null,"effect":"favorable","theme":"Synthèse des preuves","l1":"39 articles pédiatriques : laser basse énergie, palifermine, miel et zinc réduisent incidence, durée, sévérité et douleur de la mucite, mais les preuves restent insuffisantes pour un protocole précis.","l2":"Revue de 2 655 articles avec comparaison des approches : cryothérapie, laser, composés naturels.\n\nConclusion prudente : plusieurs options efficaces, sans protocole clinique consensuel.","l3":{"points":["39 articles inclus."],"limites":["Pas de méta-analyse."],"pratique":"La PBM fait partie des options validées chez l'enfant."},"topic":"mucite-orale","title":"Oral Mucositis Management in Children under Cancer Treatment: A Systematic Review.","journal":"Cancers","journal_abbr":"Cancers (Basel)","authors":["Braguês R","Marvão MF","Correia P","et al."],"date":"2024-04-18","year":2024,"week":"2024-S16","doi":"10.3390/cancers16081548","pmcid":"PMC11048343","license":"cc by","oa":true,"pdf":"pdfs/38672630.pdf","cited_by":10,"pub_types":["Review"],"abstract":[{"label":"","text":"Children undergoing antineoplastic treatment often present severe side effects due to the dosage and duration of treatments, with oral mucositis emerging as one of the most prevalent and painful inflammatory conditions. There is a growing body of evidence on therapeutic interventions such as cryotherapy, low-level laser therapy, and natural compounds for this condition. The aim of this systematic review was to identify and compare therapies for the management of cancer treatment-induced oral mucositis in pediatric patients. From 2655 articles obtained in initial searches, 39 articles were considered in this systematic review, after applying inclusion/exclusion criteria. Low-level laser therapy, palifermin, honey, and zinc demonstrated reductions in oral mucositis incidence, duration, severity, and pain reported by the patient. Although there are several therapies in place for the prevention and treatment of oral mucositis in children, evidence of their efficacy is still inconclusive to establish accurate clinical protocols."}],"keywords":["calcium phosphate","honey","low-level laser therapy","oral mucositis","palifermin","pediatric cancer","prevention","therapeutics","zinc"]},{"pmid":"38619258","title_fr":"Soulagement immédiat de la douleur dans la mucite et les ulcérations buccales par PBM : le concept de traitement préemptif","type":"review","level":5,"pop":"mixte","ctx":["mixte"],"aim":"traitement","mode":["haute puissance"],"nm":[],"n":null,"effect":"favorable","theme":"Douleur","l1":"Revue cartographique : certains protocoles de PBM soulagent la douleur immédiatement. Les auteurs proposent une PBM « préemptive », débutée dès les premiers signes.","l2":"Revue PubMed : la plupart des essais rapportent un soulagement retardé, mais certains protocoles donnent un effet immédiat, proche de la disparition de la douleur.\n\nLe concept s'étend aux aphtes et à la GVH chronique. Même école que les travaux sur la PBM haute puissance (2023, 2026).","l3":{"points":["Concept : PBM préemptive, précoce et répétée pendant tout le traitement."],"limites":["Revue non systématique, une seule base."],"pratique":"Penser à l'effet antalgique immédiat comme critère à part entière."},"topic":"mucite-orale","title":"Immediate pain alleviation in oral mucositis and other oral ulcerative diseases through photobiomodulation therapy: the preemptive treatment concept.","journal":"Quintessence international (Berlin, Germany : 1985)","journal_abbr":"Quintessence Int","authors":["Elad S","Keegan R","Fregnani ER","et al."],"date":"2024-04-15","year":2024,"week":"2024-S16","doi":"10.3290/j.qi.b5213529","pmcid":null,"license":null,"oa":false,"pdf":null,"cited_by":8,"pub_types":["Review"],"abstract":[{"label":"OBJECTIVE","text":"Photobiomodulation therapy is recommended by multiple international societies for managing oral mucositis. These recommendations are based on extensive evidence. However, the search for an optimal photobiomodulation protocol continues. This mapping review focuses on a novel aspect of photobiomodulation therapy which is the immediate effect on pain levels associated with oral ulcerative conditions."},{"label":"DATA SOURCES","text":"This literature review systematically compiles and evaluates the evidence about oral mucositis, alongside other oral ulcerative conditions, as the protocols that achieved pain relief for these oral conditions may have potential applicability to oral mucositis management. The scientific database used was PubMed."},{"label":"CONCLUSION","text":"Whereas most of the randomized controlled trials about photobiomodulation therapy for oral mucositis and other ulcerative oral diseases reported delayed pain relief, certain photobiomodulation therapy protocols reported immediate pain relief. The results of this review highlight the concept of preemptive photobiomodulation therapy, in which photobiomodulation therapy is delivered early in the development of oral mucositis throughout the oncotherapy and may achieve immediate pain relief consistently in most of the patients and close to a negligible pain level. Photobiomodulation therapy, as a powerful nonpharmacologic tool for immediate pain relief, has a great beneficial value in patients suffering from oral mucositis and other painful oral ulcerative diseases such as recurrent aphthous stomatitis and chronic graft-versus-host disease."}],"keywords":["aphthous stomatitis","cancer","graft vs host disease","laser","oral mucositis","pain relief","photobiomodulation"]},{"pmid":"38615257","title_fr":"PBM quotidienne versus un jour sur trois pour prévenir la mucite pendant la radiochimiothérapie du cancer buccal : essai en triple aveugle","type":"rct","level":2,"pop":"adulte","ctx":["ORL"],"aim":"prévention","mode":["intrabuccal","laser"],"nm":[660],"n":16,"effect":"favorable","theme":"Dosimétrie & fréquence","key":true,"l1":"Chez 16 patients atteints de cancer buccal, espacer la PBM (un jour sur trois au lieu de chaque jour) augmente les mucites de grade 2–3, la douleur et la perte salivaire : la PBM quotidienne est supérieure.","l2":"Essai brésilien en triple aveugle : PBM quotidienne contre PBM tous les trois jours intercalée de simulations. Laser 660 nm, 0,1 W, 1 J par point, 9 points par zone, du début à la fin de la radiochimiothérapie.\n\nLe groupe espacé a plus de mucites de grade 2 et 3, plus de douleur et une baisse du débit salivaire plus marquée. Résultat à opposer à la pratique fréquente de trois séances par semaine.","l3":{"points":["Zones : lèvres, joues, bords et corps de langue, plancher.","Mesures quotidiennes : OMS, douleur, EVA ; poids, débit salivaire, OHIP-14, CAOD."],"limites":["Très petit effectif (8 par groupe)."],"pratique":"Quand c'est possible, préférer une PBM quotidienne pendant la radiochimiothérapie du cancer buccal."},"topic":"mucite-orale","title":"Comparison of a daily and alternate-day photobiomodulation protocol in the prevention of oral mucositis in patients undergoing radiochemotherapy for oral cancer: a triple-blind, controlled clinical trial.","journal":"Medicina oral, patologia oral y cirugia bucal","journal_abbr":"Med Oral Patol Oral Cir Bucal","authors":["Oliveira FM","Borges MM","Malta CE","et al."],"date":"2024-04-14","year":2024,"week":"2024-S15","doi":"10.4317/medoral.26436","pmcid":"PMC11175575","license":"cc by","oa":true,"pdf":"pdfs/38615257.pdf","cited_by":7,"pub_types":["Comparative Study","Randomized Controlled Trial"],"abstract":[{"label":"BACKGROUND","text":"Preventive Photobiomodulation Therapy (PBMT) significantly reduces oral mucositis (OM) severity in patients undergoing Radiochemotherapy (RCT) for the treatment of oral cancer, but daily applications generate cost, overload the dental team, and reduce the number of patients assisted.To evaluate the effectiveness of two PBMT protocols in preventing OM in patients undergoing RCT for oral cancer."},{"label":"MATERIAL AND METHODS","text":"16 patients diagnosed with oral cancer undergoing RCT were included, equally divided into two groups: a group treated daily with PBMT, and another group also submitted to daily treatment, however, performing the application of PBMT every three days, interspersed with a simulation of PBMT (placebo). A red laser was used (~660 nm), 0.1W power, 1J of energy applied per point, 9 points per area (labial mucosa, buccal mucosa, lateral borders of the tongue, body of the tongue, and floor of the mouth) from the beginning of RCT until the end of the oncological treatment. Daily assessments were performed regarding OM scores, the World Health Organization (WHO) pain scale, and the visual analog scale (VAS). Weight, salivary flow (SGAPP), OHIP-14, and DMFT were evaluated on the initial and final days of RT. OM incidence and clinical data were compared by Pearson's chi-square test or Fisher's exact test. Pain and other scale scores were compared using the Mann-Whitney and Friedman/Dunn tests (SPSS v20.0 p<0.05)."},{"label":"RESULTS","text":"In the group with PBMT on alternate days, there was an increase in the frequency of grade 2 and grade 3 oral mucositis and an increased risk of grade 2 oral mucositis, in addition to higher mean pain scores and greater reduction in salivary flow."},{"label":"CONCLUSIONS","text":"The daily PBMT protocol proved more effective in controlling the frequency and severity of OM, pain, and salivary flow."}],"keywords":[]},{"pmid":"38556719","title_fr":"Création d'une équipe de photothérapie pour administrer la PBM en greffe pédiatrique : protocole standardisé","type":"cohort","level":4,"pop":"pédiatrie","ctx":["CSH"],"aim":"les deux","mode":[],"nm":[],"n":62,"effect":"favorable","theme":"Implantation","key":true,"l1":"Dans un centre américain de greffe pédiatrique, une infirmière dédiée à la PBM a rendu le programme faisable : mucite plus courte (9 contre 11,3 jours), moins de sorties sous nutrition parentérale (18 % contre 61 %) et coûts réduits.","l2":"PBM du premier jour du conditionnement à J+20 chez 62 enfants (octobre 2022–septembre 2023), comparés aux patients de 2021. Le brossage des dents augmentait (90 % contre 75 %).\n\nCoûts de support liés à la mucite (nutrition parentérale, PCA) : médiane 31 230 $ contre 37 371 $. L'étude insiste sur le rôle d'une infirmière dédiée.","l3":{"points":["Sortie sous nutrition parentérale : 18 % contre 61 % (p < 0,001).","Coûts médians : 31 229,87 $ contre 37 370,66 $ (p = 0,02)."],"limites":["Comparaison avant-après."],"pratique":"Modèle d'organisation : infirmière dédiée et protocole écrit."},"topic":"mucite-orale","title":"Implementation of a light therapy team to administer photobiomodulation therapy: A standardized protocol to prevent and treat oral mucositis in the pediatric hematopoietic stem cell transplant population.","journal":"Pediatric blood & cancer","journal_abbr":"Pediatr Blood Cancer","authors":["Magee K","Robins J","Staton S","et al."],"date":"2024-03-31","year":2024,"week":"2024-S13","doi":"10.1002/pbc.30966","pmcid":null,"license":null,"oa":false,"pdf":null,"cited_by":5,"pub_types":["Research Support, Non-U.S. Gov't"],"abstract":[{"label":"BACKGROUND","text":"Oral mucositis (OM) is a painful and common complication of hematopoietic stem cell transplant (HSCT). The Children's Oncology Group recently published guidelines recommending photobiomodulation (PBM) for preventing and treating OM in pediatric HSCT patients. However, this is a rarely used intervention in pediatric hospitals."},{"label":"PROCEDURE","text":"Patients undergoing allogeneic HSCT, or autologous HSCT for a neuroblastoma diagnosis, had PBM administered from the first day of conditioning to transplant Day +20. We successfully developed a standardized treatment protocol and workflow to ensure consistent and uniform delivery of PBM. In addition, clinical patient data were compared before and after PBM implementation."},{"label":"RESULTS","text":"The administration of PBM at our center was feasible, but required dedicated staff. A registered nurse (RN) was determined to be the best fit to deliver PBM. Sixty-two patients received PBM from October 2022 to September 2023; patients from 2021 before PBM implementation were used for comparison. Patients receiving PBM were more likely (p = .03) to engage in teeth brushing (56/62 = 90%) compared to baseline (61/81 = 75%). Mean days of OM decreased from 11.3 to 9 days; patients who received PBM were less likely (p < .001) to be discharged on total parental nutrition (TPN) (11/62 = 18%) compared to baseline (50/82 = 61%). OM-related supportive care costs (TPN and patient-controlled anesthesia [PCA]) were lower (p = .02) for those who received PBM (median cost = $31,229.87 vs. $37,370.66)."},{"label":"CONCLUSION","text":"PBM, as the standard of care in the pediatric HSCT population, is safe, feasible, and well-tolerated. At our center, a dedicated RN was critical to providing standardized treatment and ensuring sustainability."}],"keywords":["oral mucositis","pediatric HSCT","photobiomodulation"]},{"pmid":"38438625","title_fr":"PBM préventive versus curative dans le myélome multiple en autogreffe","type":"cohort","level":4,"pop":"adulte","ctx":["CSH"],"aim":"les deux","mode":[],"nm":[],"n":24,"effect":"favorable","theme":"Moment d'application","l1":"Chez 24 patients atteints de myélome en autogreffe, la PBM débutée dès le conditionnement réduit beaucoup plus la sévérité et la douleur de la mucite que la PBM débutée à l'apparition des lésions.","l2":"Série rétrospective italienne avec le même conditionnement et le même protocole de greffe. Préventif : du premier jour du conditionnement à J+2. Curatif : quatre jours dès l'apparition de la mucite.\n\nTous les patients ont eu une mucite, mais la sévérité et la douleur étaient bien moindres en préventif (p < 0,0001), avec moins de gêne pour avaler, mâcher et parler. Aucun effet indésirable.","l3":{"points":["Sévérité et douleur : p < 0,0001 en faveur du préventif."],"limites":["Rétrospectif, petit effectif."],"pratique":"En autogreffe pour myélome, débuter la PBM dès le premier jour du conditionnement."},"topic":"mucite-orale","title":"Preventive versus curative photobiomodulation for oral mucositis in patients with multiple myeloma undergoing hematopoietic stem cell transplantation: which approach is more effective?","journal":"Supportive care in cancer : official journal of the Multinational Association of Supportive Care in Cancer","journal_abbr":"Support Care Cancer","authors":["Rupel K","Cornacchia A","Poiani M","et al."],"date":"2024-03-04","year":2024,"week":"2024-S10","doi":"10.1007/s00520-024-08414-5","pmcid":null,"license":null,"oa":false,"pdf":null,"cited_by":0,"pub_types":[],"abstract":[{"label":"PURPOSE","text":"There is increasing evidence that photobiomodulation (PBM) therapy is both an effective and safe approach in hematopoietic stem cell transplantation (HSCT) for both prevention and management of oral mucositis (OM), but its use in clinical practice is still limited and the timing of application is under discussion. The aim of this retrospective study was to evaluate possible differences between patients treated either with preventive or curative PBM therapy."},{"label":"METHODS","text":"The retrospective case series included 24 patients suffering from multiple myeloma who underwent the same conditioning and transplantation protocol. Patients were treated either with preventive PBM starting from the first day of conditioning up to two days post-HSCT or with curative PBM (starting at OM onset for four consecutive days). OM score, pain, and functional parameters were recorded."},{"label":"RESULTS","text":"All patients developed OM. Preventive PBM was significantly more effective in reducing OM severity (p < 0.0001) and pain (p < 0.0001) post-HSCT than curative PBM. Furthermore, we found a lower number of patients reporting discomfort in all subjective parameters (pain during swallowing, chewing, and speaking) in the preventive PBM group. No adverse events related to PBM therapy were recorded in both groups."},{"label":"CONCLUSION","text":"The timing for PBM therapy in patients undergoing HSCT is crucial: when started on the first day of conditioning, it significantly reduces both pain and OM severity, providing an important benefit also in subjective oral functions such as speaking, swallowing, and chewing, thus increasing the overall adherence to the oncological therapies."}],"keywords":["Hematopoietic stem cell transplantation","Laser therapy","Multiple myeloma","Oral mucositis","Photobiomodulation","Supportive care in hematological cancer"]},{"pmid":"37858427","title_fr":"La photobiomodulation dans la prévention et la prise en charge des effets secondaires des traitements anticancéreux : bases, résultats et perspectives","type":"sr","level":3,"pop":"mixte","ctx":["mixte"],"aim":"les deux","mode":[],"nm":[],"n":null,"effect":"favorable","theme":"Sécurité oncologique","key":true,"l1":"Synthèse française (Bulletin du Cancer) : la PBM est efficace en essais randomisés contre la mucite aiguë, la radiodermite et le lymphœdème ; elle doit rester dans les doses recommandées, avec consentement éclairé près d'une tumeur.","l2":"Revue systématique des études prospectives (Embase, Medline, Cochrane, EBSCO, Scopus, LILACS) sur de nombreuses toxicités. Le niveau de preuve est hétérogène, globalement modéré ; la principale faiblesse est l'imprécision des protocoles.\n\nPour les autres effets (dysgueusie, ostéonécrose, neuropathie, alopécie, syndrome main-pied, GVH), les études sont trop limitées. Les auteurs ont pu définir des paramètres et doses de pratique sûre, et recommandent la prudence et l'information du patient en zone tumorale.","l3":{"points":["Efficacité en ECR : mucite aiguë, épithélite, lymphœdème du membre supérieur.","Recommandation : rester dans les paramètres validés ; informer et recueillir le consentement en zone tumorale."],"limites":["Études rétrospectives exclues ; analyse en partie qualitative."],"pratique":"Référence francophone pour rédiger le consentement et le protocole de sécurité."},"topic":"mucite-orale","title":"[Photobiomodulation in the prevention and the management of side effects of cancer treatments: Bases, results and perspectives].","journal":"Bulletin du cancer","journal_abbr":"Bull Cancer","authors":["Lairedj K","Klausner G","Robijns J","et al."],"date":"2024-03-01","year":2024,"week":"2024-S09","doi":"10.1016/j.bulcan.2023.08.011","pmcid":null,"license":null,"oa":false,"pdf":null,"cited_by":3,"pub_types":["English Abstract","Review"],"abstract":[{"label":"BACKGROUND","text":"Assess the current and potential indications of photobiomodulation (PBM) therapy and their level of evidence in the prevention or treatment of side effects related to oncology treatments (radiation therapy, and to a minimal extent favored and hematopoietic stem cell transplants). And report on the recommended modalities (parameters and doses) of PBM therapy."},{"label":"MATERIALS AND METHODS","text":"The Embase, Medline/PubMed, Cochrane, EBSCO, Scopus, and LILACS databases were systematically reviewed to include and analyze publications of clinical studies that evaluated PBM in the prevention or management side effects related to cancer treatments. The keywords used were \"photobiomodulation\"; \"low level laser therapy\"; \"acute oral mucositis\"; \"acute dysphagia\"; \"acute radiation dermatitis\"; \"lymphedema\"; \"xerostomia\"; \"dysgeusia\"; \"hyposalivation\"; \"lockjaw\"; \"bone necrosis\"; \"osteoradionecrosis\"; \"radiation induced fibrosis\"; \"voice and speech alterations\"; \"palmar-plantar erythrodysesthesia\"; \"graft versus host disease\"; \"peripheral neuropathy\"; \"chemotherapy induced alopecia\". Prospective studies were included, while retrospective cohorts and non-original articles were excluded from the analysis."},{"label":"RESULTS","text":"PBM in the red or infrared spectrum has been shown to be effective in randomized controlled trials in the prevention and management of certain complications related to radiotherapy, in particular acute mucositis, epitheliitis and upper limb lymphedema. The level of evidence associated with PBM was heterogeneous, but overall remained moderate. The main limitations were the diversity and the lack of precision of the treatment protocols which could compromise the efficiency and the reproducibility of the results of the PBM. For other effects related to chemo/radiation therapy (dysgeusia, osteonecrosis, peripheral neuropathy, alopecia, palmar-plantar erythrodysaesthesia) and haematopoietic stem cell transplantation (graft versus host disease), treatment with PBM suffers from a lack of studies or limited studies at the origin of a weakened level of proof. However, based on these results, it was possible to establish safe practice parameters and doses of PBM."},{"label":"CONCLUSION","text":"Published data suggest that PBM could therefore be considered as supportive care in its own right for patients treated with radiation, chemotherapy, immunotherapy, hormone therapy or targeted therapies, whether in clinical practice or clinical trials. therapies. However, until solid data have been published on its long-term safety, the use of PBM should be considered with caution and within the recommended parameters and doses, particularly when practiced in areas of known or possible tumours. In this case, the patient should be informed of the theoretical benefits and risks of PBM in order to obtain informed consent before treatment."}],"keywords":["Chemotherapy","Effets secondaires des anticancéreux","Low-level light therapy","Mucite","Neoplasms","Neuropathies périphériques post-chimiothérapie","Photobiomodulation","Photobiomodulation (PBM)","Radiation therapy","Radiodermite"]},{"pmid":"38377968","title_fr":"Utilisation de la PBM pour prévenir et traiter la mucite en greffe pédiatrique","type":"cohort","level":4,"pop":"pédiatrie","ctx":["CSH"],"aim":"les deux","mode":[],"nm":[],"n":40,"effect":"favorable","theme":"Vie réelle","l1":"Chez 40 enfants allogreffés, une PBM quotidienne sur six sites est faisable (93 %) ; 20 % ont eu une mucite de grade 3 contre 51 % attendus, avec une hospitalisation plus courte.","l2":"Étude américaine de faisabilité : traitement quotidien jusqu'à J+20 ou la prise de greffe. 979 des 1 035 rencontres ont permis de traiter au moins quatre sites.\n\nPar rapport à des témoins appariés : moins de jours d'hospitalisation (p = 0,009) et une mucite moins sévère (p = 0,03). Le Children's Oncology Group recommande désormais la PBM.","l3":{"points":["Faisabilité 93,3 % ; mucite de grade 3 : 20 % (IC95 % 9,1–35,6)."],"limites":["Témoins appariés non randomisés."],"pratique":"Faisable en routine en greffe pédiatrique."},"topic":"mucite-orale","title":"Utilization of Photobiomodulation for the Prevention and Treatment of Oral Mucositis.","journal":"Journal of pediatric hematology/oncology nursing","journal_abbr":"J Pediatr Hematol Oncol Nurs","authors":["Pritchard M","Ogg SW","Bosi J","et al."],"date":"2024-02-20","year":2024,"week":"2024-S08","doi":"10.1177/27527530231214525","pmcid":"PMC13686199","license":null,"oa":false,"pdf":null,"cited_by":2,"pub_types":["Research Support, Non-U.S. Gov't","Research Support, N.I.H., Extramural"],"abstract":[{"label":"","text":"Background: Oral mucositis is a significant and common toxicity experienced by patients who receive high-dose chemotherapy as a preparatory regimen for a hematopoietic cell transplant (HCT). Photobiomodulation (PBM) has been found to be feasible with significant efficacy in preventing the progression of oral mucositis in adult patients undergoing HCT. The purpose of this study was to determine the feasibility and efficacy of PBM in pediatric oncology patients undergoing HCT. Method: Forty children and adolescents admitted to the transplant unit for an allogeneic HCT for acute lymphoblastic leukemia or acute myeloid leukemia were treated daily at six sites until day + 20 or engraftment. Results: There were 1,035 patient encounters, with successful treatment of four or more sites during 979 patient encounters for a feasibility 93.3% CI [0.926, 0.039]. We had estimated a meaningful effect size of 20% for PBM and estimated 51% of patients treated with PBM would have at least one day or more of Grade 3 mucositis. The rate of patients who received PBM and developed Grade 3 mucositis was 20% CI [0.091, 0.356]. Patients treated with PBM had fewer days of hospitalization (p = .009) and less severe mucositis in comparison to the matched control group (p = .03). Conclusion: PBM is feasible and effective in preventing and treating oral mucositis and is now supported by the Children's Oncology Group for prevention and treatment of oral mucositis in patients undergoing an allogeneic HCT or receiving head/neck radiation."}],"keywords":["light therapy","mucositis","pediatric oncology","photobiomodulation"]},{"pmid":"38265122","title_fr":"Efficacité de la PBM dans la mucite des patients ORL : revue systématique et méta-analyse d'essais randomisés","type":"ma","level":1,"pop":"adulte","ctx":["ORL"],"aim":"les deux","mode":["laser"],"nm":[632.8,660],"n":869,"effect":"favorable","theme":"Synthèse des preuves","key":true,"metric":{"kind":"rr","outcome":"Mucite sévère en fin de traitement","rr":0.45,"lo":0.24,"hi":0.85,"k":14},"l1":"Quatorze essais (869 patients ORL) : la PBM réduit la mucite sévère (RR 0,45 en fin de traitement) et la douleur ; les meilleurs résultats sont obtenus à faible puissance (10–25 mW).","l2":"Méta-analyse dans Head & Neck (PubMed, Embase, Cochrane, LILACS, Web of Science ; 2000–octobre 2023). L'incidence de mucite baisse dès la 2e semaine (RR 0,49) et jusqu'à la 7e (RR 0,77) ; la mucite sévère baisse dès la 3e semaine (RR 0,51) et jusqu'à la fin (RR 0,45 ; IC95 % 0,24–0,85).\n\nLa douleur diminue d'environ 1 point (DMP −1,09). Les lasers He-Ne ou InGaAlP de 10 à 25 mW donnent les meilleurs résultats. L'hétérogénéité est forte sur certaines estimations (I² jusqu'à 89 %).","l3":{"points":["14 ECR, 869 patients.","Mucite sévère fin de traitement : RR 0,45 (0,24–0,85), I² = 80 %.","Douleur : DMP −1,09 (−1,38 à −0,88), I² = 13 %.","Paramètres favorables : He-Ne (632,8 nm) ou InGaAlP (660 nm), 10–25 mW."],"limites":["Forte hétérogénéité pour plusieurs critères."],"pratique":"Référence de début 2024 en ORL ; cohérente avec les méta-analyses de 2026."},"topic":"mucite-orale","title":"Efficacy of photobiomodulation therapy in the management of oral mucositis in patients with head and neck cancer: A systematic review and meta-analysis of randomized controlled trials.","journal":"Head & neck","journal_abbr":"Head Neck","authors":["Shen B","Zhou Y","Wu D","et al."],"date":"2024-01-24","year":2024,"week":"2024-S04","doi":"10.1002/hed.27655","pmcid":null,"license":null,"oa":false,"pdf":null,"cited_by":13,"pub_types":["Meta-Analysis","Systematic Review"],"abstract":[{"label":"","text":"Numerous studies have examined the effectiveness of photobiomodulation therapy (PBMT) in reducing chemoradiotherapy (CRT)-induced oral mucositis (OM) in patients with head and neck cancer (HNC). Despite this, there is an urgent need to update the meta-analyses on this topic. This meta-analysis aims to explore the impact of PBMT on CRT-induced OM in these patients. We conducted a systematic search in PubMed, Embase, Cochrane, LILACS, and Web of Science from January 2000 to October 2023. This search focused on randomized controlled trials (RCTs) that assessed the effects of PBMT on CRT-induced OM. The study included a total of 14 RCTs encompassing 869 patients with HNC. The incidence of OM in the PBMT group was significantly lower from the second week onwards compared to the control group (RR = 0.49, CI = 0.25-0.97, I2  = 71%, p = 0.04), and this was present until the seventh week (RR = 0.77, CI = 0.61-0.99, I2  = 89%, p = 0.04). Furthermore, the occurrence of severe mucositis in the PBMT group decreased from the third week (RR = 0.51, CI = 0.29-0.90, I2  = 12%, p = 0.02) until the conclusion of the intervention (RR = 0.45, CI = 0.24-0.85, I2  = 80%, p = 0.01). Additionally, PBMT showed beneficial effects in alleviating OM-related pain (WMD = -1.09, 95% CI = -1.38 to -0.880, I2  = 13%, p < 0.00001). The use of He-Ne or InGaAlP lasers with a power range of 10-25 mW demonstrated the most favorable outcomes in preventing and treating OM. PBMT has shown considerable efficacy in reducing the incidence, severity, and pain associated with OM in patients with HNC. Future studies are encouraged to further investigate the most effective parameters for PBMT in the management of OM."}],"keywords":["concurrent radiotherapy","head and neck cancer","meta-analysis","oral mucositis","photobiomodulation"]},{"pmid":"38183418","title_fr":"Dispositif de PBM auto-administré : la prochaine génération de prévention et de traitement de la mucite","type":"letter","level":0,"pop":"—","ctx":["mixte"],"aim":"prévention","mode":["domicile"],"nm":[],"n":null,"effect":"—","theme":"PBM à domicile","l1":"Lettre défendant les dispositifs de PBM auto-administrés. Pas de résumé ; le sujet a été repris en 2025 par une étude pilote à domicile.","l2":"Courrier dans Supportive Care in Cancer. Voir la fiche « Du cabinet au canapé » (novembre 2025) pour les premières données de faisabilité.","l3":{"points":["Correspondance."],"limites":["Contenu non indexé."],"pratique":"—"},"topic":"mucite-orale","title":"Self-applied photobiomodulation device: the next generation in oral mucositis prevention and treatment.","journal":"Supportive care in cancer : official journal of the Multinational Association of Supportive Care in Cancer","journal_abbr":"Support Care Cancer","authors":["Zadik Y","Gavish L"],"date":"2024-01-06","year":2024,"week":"2024-S01","doi":"10.1007/s00520-023-08292-3","pmcid":null,"license":null,"oa":false,"pdf":null,"cited_by":2,"pub_types":["Letter"],"abstract":[],"keywords":[]}],"syntheses":{"mucite-orale":{"years":{"2024":{"headline":"L'année du bilan : un effet constant, des preuves fragiles","l1":"En 2024, les méta-analyses confirment que la PBM réduit d'environ moitié la mucite sévère en ORL, mais une revue parapluie montre que les revues sont de faible qualité, et une méta-analyse pédiatrique est négative.","l2":"La méta-analyse de Head & Neck (14 essais, 869 patients ORL) chiffre l'effet : mucite sévère réduite de 55 % en fin de traitement (RR 0,45), douleur diminuée d'environ 1 point, meilleurs résultats à faible puissance (10–25 mW). La revue parapluie de BMC Oral Health tempère ce résultat : 71 % des revues sont de qualité critiquement faible et une seule estimation est « fortement suggestive ». En pédiatrie, la méta-analyse d'In Vivo ne trouve pas d'effet préventif (RR 0,99). Cochrane publie le protocole d'une revue dédiée.\n\nSur le terrain, les études montrent l'importance du moment et de la fréquence : la PBM préventive dès le conditionnement fait mieux que la curative dans le myélome, la PBM quotidienne fait mieux qu'un jour sur trois. Les programmes pédiatriques de greffe (infirmière dédiée, Children's Oncology Group) prouvent la faisabilité et réduisent les coûts. Côté sécurité, deux signaux précliniques (mélanome à 808 nm, cellules de cancer buccal) et la synthèse du Bulletin du Cancer rappellent de respecter les doses et d'informer le patient.","l3":[{"h":"Efficacité","p":"Méta-analyse ORL de référence : RR 0,45 (0,24–0,85) pour la mucite sévère en fin de traitement ; effet dès la 2e semaine sur l'incidence. La méta-analyse des interventions non médicamenteuses (27 essais) va dans le même sens (RR 0,54 toutes interventions confondues). En revanche, chez l'enfant, la méta-analyse In Vivo (5 essais poolés) ne montre pas d'effet ; l'essai égyptien en LAM (42 enfants, double aveugle) est, lui, nettement positif."},{"h":"Qualité des preuves","p":"La revue parapluie (21 revues, 40 méta-analyses) est le fait marquant méthodologique : l'effet est constant mais repose sur des synthèses de qualité critiquement faible. Le protocole Cochrane annonce la revue qui devrait servir de référence."},{"h":"Protocole et organisation","p":"Préventif plutôt que curatif (myélome, p < 0,0001), quotidien plutôt qu'espacé (cancer buccal), puissance faible (10–25 mW). La greffe pédiatrique montre qu'une infirmière dédiée rend le programme durable, avec moins de nutrition parentérale (18 % contre 61 %) et des coûts réduits. La revue médico-économique (GRADE modéré) conclut à un bon rapport coût-efficacité à moyen terme."},{"h":"Douleur","p":"La PBM haute puissance soulage immédiatement la mucite réfractaire (94 % des séances), et le concept de PBM « préemptive » se développe. Un débat par lettres accompagne ces travaux (Oral Diseases, Int J Paediatr Dent)."},{"h":"Sécurité oncologique","p":"Signal préclinique à 808 nm sur le mélanome murin ; effets dépendants de la puissance sur les cellules de carcinome buccal ; revue de Frontiers in Oncology aux résultats discordants. Cliniquement, la cohorte brésilienne de 751 patients ne montre pas de surmortalité (le gain de survie apparent est probablement un biais). La synthèse francophone du Bulletin du Cancer fixe le cadre : doses validées, information et consentement en zone tumorale."}]},"2025":{"headline":"L'année du « comment » : voie extrabuccale, LED, domicile","l1":"En 2025, la question n'est plus de savoir si la PBM marche mais comment la délivrer : la voie extrabuccale égale l'intrabuccale en ORL, l'ajouter sur les glandes salivaires apporte un bénéfice, et les dispositifs automatiques ou à domicile apparaissent.","l2":"L'essai multicentrique brésilien (58 patients ORL) montre une efficacité équivalente de la PBM extrabuccale (810/980 nm) et intrabuccale (660 nm), avec une déglutition et un goût mieux préservés. Un autre essai (75 patients) montre que l'association des deux voies améliore mucite, débit salivaire et qualité de vie. Les dispositifs se diversifient : gouttière laser à trois longueurs d'onde, appareil à domicile auto-administré, LED (revue GRADE faible mais favorable).\n\nLes synthèses restent favorables : recommandations officielles polonaises avec une fenêtre de paramètres (630–670 nm en intrabuccal, 780–850 nm en extrabuccal, 2–6 J/cm²), méta-analyse pédiatrique positive qui contredit celle de 2024, revue de 13 essais en chimiothérapie. Le seul résultat défavorable vient d'un gel d'Arrabidaea chica qui cicatrise plus vite que le laser en greffe. Les études de vie réelle identifient les patients qui font une mucite sévère malgré la PBM : HPV positif, dénutrition, chimioradiothérapie, conditionnement au melphalan ou au méthotrexate.","l3":[{"h":"Voies d'administration","p":"Essai de référence intrabuccal contre extrabuccal en ORL (Supportive Care in Cancer, 58 patients) : pas de différence de grade (p = 0,69) et moins de dysphagie en extrabuccal. Essai d'association (Lasers Med Sci, 75 patients) : la combinaison surpasse l'intrabuccal seul dès la 2e semaine. La revue exploratoire de 2026 sur l'extrabuccal arrive juste avant ces données."},{"h":"Nouveaux dispositifs","p":"Gouttière intrabuccale à 43 points (6,6 % de mucites sévères chez 15 patients), appareil à domicile (60 % d'observance, mais 41 % de mucites sévères en fin de radiothérapie), LED (5 études, GRADE faible). Ce sont des preuves de faisabilité, pas encore d'efficacité comparative."},{"h":"Chimiothérapie et pédiatrie","p":"Préconditionnement avant la première cure : 80 à 87 % des patients sans mucite contre 7 % (deux publications issues probablement de la même cohorte syrienne). Méta-analyse pédiatrique de J Dent (5 essais, 177 enfants) positive. Essai iranien chez l'enfant sous MTX : incidence de 66,7 % à 6,7 % (avant-après) et thérapie photodynamique au vert d'indocyanine en curatif."},{"h":"Associations et comparateurs","p":"L-glutamine et acide hyaluronique ajoutés à la PBM : bénéfice sur l'échelle OMS seulement. Gel d'A. chica supérieur au laser pour la cicatrisation (5,2 contre 11,2 jours, essai ouvert). Dispositif Germinator équivalent au laser (sans bras témoin)."},{"h":"Implantation","p":"Acceptabilité élevée chez l'enfant et les parents au Royaume-Uni, freinée par la charge de travail des équipes. Au Brésil, les tribunaux reconnaissent la PBM comme un soin opposable aux assureurs (50 décisions)."},{"h":"Sécurité","p":"Signal sur l'épithélium de xénope à 660 nm (anomalies chez 30,6 % des têtards contre 3,8 %), sans équivalent clinique connu. Les revues mécanistiques confirment la modulation des cytokines (IL-6, TNF-α) et du stress oxydatif (nitrite salivaire, SOD)."}]},"2026":{"headline":"L'année de la consolidation : de grands essais et de nouveaux critères","l1":"En 2026, la plus grande méta-analyse (30 essais, 1 748 patients ORL) confirme une baisse de 54 % des mucites sévères, un essai multicentrique contre simulation valide un dispositif LED, et l'attention se porte sur la douleur, les opioïdes et la voie extrabuccale chez l'enfant.","l2":"Deux méta-analyses convergent : RR 0,46 pour la mucite sévère en ORL (avec un RR de 0,35 pour la douleur sévère) et RR 0,50 sur les essais récents, avec une certitude toujours faible. Les essais randomisés contre placebo ou simulation se multiplient : dispositif LED intrabuccal (85 patients, 12 centres américains, 36,8 % contre 57,1 % de mucites sévères), méthotrexate haute dose chez l'adulte (11,5 % contre 51,9 %, il suffit de traiter 2 patients), greffe (POMFITT, 0 % contre 26,6 %). Un essai en bouche divisée montre aussi un effet curatif (guérison 52,4 % contre 11,9 %).\n\nEn pédiatrie, deux essais brésiliens et une enquête de préférence placent la PBM extrabuccale au moins à égalité avec l'intrabuccale, avec un temps de séance divisé par dix et beaucoup moins d'inconfort. En France, une cohorte appariée de 280 patients montre que la PBM préventive réduit la morphine sans changer le grade de mucite : un argument pour mesurer la douleur et pas seulement le grade. Les revues parapluie et une étude sur le « spin » des résumés rappellent les limites méthodologiques du domaine.","l3":[{"h":"Synthèses quantitatives","p":"Méta-analyse à trois niveaux (Supportive Care in Cancer, mars) : mucite sévère RR 0,46 (0,29–0,71), douleur sévère RR 0,35 (0,23–0,53), débit salivaire DMS 0,75, xérostomie et qualité de vie non significatives ; certitude très faible à faible. Méta-analyse des essais 2020–2026 : RR 0,50 (0,35–0,73). Revue de 15 essais contre simulation chez l'adulte : bénéfice surtout en prévention intrabuccale. Revues parapluie (LLLT toutes indications ; complications ORL) : signal favorable sur la mucite, mais 90 % des revues de confiance faible ou critiquement faible."},{"h":"Essais majeurs","p":"LED intrabuccale contre simulation en IMRT (NCT03972527) : critère principal atteint (p = 0,046) avec un bénéfice sur le goût et la douleur. MTX-HD chez l'adulte : réduction relative du risque de 77,7 % et méthotrexate salivaire comme biomarqueur. POMFITT en greffe : mucite sévère évitée, sans bénéfice fonctionnel. Essai en bouche divisée (Radiotherapy & Oncology) : effet curatif sur la cicatrisation et la douleur dès la première semaine."},{"h":"Extrabuccal chez l'enfant","p":"Essai de 155 enfants : 30,5 % contre 41,1 % (NS), 30 s contre 6 min. Essai de 31 enfants randomisés par cure : avantage à l'extrabuccal (RR 1,84 en défaveur de l'intrabuccal). Préférence des parents : 65,6 % pour l'extrabuccal, 6,3 % d'inconfort contre 43,8 %. Un protocole d'essai de non-infériorité en ORL adulte (808 nm transcutané) est en cours."},{"h":"Douleur, opioïdes, odynophagie","p":"Cohorte française appariée : morphine 22,9 contre 37,4 mg/j en fin de radiothérapie et 10,3 contre 27,2 mg/j à un mois, avec des grades de mucite identiques (63 %). PBM haute puissance 940 nm sur l'odynophagie : baisse de 40 % de la douleur sur près de 4 jours. Vie réelle italienne : 10 séances ou plus réduisent odynophagie et dysphagie persistante, sans effet sur la survie."},{"h":"Méthodologie et implantation","p":"Spin dans 14 des 16 résumés d'essais analysés. Microbiome : hypothèse de recherche et non mécanisme établi. Royaume-Uni : l'adoption dépend de l'organisation locale, les LED réduisent les obstacles. Australie et Nouvelle-Zélande : PBM rarement utilisée, absente des recommandations nationales EviQ."}]}},"global":{"headline":"2024 → 2026 : ce qui est acquis, ce qui bouge, ce qui manque","l1":"Sur trois ans, l'effet préventif de la PBM sur la mucite sévère reste remarquablement stable (RR de 0,45 à 0,54 dans les méta-analyses). Les recherches sont passées de la question de l'efficacité à celle de la délivrance (extrabuccal, LED, domicile) et de critères centrés sur le patient (douleur, opioïdes). La qualité méthodologique reste le point faible.","l2":"Acquis : la PBM préventive réduit environ de moitié la mucite sévère et d'environ deux tiers la douleur sévère, en ORL comme en chimiothérapie haute dose et en greffe. L'estimation ne bouge pas de 2024 (RR 0,45, 14 essais) à 2026 (RR 0,46, 30 essais), alors que le nombre de patients a doublé. Elle est désormais confirmée par des essais multicentriques contre simulation. Le moment compte (préventif plutôt que curatif) et la fréquence aussi (quotidien plutôt qu'espacé).\n\nCe qui bouge : la voie extrabuccale passe d'une option peu étayée (revue exploratoire) à une alternative validée en essai randomisé, chez l'adulte ORL en 2025 puis chez l'enfant en 2026. Les critères de jugement s'élargissent à la douleur, aux opioïdes, au goût, à la déglutition et aux coûts. La pédiatrie passe d'une méta-analyse négative (2024) à une méta-analyse positive (2025) et à des essais spécifiques (2026). Ce qui manque : des revues de qualité (la revue Cochrane est attendue), une dosimétrie standardisée, des données de sécurité oncologique à long terme au-delà des cohortes rassurantes, et des comparaisons directes avec les autres options (cryothérapie, topiques).","l3":[{"h":"1. Un effet stable malgré l'accumulation des données","p":"Les quatre estimations poolées sur la mucite (2024 : RR 0,45 et RR 0,54 ; 2026 : RR 0,46 et RR 0,50) se recouvrent largement. Quand un effet ne s'érode pas alors que les essais s'accumulent, c'est un argument contre un simple biais de publication. La seule méta-analyse discordante, pédiatrique (2024, RR 0,99), reposait sur très peu d'essais et a été contredite en 2025."},{"h":"2. Une qualité méthodologique qui ne progresse pas assez","p":"Les revues parapluie de 2024 (71 % de revues critiquement faibles) et de 2026 (60 %) font le même constat, et l'étude de 2026 trouve du spin dans 14 résumés d'essais sur 16. La certitude GRADE de la plus grande méta-analyse reste très faible à faible. Les essais de 2026 (LED multicentrique en double aveugle, MTX-HD en double aveugle) montrent cependant une montée en qualité des études primaires."},{"h":"3. De la question « est-ce efficace ? » à la question « comment le délivrer ? »","p":"2024 : fréquence (quotidien plutôt qu'espacé) et moment (préventif plutôt que curatif). 2025 : extrabuccal équivalent en ORL, association des deux voies, gouttières, domicile. 2026 : extrabuccal chez l'enfant, LED autonome validée, 10 séances au moins en vie réelle. La logistique, c'est-à-dire le temps de séance, le personnel et l'inconfort, est devenue un objet de recherche à part entière."},{"h":"4. Des critères centrés sur le patient","p":"La cohorte française de 2026 illustre un tournant : moins de morphine sans changement du grade de mucite. La douleur, l'odynophagie, le goût, la déglutition, la nutrition et les coûts deviennent des critères principaux. Pour la pratique, il faut suivre la consommation d'antalgiques et la qualité de vie, pas seulement le grade OMS."},{"h":"5. La sécurité oncologique, une vigilance qui reste nécessaire","p":"Les signaux défavorables viennent tous de modèles précliniques (mélanome murin à 808 nm en 2024, xénope en 2025, lignées tumorales). Les données cliniques sont rassurantes (aucune surmortalité en 2024, aucun effet sur la survie sans progression ni la survie globale en 2026) mais rétrospectives. La position francophone (Bulletin du Cancer 2024) reste d'actualité : doses validées, information et consentement près d'une tumeur."},{"h":"6. Implantation : les preuves ne suffisent pas","p":"Royaume-Uni (2025–2026) : acceptable mais peu déployée, faute d'organisation. Australie et Nouvelle-Zélande : rarement utilisée (coût, recommandations nationales). Brésil : opposable par voie judiciaire. États-Unis (pédiatrie) : faisable avec une infirmière dédiée et rentable. Les LED et l'extrabuccal semblent lever une partie des obstacles."}],"table":{"cols":["2024","2025","2026"],"rows":[{"label":"Question dominante","cells":["Est-ce efficace ? Avec quelle qualité de preuve ?","Comment la délivrer (voie, dispositif) ?","Confirmer en multicentrique, élargir les critères"]},{"label":"Méta-analyse phare","cells":["Head & Neck : 14 ECR, RR 0,45","Pédiatrie (J Dent) : 5 ECR, bénéfice significatif","Supportive Care in Cancer : 30 ECR, RR 0,46"]},{"label":"Essai phare","cells":["Quotidien contre un jour sur trois (n = 16)","Intra contre extrabuccal ORL (n = 58)","LED contre simulation, 12 centres (n = 85)"]},{"label":"Pédiatrie","cells":["Méta-analyse négative ; ECR LAM positif","Méta-analyse positive ; acceptabilité","Extrabuccal ≥ intrabuccal (n = 155 et n = 31)"]},{"label":"Douleur","cells":["Haute puissance, effet immédiat","Curatif 810 nm, douleur ↓","Morphine ↓ (cohorte FR, n = 280) ; odynophagie"]},{"label":"Sécurité","cells":["Signal 808 nm (mélanome murin) ; Bulletin du Cancer","Signal Xenopus","Pas d'effet sur la survie (vie réelle)"]},{"label":"Qualité des preuves","cells":["Parapluie : 71 % critiquement faibles","GRADE faible (LED)","Parapluie : 60 % critiquement faibles ; spin"]}]}}}}};
